const STORIES = [{"e":"b","t":"Creation","r":"Genesis 1-2","p":"Adam; Eve","s":"In six days God speaks light, sky, land, seas, plants, stars and animals into being, and calls it all good. On the sixth day He makes people in His own image, man and woman. On the seventh day He rests.","w":"Everything, including you, starts with a good God who made it on purpose.","q":"Where do I see God's goodness in what He made today?","l":["In the beginning there was nothing but God. The earth was formless and empty, darkness covered the deep waters, and the Spirit of God was hovering over them. Then God spoke: “Let there be light,” and there was light. He separated the light from the darkness and called them day and night. That was the first day.","Day by day God kept speaking, and creation obeyed. On the second day He separated the waters above from the waters below with the sky. On the third He gathered the seas so dry land appeared, and filled the land with plants and fruit trees. On the fourth He hung the sun, moon and stars in the sky. On the fifth He filled the seas with fish and the sky with birds. After each day, God saw that it was good.","On the sixth day God made the animals, and then He did something different. “Let us make mankind in our image,” He said. He formed the man from the dust of the ground and breathed into him the breath of life. He planted a garden in Eden, put the man there to work it and take care of it, and gave him one command: do not eat from the tree of the knowledge of good and evil.","God said it was not good for the man to be alone. He brought the animals to Adam to name, but none was a suitable helper. So God put Adam into a deep sleep, took one of his ribs and made a woman. Adam woke and said, “This is now bone of my bones and flesh of my flesh.” God looked at everything He had made, and it was very good. On the seventh day He rested, and blessed that day and made it holy."],"id":"story-creation"},{"e":"b","t":"The fall","r":"Genesis 3","p":"Adam; Eve; The serpent","s":"The serpent questions what God said, and Eve and Adam eat the fruit God told them not to eat. Their eyes are opened, they feel shame and hide. God judges the serpent, but promises that a son of the woman will crush its head.","w":"Sin starts with doubting God's word, and the first promise of a Saviour comes straight after it.","q":"Where am I hiding from God instead of coming to Him?","l":["Of all the animals, the serpent was the most crafty. It came to the woman with a question: “Did God really say, ‘You must not eat from any tree in the garden’?” Eve corrected it, but added that they weren’t even allowed to touch the fruit of the forbidden tree, or they would die.","“You will not certainly die,” the serpent said. “God knows that when you eat from it your eyes will be opened, and you will be like God, knowing good and evil.” Eve looked at the fruit. It was good for food, pleasing to the eye and desirable for gaining wisdom. She took some and ate it, and gave some to Adam, who was with her, and he ate it too.","Immediately their eyes were opened, and they realised they were naked. They sewed fig leaves together to cover themselves. In the cool of the day they heard God walking in the garden, and they hid among the trees. God called, “Where are you?” Adam said he was afraid because he was naked. When God asked what happened, Adam blamed the woman, and the woman blamed the serpent.","God pronounced judgement. The serpent would crawl on its belly. The woman would have pain in childbirth, and conflict would enter marriage. The ground would be cursed, and the man would work by the sweat of his brow until he returned to dust. But hidden in the curse was a promise: the woman’s offspring would crush the serpent’s head, even though it would strike his heel. God made garments of skin to clothe Adam and Eve, and sent them out of the garden, placing cherubim and a flaming sword to guard the way to the tree of life."],"id":"story-the-fall"},{"e":"b","t":"Cain and Abel","r":"Genesis 4:1-16","p":"Cain; Abel","s":"Both brothers bring offerings, and God accepts Abel's but not Cain's. God warns Cain that sin is crouching at his door, but Cain kills his brother. Even so, God puts a mark on Cain to protect him.","w":"Unchecked anger grows into something much worse, yet God's mercy reaches even Cain.","q":"What is crouching at my door that I need to master?","l":["Adam and Eve had two sons. Cain, the older, worked the soil, and Abel kept flocks. In time, Cain brought some of his crops as an offering to the Lord, and Abel brought fat portions from the firstborn of his flock.","God looked with favour on Abel and his offering, but not on Cain and his. Cain was very angry, and his face was downcast. God came to him and said, “Why are you angry? If you do what is right, will you not be accepted? But if you do not do what is right, sin is crouching at your door; it desires to have you, but you must rule over it.”","Cain did not listen. He said to his brother, “Let’s go out to the field.” While they were there, Cain attacked Abel and killed him. Then the Lord asked, “Where is your brother Abel?” Cain answered with the famous lie and shrug: “I don’t know. Am I my brother’s keeper?”","God said, “Your brother’s blood cries out to me from the ground.” Cain would be a restless wanderer, and the ground would no longer yield crops for him. Cain said his punishment was more than he could bear, and that whoever found him would kill him. Even then God showed mercy: He put a mark on Cain so that no one would kill him. Cain went out from the Lord’s presence and lived in the land of Nod, east of Eden. Later Adam and Eve had another son, Seth, and people began to call on the name of the Lord."],"id":"story-cain-and-abel"},{"e":"b","t":"Enoch walks with God","r":"Genesis 5:21-24; Hebrews 11:5-6","p":"Enoch; Methuselah","s":"In a long list of names where each one ends with 'and then he died', Enoch stands out. He walked faithfully with God for 300 years, and then he was no more, because God took him away.","w":"A whole life can be summed up as walking with God.","q":"What would it look like to walk with God today, one ordinary step at a time?","l":["Genesis 5 reads like a family register from Adam to Noah. Each entry follows the same pattern: someone lived so many years, had a son, lived many more years, had other sons and daughters, ‘and then he died’. It is a steady drumbeat that reminds us of the curse in the garden.","Then comes Enoch, the seventh from Adam. At 65 he became the father of Methuselah, who would live longer than anyone in the Bible, 969 years. After that, the text says something it says of no one else in the list: ‘Enoch walked faithfully with God 300 years.’ And instead of ‘then he died’, his entry ends: ‘Enoch walked faithfully with God; then he was no more, because God took him away.’","The writer of Hebrews explains that by faith Enoch was taken from this life so that he did not experience death, and that before he was taken he was commended as one who pleased God. Then comes one of the great verses of the Bible: without faith it is impossible to please God, because anyone who comes to Him must believe that He exists and that He rewards those who earnestly seek Him. Enoch did not do anything recorded as spectacular. He simply walked with God, day after day, for three centuries."],"id":"story-enoch-walks-with-god"},{"e":"b","t":"Noah and the flood","r":"Genesis 6-9","p":"Noah","s":"The earth is full of violence, but Noah walks with God. God tells him to build an ark for his family and the animals, then floods the earth. Afterwards God sets a rainbow in the sky as His promise never to flood the whole earth again.","w":"God judges evil, saves those who trust Him, and keeps His promises.","q":"What promise of God do I need to hold onto this week?","l":["As people multiplied on the earth, wickedness spread with them. God saw that every inclination of the human heart was evil all the time, and it grieved Him to His heart. But one man, Noah, found favour in the eyes of the Lord. Noah was righteous and blameless among the people of his time, and he walked faithfully with God.","God told Noah to build an ark of cypress wood, about 135 metres long, coated with pitch inside and out, with three decks, a roof and a door in its side. God said He would bring floodwaters on the earth, but He would make a covenant with Noah. Noah was to bring his wife, his sons Shem, Ham and Japheth and their wives, and pairs of every kind of animal, with food for all of them. Noah did everything just as God commanded.","When Noah was 600 years old, the springs of the deep burst open and the floodgates of heaven were opened. It rained for forty days and forty nights, and the waters rose until even the highest mountains were covered. Every living thing on land died. Only Noah and those with him in the ark were left. Then God remembered Noah. He sent a wind, the waters receded, and the ark came to rest on the mountains of Ararat.","Noah sent out a raven, and then a dove. The dove came back with nothing the first time, then with a fresh olive leaf, and the third time it did not return. When the ground was dry, God told Noah to come out. The first thing Noah did was build an altar and worship. God promised never again to destroy all life with a flood, and set His rainbow in the clouds as the sign of His covenant with every living creature."],"id":"story-noah-and-the-flood"},{"e":"b","t":"The Tower of Babel","r":"Genesis 11:1-9","p":"The people of Babel","s":"Everyone speaks one language, and people decide to build a city and a tower reaching to the heavens to make a name for themselves. God confuses their language and scatters them over the earth.","w":"Building life around our own name ends in confusion. Pentecost later reverses Babel.","q":"Where am I trying to make a name for myself?","l":["After the flood, Noah’s descendants spread out, and the whole world still had one language. As people moved east, they found a plain in Shinar, in the land later called Babylon, and settled there.","They had learned to bake bricks and use tar for mortar, and they had an idea. “Come, let us build ourselves a city, with a tower that reaches to the heavens, so that we may make a name for ourselves; otherwise we will be scattered over the face of the whole earth.” God had told humanity to fill the earth, but they wanted to stay together and make themselves great.","God came down to see the city and the tower they were building. Their great tower was so small that God had to come down to look at it. He said that if, as one people with one language, they had begun to do this, nothing they planned would be impossible for them. So He confused their language, so that they could not understand one another.","The building stopped, and God scattered them over the whole earth. The city was called Babel, because there the Lord confused the language of the whole world. Centuries later, at Pentecost, God did the reverse: people from many nations each heard the good news in their own language, and were gathered into one family by the Holy Spirit."],"id":"story-the-tower-of-babel"},{"e":"p","t":"The call of Abram","r":"Genesis 12:1-9","p":"Abram; Sarai; Lot","s":"God tells Abram to leave his country and family for a land He will show him. He promises to make Abram a great nation and to bless all peoples through him. Abram goes, at 75 years old.","w":"The whole story of salvation runs through this promise, and it starts with simple obedience.","q":"What is God asking me to step out on without seeing the whole map?","l":["Abram lived in Haran with his wife Sarai, his nephew Lot and his father’s household. His family had originally come from Ur of the Chaldeans. Sarai could not have children. Into this ordinary, childless family, God spoke.","“Go from your country, your people and your father’s household to the land I will show you,” God said. Then came the promises. God would make him into a great nation, bless him and make his name great, so that he would be a blessing. And through him all the peoples on earth would be blessed.","Abram was seventy-five years old. He had no map, no children and no land, only a word from God. So Abram went, as the Lord had told him. He took Sarai, Lot and all their possessions and people, and set out for Canaan.","When they arrived, Abram travelled through the land to the great tree of Moreh at Shechem. The Canaanites still lived there. But the Lord appeared to Abram and said, “To your offspring I will give this land.” Abram built an altar there to the Lord. He moved on toward the hills east of Bethel, pitched his tent, built another altar and called on the name of the Lord. Wherever Abram went, he marked the land with worship."],"id":"story-the-call-of-abram"},{"e":"p","t":"Lot chooses the plain","r":"Genesis 13-14","p":"Abram; Lot; Melchizedek","s":"When their herds grow too big, Abram lets his nephew Lot choose first. Lot picks the well-watered plain near Sodom. Later Abram rescues Lot from raiding kings, and the priest-king Melchizedek blesses him.","w":"Generosity trusts God to provide; choosing by what looks best can lead you toward trouble.","q":"Am I choosing by what looks good or by what honours God?","l":["Abram and Lot both became very wealthy, with flocks, herds and tents. The land could not support them both, and their herdsmen began to quarrel. Abram, the older man with the stronger claim, did something surprising. He said to Lot, “Let’s not have any quarrelling between you and me, for we are close relatives. Is not the whole land before you? If you go to the left, I’ll go to the right.”","Lot looked around and saw that the whole plain of the Jordan was well watered, like the garden of the Lord. So he chose it for himself, and pitched his tents near Sodom. The Bible quietly adds that the people of Sodom were wicked and were sinning greatly against the Lord. After Lot left, God told Abram to look north, south, east and west: all the land he could see would belong to his offspring, who would be as countless as the dust of the earth.","Later, four kings from the east raided the cities of the plain, including Sodom, and carried off Lot and his possessions. When Abram heard, he called out 318 trained men from his household, chased the raiders far to the north, attacked them at night and brought back Lot, his goods, the women and the other people.","On his return, Melchizedek, king of Salem and priest of God Most High, came out with bread and wine and blessed Abram: “Blessed be Abram by God Most High, Creator of heaven and earth.” Abram gave him a tenth of everything. The king of Sodom offered Abram the goods, but Abram refused even a thread or a sandal strap, so that no one could say anyone but God had made him rich."],"id":"story-lot-chooses-the-plain"},{"e":"p","t":"God's promise under the stars","r":"Genesis 15","p":"Abram","s":"Abram is still childless and tells God his servant will inherit everything. God takes him outside and tells him to count the stars: his offspring will be like that. Abram believes the Lord, and God credits it to him as righteousness. Then God makes a covenant with him in a strange night ceremony.","w":"We are made right with God by trusting His promise, just as Abram was.","q":"Which of God's promises do I need to look up and believe again?","l":["After Abram rescued Lot, the word of the Lord came to him in a vision: “Do not be afraid, Abram. I am your shield, your very great reward.” But Abram was honest with God. “Sovereign Lord, what can you give me since I remain childless?” His servant Eliezer of Damascus would inherit everything.","God answered, “This man will not be your heir, but a son who is your own flesh and blood.” He took Abram outside and said, “Look up at the sky and count the stars, if indeed you can count them. So shall your offspring be.” Abram believed the Lord, and He credited it to him as righteousness. Paul quotes this verse in Romans 4 and Galatians 3 to show that we are made right with God by faith, not by works.","Abram asked how he could know he would possess the land. God told him to bring a heifer, a goat, a ram, a dove and a pigeon. Abram cut the animals in half and arranged the halves opposite each other, and spent the day driving away the birds of prey. In those days, two parties making a covenant would walk between such pieces, meaning ‘may this happen to me if I break my word’.","As the sun set, Abram fell into a deep sleep, and a thick, dreadful darkness came over him. God told him his descendants would be strangers and slaves in a foreign land for four hundred years, and then come out with great possessions. After dark, a smoking firepot with a blazing torch passed between the pieces. Only God walked through. He took the whole weight of the promise on Himself."],"id":"story-god-s-promise-under-the-stars"},{"e":"p","t":"Hagar and the God who sees","r":"Genesis 16; 21:8-21","p":"Hagar; Sarai; Abram; Ishmael","s":"Sarai gives her servant Hagar to Abram to have a child, and then mistreats her. Hagar runs away, and the angel of the Lord finds her in the desert. She names Him 'the God who sees me'. Years later, sent away with Ishmael, God hears the boy and shows her a well.","w":"God sees the overlooked person in the desert.","q":"Where do I need to know that God sees me?","l":["Ten years after arriving in Canaan, Sarai still had no children. She had an Egyptian servant named Hagar, and following the custom of the day, she gave Hagar to Abram so that she might build a family through her. Hagar became pregnant, and began to despise her mistress. Sarai blamed Abram, then mistreated Hagar so harshly that Hagar fled into the desert.","By a spring on the road to Shur, the angel of the Lord found her. “Hagar, slave of Sarai, where have you come from, and where are you going?” He told her to go back, and gave her a promise: her descendants would be too many to count. She would have a son, and she was to call him Ishmael, which means ‘God hears’, because the Lord had heard of her misery.","Hagar gave a name to the Lord who spoke to her: “You are the God who sees me,” for she said, “I have now seen the One who sees me.” The well was called Beer Lahai Roi, the well of the Living One who sees me. She returned and bore Abram a son, Ishmael.","Years later, after Isaac was born, Sarah saw Ishmael mocking and demanded that Hagar and her son be sent away. Abraham was distressed, but God told him to listen, promising to make Ishmael a nation too. Hagar wandered in the desert of Beersheba until the water ran out. She put the boy under a bush and sat a little way off, sobbing, because she could not watch him die. God heard the boy crying. The angel called to Hagar not to be afraid, and God opened her eyes to see a well of water. God was with the boy as he grew up."],"id":"story-hagar-and-the-god-who-sees"},{"e":"p","t":"Sodom and Gomorrah","r":"Genesis 18:16-19:29","p":"Abraham; Lot; Lot's wife","s":"Abraham pleads with God to spare Sodom, and God agrees to spare it for just ten righteous people. Not even ten are found. Angels rush Lot's family out, but Lot's wife looks back and becomes a pillar of salt.","w":"God listens to intercession, and He calls us not to look back at what He is rescuing us from.","q":"Who could I stand in the gap for in prayer?","l":["Three visitors came to Abraham’s tent near the great trees of Mamre. One of them was the Lord Himself. After promising that Sarah would have a son within a year, the visitors set out toward Sodom, and the Lord told Abraham that the outcry against Sodom and Gomorrah was so great and their sin so grievous that He was going to see for Himself.","Abraham stood before the Lord and began to plead. “Will you sweep away the righteous with the wicked? What if there are fifty righteous people in the city?” God said He would spare it for fifty. Abraham, humbly calling himself dust and ashes, kept asking: forty-five, forty, thirty, twenty. Finally he asked, “What if only ten can be found there?” God answered, “For the sake of ten, I will not destroy it.”","Two angels arrived at Sodom in the evening, and Lot insisted they stay at his house. Before they went to bed, every man in the city surrounded the house and demanded that Lot bring the visitors out so they could abuse them. The angels pulled Lot inside and struck the men with blindness. They told Lot to get his family out, because God was about to destroy the city. Lot’s future sons-in-law thought he was joking.","At dawn Lot still hesitated, so the angels took him, his wife and his two daughters by the hand and led them out, because the Lord was merciful to them. “Flee for your lives! Don’t look back!” Then the Lord rained burning sulphur on Sodom and Gomorrah. But Lot’s wife looked back, and she became a pillar of salt. Early the next morning Abraham looked down toward the plain and saw dense smoke rising like smoke from a furnace. God had remembered Abraham, and brought Lot out."],"id":"story-sodom-and-gomorrah"},{"e":"p","t":"Isaac is born","r":"Genesis 18:1-15; 21:1-7","p":"Abraham; Sarah; Isaac","s":"When Sarah overhears a visitor say she will have a son within a year, she laughs to herself, because she is about ninety. The Lord asks, 'Is anything too hard for the Lord?' A year later Isaac is born, and Sarah says God has brought her laughter.","w":"God keeps His word, even when it takes decades and seems impossible.","q":"What have I laughed off as impossible for God?","l":["One hot afternoon, Abraham was sitting at the entrance of his tent near the great trees of Mamre when he looked up and saw three men. He ran to meet them, bowed low and begged them to stay. He hurried to Sarah to bake bread, chose a tender calf, and served them under a tree while he stood nearby.","“Where is your wife Sarah?” they asked. “In the tent.” Then the Lord said, “I will surely return to you about this time next year, and Sarah your wife will have a son.” Sarah was listening at the tent entrance behind him. She was long past the age of childbearing, and she laughed to herself: after I am worn out and my lord is old, will I now have this pleasure? The Lord asked Abraham, “Why did Sarah laugh? Is anything too hard for the Lord?” Sarah was afraid and denied it, but He said, “Yes, you did laugh.”","The Lord was gracious to Sarah, as He had said. She became pregnant and bore Abraham a son in his old age, at the very time God had promised. Abraham was a hundred years old. He named the boy Isaac, which means ‘he laughs’, and circumcised him on the eighth day as God had commanded.","Sarah’s laugh of disbelief became a laugh of joy. “God has brought me laughter,” she said, “and everyone who hears about this will laugh with me. Who would have said to Abraham that Sarah would nurse children? Yet I have borne him a son in his old age.” Twenty-five years after the first promise, God kept His word."],"id":"story-isaac-is-born"},{"e":"p","t":"Abraham offers Isaac","r":"Genesis 22:1-19","p":"Abraham; Isaac","s":"God tests Abraham by asking him to sacrifice Isaac, the son of the promise. Abraham obeys, saying 'God will provide the lamb'. At the last moment God stops him and provides a ram caught in a thicket.","w":"It points straight to the Father giving His own Son.","q":"What am I holding onto too tightly to offer to God?","l":["Abraham waited twenty-five years for the son God promised. Isaac, whose name means ‘he laughs’, was finally born when Abraham was one hundred. Every promise of God was now wrapped up in this boy. Then God tested Abraham: “Take your son, your only son, whom you love, Isaac, and go to the region of Moriah. Sacrifice him there as a burnt offering on a mountain I will show you.”","Early the next morning Abraham got up, saddled his donkey, cut wood and set out with Isaac and two servants. On the third day he saw the place in the distance. He told the servants, “Stay here. The boy and I will go over there. We will worship and then we will come back to you.” Hebrews 11 says Abraham reasoned that God could even raise the dead.","Isaac carried the wood, and Abraham carried the fire and the knife. As they walked, Isaac said, “Father, the fire and wood are here, but where is the lamb for the burnt offering?” Abraham answered, “God himself will provide the lamb, my son.” At the place, Abraham built an altar, arranged the wood, bound his son and laid him on it. He reached out his hand and took the knife.","The angel of the Lord called from heaven, “Abraham! Abraham! Do not lay a hand on the boy. Now I know that you fear God, because you have not withheld from me your son, your only son.” Abraham looked up and saw a ram caught by its horns in a thicket, and sacrificed it instead. He called the place ‘The Lord Will Provide’. Centuries later, on the same hills of Moriah, God did not spare His own Son."],"id":"story-abraham-offers-isaac"},{"e":"p","t":"A bride for Isaac","r":"Genesis 24","p":"Abraham's servant; Rebekah; Isaac","s":"Abraham sends his servant back to his homeland to find a wife for Isaac. At the well the servant asks God for a sign: a woman who offers to water his camels too. Rebekah does exactly that, and agrees to go.","w":"God guides people who pray specifically and act faithfully.","q":"Where do I need to ask God for clear guidance?","l":["Sarah had died, and Abraham was very old. He did not want Isaac to marry one of the Canaanite women, so he sent his most senior servant back to his relatives in Mesopotamia to find a wife for his son. The servant was worried: what if the woman would not come? Abraham assured him that God would send His angel ahead of him.","The servant travelled with ten camels loaded with gifts to the town of Nahor. In the evening, when the women came out to draw water, he stopped by the well and prayed a specific prayer: “May it be that when I say to a young woman, ‘Please let down your jar that I may have a drink,’ and she says, ‘Drink, and I’ll water your camels too,’ let her be the one you have chosen for Isaac.”","Before he had finished praying, Rebekah came out with her jar on her shoulder. She was beautiful, and she was from Abraham’s own family. When the servant asked for a drink, she gave it and then said, “I’ll draw water for your camels too, until they have finished drinking.” Ten thirsty camels drink a great deal of water, and she kept running back and forth to the well. The servant watched in silence, then bowed down and worshipped God for leading him.","At her family’s home the servant told the whole story, and her brother Laban and father Bethuel agreed: “This is from the Lord.” They asked Rebekah herself, “Will you go with this man?” She answered, “I will go.” When the caravan arrived, Isaac was out in the field in the evening to meditate. Rebekah married Isaac, and he loved her, and was comforted after his mother’s death."],"id":"story-a-bride-for-isaac"},{"e":"p","t":"Esau sells his birthright","r":"Genesis 25:19-34","p":"Isaac; Rebekah; Esau; Jacob","s":"Rebekah's twins struggle even in the womb, and God tells her the older will serve the younger. Esau comes in from hunting, famished, and Jacob offers him stew in exchange for his birthright. Esau shrugs it off and sells it for one meal.","w":"Trading what is precious for what is instant.","q":"What lasting thing am I tempted to trade for something quick?","l":["Isaac married Rebekah, but she could not have children, so Isaac prayed for her. The Lord answered, and she became pregnant with twins who jostled each other inside her. When she asked God why, He told her that two nations were in her womb, and the older would serve the younger.","The first baby came out red, his whole body like a hairy garment, so they named him Esau. His brother came out with his hand grasping Esau’s heel, so he was named Jacob. As they grew up, Esau became a skilful hunter, a man of the open country, and Isaac loved him because he enjoyed eating wild game. Jacob was content to stay at home among the tents, and Rebekah loved him.","Once, when Jacob was cooking some stew, Esau came in from the open country, famished. “Quick, let me have some of that red stew! I’m starving!” Jacob saw his chance. “First sell me your birthright.” The birthright meant a double share of the inheritance and leadership of the family after their father.","“Look, I am about to die,” Esau said. “What good is the birthright to me?” Jacob made him swear an oath first, and Esau sold his birthright to Jacob. Jacob gave him bread and lentil stew. Esau ate and drank, and then got up and left. The Bible sums it up in one line: so Esau despised his birthright. Hebrews 12 later warns us not to be like Esau, who for a single meal sold his inheritance rights."],"id":"story-esau-sells-his-birthright"},{"e":"p","t":"Jacob steals the blessing","r":"Genesis 27","p":"Isaac; Rebekah; Jacob; Esau","s":"With his mother's help, Jacob dresses in goat skins and pretends to be his hairy brother Esau. Their blind father Isaac gives Jacob the blessing meant for the firstborn. Esau is furious, and Jacob has to flee.","w":"Grabbing God's promises by deceit costs a family dearly.","q":"Where am I trying to get ahead by my own scheming?","l":["Isaac and Rebekah had twins. Esau was born first, red and hairy, and Jacob came out holding his brother’s heel. Esau grew up a skilful hunter, his father’s favourite, while Jacob was quiet, staying among the tents, his mother’s favourite. Once, when Esau came home starving, Jacob had already persuaded him to sell his birthright for a bowl of red stew.","When Isaac was old and almost blind, he told Esau to hunt some game and make him his favourite meal, so that he could give him his blessing before he died. Rebekah overheard and quickly made a plan. She cooked two young goats the way Isaac liked, dressed Jacob in Esau’s best clothes, and covered his hands and neck with goatskins.","Jacob went in to his father and lied: “I am Esau your firstborn.” Isaac was suspicious. “The voice is the voice of Jacob, but the hands are the hands of Esau.” He asked again, “Are you really my son Esau?” and Jacob said, “I am.” Isaac smelled Esau’s clothes and gave Jacob the blessing: abundance, rule over nations, and lordship over his brothers.","Moments after Jacob left, Esau came in with his meal. Isaac trembled violently when he realised what had happened. Esau burst out with a loud and bitter cry, “Bless me, me too, my father!” but the blessing could not be taken back. Esau held a grudge and planned to kill Jacob once their father died. Rebekah heard, and sent Jacob away to her brother Laban in Haran, saying it would only be for a while. She never saw her favourite son again."],"id":"story-jacob-steals-the-blessing"},{"e":"p","t":"Jacob's ladder","r":"Genesis 28:10-22","p":"Jacob","s":"Running from Esau, Jacob sleeps with a stone for a pillow. He dreams of a stairway to heaven with angels going up and down, and God renews Abraham's promise to him. He wakes saying 'Surely the Lord is in this place, and I was not aware of it'.","w":"God meets us on the run and in our failures.","q":"Where has God been with me without my noticing?","l":["Jacob left Beersheba alone, running from his brother’s anger, with nothing but the blessing he had taken by deceit. When the sun set, he stopped for the night in the open country. He took one of the stones there, put it under his head and lay down to sleep.","He dreamed of a stairway resting on the earth, with its top reaching to heaven, and the angels of God were ascending and descending on it. Above it stood the Lord, who said: “I am the Lord, the God of your father Abraham and the God of Isaac. I will give you and your descendants the land on which you are lying.” Then God added the promise Jacob needed most: “I am with you and will watch over you wherever you go, and I will bring you back to this land. I will not leave you until I have done what I have promised.”","Jacob woke up and said, “Surely the Lord is in this place, and I was not aware of it.” He was afraid, and said, “How awesome is this place! This is none other than the house of God; this is the gate of heaven.”","Early the next morning he set up his stone pillow as a pillar, poured oil on it and named the place Bethel, which means ‘house of God’. He made a vow: if God would be with him, give him food and clothing, and bring him safely home, then the Lord would be his God, and he would give God a tenth of everything. Jesus later told Nathanael that he would see heaven open and the angels ascending and descending on the Son of Man. Jesus Himself is the stairway between heaven and earth."],"id":"story-jacob-s-ladder"},{"e":"p","t":"Jacob, Leah and Rachel","r":"Genesis 29:1-30","p":"Jacob; Laban; Leah; Rachel","s":"Jacob works seven years for Rachel, and they feel like a few days because he loves her. On the wedding night his uncle Laban swaps in her older sister Leah. Jacob works seven more years for Rachel.","w":"The deceiver gets deceived, and God works even through a messy family.","q":"Where am I reaping what I've sown, and what is God teaching me in it?","l":["Jacob arrived in the land of the east and found a well where shepherds were waiting with their flocks. While he was asking about his uncle Laban, Laban’s daughter Rachel arrived with her father’s sheep. Jacob rolled the heavy stone off the well by himself, watered her flock, kissed her and wept aloud. He had found his family.","Jacob stayed and worked for Laban. Laban had two daughters: Leah, the older, had weak or tender eyes, and Rachel was lovely in form and beautiful. Jacob loved Rachel and offered to work seven years for her. So Jacob served seven years to get Rachel, but they seemed like only a few days to him because of his love for her.","When the time came, Laban held a wedding feast. But in the evening he brought his older daughter Leah, veiled, to Jacob instead. When morning came, there was Leah! Jacob, the man who had disguised himself to deceive his blind father, had now been deceived in the dark. He confronted Laban, who replied that it was not their custom to give the younger daughter before the older.","Laban offered Rachel too, if Jacob would complete Leah’s bridal week and work another seven years. Jacob agreed, and he loved Rachel more than Leah. The rivalry between the sisters shaped the whole family. But God saw that Leah was unloved and gave her children first. One of her sons was Judah, from whose line King David and, much later, Jesus would come. God worked His plan even through an unloved wife and a family full of hurt."],"id":"story-jacob-leah-and-rachel"},{"e":"p","t":"Jacob wrestles with God","r":"Genesis 32:22-32","p":"Jacob","s":"The night before meeting Esau, Jacob wrestles with a man until daybreak and refuses to let go without a blessing. The man touches his hip, and Jacob limps afterwards. He is renamed Israel.","w":"God sometimes blesses us by breaking our self-reliance.","q":"What am I wrestling with God about right now?","l":["After twenty years with Laban, God told Jacob to go home. Jacob was now wealthy, with two wives, two concubines, eleven sons and huge flocks. But going home meant facing Esau. When messengers reported that Esau was coming with four hundred men, Jacob was terrified. He prayed, reminding God of His promises and confessing, “I am unworthy of all the kindness and faithfulness you have shown your servant.” He sent wave after wave of gifts ahead to soften his brother.","That night Jacob sent his family across the Jabbok stream and stayed behind alone. A man wrestled with him until daybreak. When the man saw that he could not overpower Jacob, he touched the socket of Jacob’s hip, so that it was wrenched. Still Jacob held on. “Let me go, for it is daybreak,” the man said. Jacob replied, “I will not let you go unless you bless me.”","The man asked, “What is your name?” For a man who had once lied about his name to get a blessing, it was a confession. “Jacob,” he answered, which means ‘he grasps the heel’ or ‘he deceives’. The man said, “Your name will no longer be Jacob, but Israel, because you have struggled with God and with humans and have overcome.” Then he blessed him.","Jacob called the place Peniel, ‘face of God’, saying, “I saw God face to face, and yet my life was spared.” The sun rose as he limped away. When Esau arrived, he ran to meet Jacob, embraced him, threw his arms around his neck and kissed him, and they wept. Jacob said that seeing his brother’s face was like seeing the face of God."],"id":"story-jacob-wrestles-with-god"},{"e":"p","t":"Joseph sold by his brothers","r":"Genesis 37","p":"Joseph; Jacob; Reuben; Judah","s":"Jacob's favourite son Joseph has dreams of his family bowing to him and wears a special robe. His jealous brothers throw him into a pit and sell him to traders going to Egypt. They tell their father he was killed by an animal.","w":"Jealousy tears families apart, but God's plan isn't stopped by a pit.","q":"Is there jealousy in me I need to hand over?","l":["Jacob had twelve sons, but he loved Joseph, Rachel’s son, more than any of the others, and he made him an ornate robe. When the brothers saw that their father loved Joseph more, they hated him and could not speak a kind word to him. At seventeen, Joseph also brought their father a bad report about some of them.","Then Joseph had dreams. In one, his brothers’ sheaves of grain bowed down to his sheaf. In another, the sun, moon and eleven stars bowed down to him. He told his family both dreams. His brothers hated him even more, and even Jacob rebuked him, though he kept the matter in mind.","One day Jacob sent Joseph to check on his brothers, who were grazing the flocks far away near Dothan. They saw him coming in the distance and said, “Here comes that dreamer! Come now, let’s kill him and throw him into one of these cisterns. Then we’ll see what comes of his dreams.” Reuben, the eldest, persuaded them to throw him into an empty cistern alive, planning to rescue him later. They stripped off his robe and threw him in, then sat down to eat.","A caravan of Ishmaelite traders came by on their way to Egypt. Judah said, “What will we gain if we kill our brother? Come, let’s sell him.” They sold Joseph for twenty shekels of silver. Then they dipped his robe in goat’s blood and brought it to their father. Jacob recognised it: “A ferocious animal has devoured him.” He tore his clothes and mourned for many days, refusing to be comforted. Meanwhile, in Egypt, Joseph was sold to Potiphar, the captain of Pharaoh’s guard. God was already at work."],"id":"story-joseph-sold-by-his-brothers"},{"e":"p","t":"Joseph in Potiphar's house and prison","r":"Genesis 39-40","p":"Joseph; Potiphar; Potiphar's wife; The cupbearer; The baker","s":"Joseph serves Potiphar so well that he is put in charge of everything. When Potiphar's wife tries to seduce him day after day, he runs away, and she falsely accuses him. In prison the Lord is still with him. He interprets the dreams of Pharaoh's cupbearer and baker, but the cupbearer forgets him.","w":"Doing right can cost you, but God is with you in the prison too.","q":"Where do I need to stay faithful even when it seems no one notices?","l":["In Egypt, Joseph was bought by Potiphar, captain of Pharaoh’s guard. The Lord was with Joseph, and everything he did prospered. Potiphar noticed, put him in charge of his whole household, and the Lord blessed the Egyptian’s house because of Joseph. Potiphar didn’t concern himself with anything except the food he ate.","Joseph was well built and handsome, and Potiphar’s wife took notice. “Come to bed with me!” she said. Joseph refused: his master trusted him with everything except her. “How then could I do such a wicked thing and sin against God?” She kept pressing him day after day. One day, when no one else was in the house, she grabbed his cloak. Joseph left the cloak in her hand and ran out of the house. She used the cloak to accuse him of attacking her, and Potiphar threw Joseph into the king’s prison.","Even there, the Lord was with Joseph and showed him kindness. The prison warden put him in charge of all the prisoners. Later Pharaoh’s cupbearer and baker were thrown into the same prison, and both had troubling dreams on the same night. Joseph said, “Do not interpretations belong to God? Tell me your dreams.”","The cupbearer had dreamed of a vine with three branches, whose grapes he squeezed into Pharaoh’s cup. Joseph said that within three days he would be restored to his job. “When all goes well with you, remember me and mention me to Pharaoh.” The baker had dreamed of three baskets of bread on his head, with birds eating from the top one. Within three days, Joseph said, he would be executed. Both came true exactly. But the chief cupbearer did not remember Joseph; he forgot him."],"id":"story-joseph-in-potiphar-s-house-and-prison"},{"e":"p","t":"Joseph interprets Pharaoh's dreams","r":"Genesis 41","p":"Joseph; Pharaoh; The cupbearer","s":"Two years later Pharaoh dreams of seven thin cows eating seven fat ones. The cupbearer finally remembers Joseph. Joseph says, 'I cannot do it, but God will give Pharaoh the answer.' He explains seven years of plenty and seven of famine, and Pharaoh makes him second in command of Egypt at thirty years old.","w":"God's timing can be slow, but He lifts people up in a moment.","q":"Am I giving God the credit for my gifts?","l":["Two full years passed. Then Pharaoh dreamed that he was standing by the Nile when seven sleek, fat cows came up out of the river, followed by seven ugly, gaunt cows that ate the fat ones. He dreamed again: seven healthy heads of grain on one stalk were swallowed up by seven thin heads scorched by the east wind. In the morning his mind was troubled, and none of the magicians and wise men of Egypt could interpret the dreams.","Then the chief cupbearer said, “Today I am reminded of my shortcomings.” He told Pharaoh about the young Hebrew in prison. Pharaoh sent for Joseph, who was quickly brought out of the dungeon, shaved and given fresh clothes. Pharaoh said he had heard Joseph could interpret dreams. Joseph replied, “I cannot do it, but God will give Pharaoh the answer he desires.”","Joseph explained that the two dreams were one: God was showing Pharaoh what He was about to do. Seven years of great abundance were coming, followed by seven years of famine so severe that the good years would be forgotten. The dream came twice because the matter was firmly decided by God. Joseph advised Pharaoh to appoint a wise man to store a fifth of the harvest during the good years.","Pharaoh said, “Can we find anyone like this man, one in whom is the spirit of God?” He put Joseph in charge of the whole land of Egypt, gave him his signet ring, fine linen and a gold chain, and had people shout before his chariot, “Make way!” Joseph was thirty years old, thirteen years after his brothers sold him. During the good years he stored grain like the sand of the sea. He had two sons, naming one Manasseh, ‘God has made me forget all my trouble’, and the other Ephraim, ‘God has made me fruitful in the land of my suffering’."],"id":"story-joseph-interprets-pharaoh-s-dreams"},{"e":"p","t":"Joseph forgives his brothers","r":"Genesis 42-45","p":"Joseph; Judah; Benjamin; Jacob","s":"Years later Joseph is ruler over Egypt, and his brothers come to buy grain in a famine. He tests them, and Judah offers himself in place of young Benjamin. Joseph weeps, reveals who he is, and says 'God sent me ahead of you to save lives'.","w":"What others mean for harm, God can use for good.","q":"Who do I need to forgive, trusting God with the story?","l":["In Egypt, through slavery, false accusation and years in prison, the Lord was with Joseph. God gave him the meaning of Pharaoh’s dreams: seven years of plenty followed by seven years of famine. Pharaoh put Joseph in charge of all Egypt to store grain. When the famine came, it reached Canaan too, and Jacob sent ten of his sons to Egypt to buy food. He kept Benjamin, Rachel’s other son, at home.","The brothers bowed before the governor of Egypt with their faces to the ground, not recognising him. Joseph recognised them, and remembered his dreams. He spoke harshly, accused them of being spies, and kept Simeon in prison until they brought their youngest brother. Among themselves the brothers said, “Surely we are being punished because of our brother.” They did not know Joseph understood every word. He turned away and wept.","When the food ran out, Judah promised his father he would personally guarantee Benjamin’s safety. In Egypt Joseph tested them one last time, hiding his silver cup in Benjamin’s sack and then declaring that Benjamin must stay as his slave. Judah, the brother who had once suggested selling Joseph, stepped forward and begged to stay as a slave in Benjamin’s place, so that his father would not die of grief.","Joseph could no longer control himself. He sent everyone else out and wept so loudly that the Egyptians heard. “I am Joseph! Is my father still living?” His brothers were terrified. But Joseph said, “Do not be distressed and do not be angry with yourselves for selling me here, because it was to save lives that God sent me ahead of you.” He kissed all his brothers and wept over them. Years later he would tell them, “You intended to harm me, but God intended it for good.”"],"id":"story-joseph-forgives-his-brothers"},{"e":"p","t":"Job","r":"Job 1-2; 38-42","p":"Job; Satan; Job's friends","s":"Job is blameless, and God allows Satan to take away his wealth, children and health. His friends insist he must have sinned. Job pours out his pain and questions. God answers from the storm with questions of His own, and Job bows in worship. God restores him.","w":"Faith can be honest about pain, and God is bigger than our questions.","q":"What honest question do I need to bring to God?","l":["Job lived in the land of Uz. He was blameless and upright, he feared God and shunned evil. He had seven sons and three daughters and great wealth, and he regularly offered sacrifices for his children in case they had sinned. One day the angels came before the Lord, and Satan came with them. God pointed to Job. Satan answered that Job only served God because God protected and blessed him: take everything away, and he would curse God to his face.","God allowed Satan to test Job, but not to touch his life. In one day messengers arrived one after another: raiders had taken his oxen and donkeys, fire had burned up his sheep, others had taken his camels, and a great wind had collapsed the house where all his children were feasting, killing them all. Job tore his robe, shaved his head and fell to the ground in worship: “The Lord gave and the Lord has taken away; may the name of the Lord be praised.” Then Satan struck Job with painful sores from head to foot. His wife told him to curse God and die, but Job said, “Shall we accept good from God, and not trouble?”","Three friends, Eliphaz, Bildad and Zophar, came to comfort him. For seven days they sat with him in silence, which was the best thing they did. Then the long conversation began. Job cursed the day he was born and poured out his anguish and questions. His friends insisted that God always punishes the wicked, so Job must have sinned. Job refused to accept that, and longed for a chance to put his case before God.","At last the Lord answered Job out of the storm, not with an explanation but with questions: “Where were you when I laid the earth’s foundation?” He took Job on a tour of creation, from the stars to the wild animals. Job replied, “My ears had heard of you but now my eyes have seen you,” and humbled himself. God rebuked the friends for not speaking rightly about Him, and asked Job to pray for them. After Job prayed for his friends, God restored his fortunes and blessed the latter part of his life more than the first."],"id":"story-job"},{"e":"e","t":"Baby Moses in a basket","r":"Exodus 2:1-10","p":"Moses; Miriam; Pharaoh's daughter","s":"Pharaoh orders every Hebrew baby boy thrown into the Nile. Moses' mother hides him, then puts him in a waterproof basket among the reeds. Pharaoh's daughter finds him, and his sister Miriam arranges for his own mother to nurse him.","w":"God protects His purposes through brave, ordinary women.","q":"Where could a small act of courage from me matter?","l":["Generations after Joseph, a new Pharaoh came to power who did not know about him. The Israelites had multiplied greatly, and he saw them as a threat. He made them slaves and worked them ruthlessly, building store cities. When that did not stop them growing, he ordered the Hebrew midwives to kill the baby boys. The midwives Shiphrah and Puah feared God and refused. So Pharaoh gave an order to all his people: every Hebrew boy must be thrown into the Nile.","A Levite woman gave birth to a son. She saw that he was a fine child, and she hid him for three months. When she could hide him no longer, she took a papyrus basket, coated it with tar and pitch, put the baby in it and placed it among the reeds along the bank of the Nile. His sister stood at a distance to see what would happen.","Pharaoh’s daughter came down to the river to bathe. She saw the basket and sent her servant to get it. When she opened it, the baby was crying, and she felt sorry for him. “This is one of the Hebrew babies,” she said. His sister stepped forward and asked, “Shall I go and get one of the Hebrew women to nurse the baby for you?” “Yes, go.” The girl went and got the baby’s own mother.","Pharaoh’s daughter said, “Take this baby and nurse him for me, and I will pay you.” So his mother was paid to raise her own son, safe from the very law meant to kill him. When the child grew older, she took him to Pharaoh’s daughter, who adopted him and named him Moses, saying, “I drew him out of the water.”"],"id":"story-baby-moses-in-a-basket"},{"e":"e","t":"Moses flees to Midian","r":"Exodus 2:11-25","p":"Moses; Jethro; Zipporah","s":"Grown up in Pharaoh's palace, Moses sees an Egyptian beating a Hebrew and kills him. When it becomes known, he flees to Midian. He defends seven sisters at a well, marries Zipporah and spends forty years as a shepherd. Meanwhile God hears Israel groaning.","w":"God's deliverer starts by acting in his own strength and has to wait forty years.","q":"Where am I trying to do God's work my own way?","l":["Moses grew up in Pharaoh’s household, educated in all the wisdom of Egypt. One day, as an adult, he went out to where his own people were labouring. He saw an Egyptian beating a Hebrew. Looking this way and that and seeing no one, he killed the Egyptian and hid him in the sand.","The next day he saw two Hebrews fighting and asked the one in the wrong why he was hitting his fellow Hebrew. The man replied, “Who made you ruler and judge over us? Are you thinking of killing me as you killed the Egyptian?” Moses realised it was known. When Pharaoh heard, he tried to kill Moses, so Moses fled to the land of Midian.","He sat down by a well. The priest of Midian had seven daughters who came to water their father’s flock, but some shepherds drove them away. Moses got up, came to their rescue and watered their flock. Their father, Reuel, also called Jethro, invited him to eat, and Moses agreed to stay. Jethro gave him his daughter Zipporah in marriage, and they had a son, Gershom, whose name sounds like ‘a foreigner there’, because Moses said, “I have become a foreigner in a foreign land.”","Forty years passed. The king of Egypt died, but the Israelites still groaned in their slavery and cried out. Their cry for help went up to God. God heard their groaning, remembered His covenant with Abraham, Isaac and Jacob, looked on the Israelites and was concerned about them. While Moses herded sheep in the wilderness, God was getting ready to act."],"id":"story-moses-flees-to-midian"},{"e":"e","t":"The burning bush","r":"Exodus 3:1-4:17","p":"Moses; Aaron","s":"Moses, now a shepherd in Midian, sees a bush on fire that doesn't burn up. God calls him to free Israel from Egypt and gives His name, 'I AM WHO I AM'. Moses makes excuse after excuse, and God gives him Aaron to speak for him.","w":"God's 'I AM' is the answer to every 'who am I?'.","q":"What excuse am I giving God?","l":["Moses was tending the flock of his father-in-law Jethro, and he led it to the far side of the wilderness to Horeb, the mountain of God. There the angel of the Lord appeared to him in flames of fire from within a bush. Moses saw that though the bush was on fire, it did not burn up, and he went over to look.","God called to him from the bush, “Moses! Moses!” “Here I am.” “Do not come any closer. Take off your sandals, for the place where you are standing is holy ground.” God said He was the God of Abraham, Isaac and Jacob, and Moses hid his face, afraid to look at God. God said, “I have indeed seen the misery of my people in Egypt. I have heard them crying out. So I have come down to rescue them. Now go. I am sending you to Pharaoh.”","Moses said, “Who am I?” God answered, “I will be with you.” Moses asked what God’s name was, in case the Israelites asked. God said, “I AM WHO I AM. Say this: ‘I AM has sent me to you.’” Moses objected that they would not believe him, so God gave him signs: his staff became a snake and back again, and his hand became leprous and was healed.","Still Moses resisted. “I have never been eloquent. I am slow of speech and tongue.” God said, “Who gave human beings their mouths? Now go; I will help you speak.” Finally Moses said, “Please send someone else.” The Lord’s anger burned, but He provided: Moses’ brother Aaron was already on his way to meet him and would speak for him. Moses took the staff of God in his hand and set out for Egypt."],"id":"story-the-burning-bush"},{"e":"e","t":"The plagues and the Passover","r":"Exodus 7-12","p":"Moses; Aaron; Pharaoh","s":"Pharaoh refuses to let Israel go, and God sends ten plagues on Egypt. In the last, the firstborn die, but the angel passes over every house with lamb's blood on the doorframe. Pharaoh finally lets the people go.","w":"The Passover lamb is a picture of Jesus, whose blood covers us.","q":"What does it mean to me that I am covered by the blood of the Lamb?","l":["Moses and Aaron went to Pharaoh and said, “This is what the Lord says: Let my people go.” Pharaoh answered, “Who is the Lord, that I should obey him?” and made the slaves’ work harder, forcing them to gather their own straw for the bricks. But God told Moses that He would show His power, and Egypt would know that He is the Lord.","Plague after plague struck Egypt. The Nile turned to blood. Frogs swarmed into houses, then gnats, then flies. The livestock died. Boils broke out on people and animals. Hail destroyed the crops, locusts ate what was left, and a darkness that could be felt covered the land for three days, while the Israelites had light in their homes. Each time Pharaoh hardened his heart, sometimes promising to let them go and then changing his mind.","God announced one last plague. At midnight every firstborn in Egypt would die. But each Israelite family was to take a year-old lamb without defect, slaughter it at twilight, and put some of its blood on the sides and tops of their doorframes. They were to eat it roasted, with bitter herbs and bread without yeast, dressed to travel. “When I see the blood, I will pass over you.” This was to be a lasting ordinance, the Passover.","At midnight the Lord struck down every firstborn in Egypt, from Pharaoh’s son to the firstborn of the prisoner, and there was loud wailing throughout the land. But He passed over the houses marked with blood. Pharaoh summoned Moses and Aaron in the night: “Up! Leave my people!” The Egyptians urged them to hurry and gave them silver, gold and clothing. After 430 years, all the Lord’s people left Egypt. Centuries later, at Passover, Jesus became our Passover lamb."],"id":"story-the-plagues-and-the-passover"},{"e":"e","t":"Crossing the Red Sea","r":"Exodus 14","p":"Moses; Pharaoh","s":"Pharaoh changes his mind and chases Israel to the sea. Moses says 'The Lord will fight for you; you need only to be still'. God parts the water, Israel crosses on dry ground, and the sea returns over the Egyptian army.","w":"God makes a way where there is none.","q":"What 'sea' am I facing that only God can open?","l":["God led the people out not by the shortest road but through the desert toward the Red Sea, going ahead of them in a pillar of cloud by day and a pillar of fire by night. Then God told Moses to turn back and camp by the sea, so that Pharaoh would think they were lost. Pharaoh changed his mind about letting his slaves go, and chased them with six hundred of his best chariots and all the army of Egypt.","When the Israelites looked up and saw the Egyptians marching after them, with the sea in front of them, they were terrified and cried out. “Was it because there were no graves in Egypt that you brought us to the desert to die?” Moses answered, “Do not be afraid. Stand firm and you will see the deliverance the Lord will bring you today. The Lord will fight for you; you need only to be still.”","The pillar of cloud moved behind them, bringing darkness to the Egyptians and light to Israel, so neither side came near the other all night. Moses stretched out his hand over the sea, and all that night the Lord drove the sea back with a strong east wind and turned it into dry land. The Israelites went through the sea on dry ground, with a wall of water on their right and on their left.","The Egyptians pursued them into the sea. God threw them into confusion and jammed their chariot wheels. “Let’s get away from the Israelites! The Lord is fighting for them!” At daybreak Moses stretched out his hand again, and the sea went back to its place, covering the whole army. Not one survived. When the Israelites saw the great power of the Lord, they feared Him and put their trust in Him. Moses and Miriam led the people in singing: “The Lord is my strength and my defence; he has become my salvation.”"],"id":"story-crossing-the-red-sea"},{"e":"e","t":"Manna and quail","r":"Exodus 16","p":"Moses; Aaron","s":"In the desert the people grumble about food. God sends quail in the evening and manna every morning, enough for each day. Anything kept overnight rots, except a double portion on the day before the Sabbath.","w":"God provides daily bread, one day at a time.","q":"Am I trusting God for today, or hoarding for tomorrow?","l":["About six weeks after leaving Egypt, the whole community came into the Desert of Sin and began to grumble against Moses and Aaron. “If only we had died in Egypt! There we sat around pots of meat and ate all the food we wanted, but you have brought us out into this desert to starve.”","God said, “I will rain down bread from heaven for you.” That evening quail came and covered the camp. In the morning there was a layer of dew, and when it lifted, thin flakes like frost were on the ground. The Israelites asked each other, “What is it?”, which in Hebrew is ‘manna’. Moses said, “It is the bread the Lord has given you to eat.” It was white like coriander seed and tasted like wafers made with honey.","Everyone was to gather as much as they needed, about two litres a person. Some gathered much and some little, but when they measured it, those who gathered much did not have too much, and those who gathered little did not have too little. No one was to keep any until morning. Some did anyway, and it was full of maggots and began to smell.","On the sixth day they were to gather twice as much, because the seventh day was a Sabbath. The extra kept fresh overnight. Some still went out on the Sabbath to look for manna and found none. Aaron kept a jar of manna before the Lord as a reminder for future generations. The Israelites ate manna every day for forty years, until they reached Canaan. Jesus later said, “I am the bread of life.”"],"id":"story-manna-and-quail"},{"e":"e","t":"Water from the rock","r":"Exodus 17:1-7","p":"Moses; Aaron","s":"At Rephidim there is no water, and the people quarrel with Moses, ready to stone him. God tells Moses to strike the rock at Horeb with his staff, and water flows out for everyone. Moses names the place Massah and Meribah, testing and quarrelling.","w":"God provides even when we complain; Paul says that rock was Christ.","q":"Am I testing God, or trusting Him in my dry places?","l":["The whole community travelled on from place to place as the Lord commanded, and camped at Rephidim, but there was no water to drink. The people quarrelled with Moses: “Give us water to drink!” Moses replied, “Why do you quarrel with me? Why do you put the Lord to the test?”","The people were thirsty and grumbled, “Why did you bring us up out of Egypt to make us, our children and our livestock die of thirst?” Moses cried out to the Lord, “What am I to do with these people? They are almost ready to stone me.”","The Lord told Moses to go ahead of the people with some of the elders, taking the staff he had used to strike the Nile. “I will stand there before you by the rock at Horeb. Strike the rock, and water will come out of it for the people to drink.” Moses did so in the sight of the elders, and water poured out.","He called the place Massah, which means testing, and Meribah, which means quarrelling, because the Israelites quarrelled and tested the Lord by saying, “Is the Lord among us or not?” Years later, in 1 Corinthians 10, Paul wrote that they all drank from the spiritual rock that accompanied them, and that rock was Christ."],"id":"story-water-from-the-rock"},{"e":"e","t":"Hands held up","r":"Exodus 17:8-16","p":"Moses; Joshua; Aaron; Hur","s":"The Amalekites attack, and Joshua leads Israel into battle. While Moses holds up his hands on the hill, Israel wins; when his arms tire, they lose. Aaron and Hur sit him on a stone and hold up his hands until sunset.","w":"Even leaders need others to hold up their arms.","q":"Whose arms could I hold up this week?","l":["While Israel was camped at Rephidim, the Amalekites came and attacked them. Moses said to Joshua, “Choose some of our men and go out to fight the Amalekites. Tomorrow I will stand on top of the hill with the staff of God in my hands.” This is the first time we meet Joshua in the Bible.","Joshua fought the Amalekites as Moses had ordered, and Moses, Aaron and Hur went up to the top of the hill. As long as Moses held up his hands, the Israelites were winning, but whenever he lowered his hands, the Amalekites were winning.","When Moses’ hands grew tired, Aaron and Hur took a stone and put it under him, and he sat on it. Aaron held up one hand and Hur the other, so that his hands remained steady till sunset. So Joshua overcame the Amalekite army.","God told Moses to write this down on a scroll as something to be remembered, and to make sure Joshua heard it. Moses built an altar and called it ‘The Lord is my Banner’. The victory was won in the valley with swords, on the hill with lifted hands, and by two friends who would not let their leader’s arms drop."],"id":"story-hands-held-up"},{"e":"e","t":"Jethro's advice","r":"Exodus 18","p":"Moses; Jethro","s":"Moses' father-in-law visits and watches Moses judge the people's disputes from morning till evening. 'What you are doing is not good,' he says. 'You will wear yourself out.' He advises Moses to appoint capable, trustworthy men over groups of thousands, hundreds, fifties and tens.","w":"Even great leaders need to share the load.","q":"Am I carrying something alone that I should share?","l":["Jethro, priest of Midian and Moses’ father-in-law, heard everything God had done for Israel. He came to the camp near the mountain of God, bringing Moses’ wife Zipporah and his two sons. Moses went out to meet him, bowed down and kissed him, and told him all the Lord had done to Pharaoh and how He had saved them along the way. Jethro was delighted. “Praise be to the Lord. Now I know that the Lord is greater than all other gods.”","The next day Moses took his seat to serve as judge for the people, and they stood around him from morning till evening. Jethro watched all of it and asked, “What is this you are doing? Why do you alone sit as judge, while all these people stand around you?” Moses explained that the people came to him to seek God’s will and settle disputes.","Jethro replied, “What you are doing is not good. You and these people will only wear yourselves out. The work is too heavy for you; you cannot handle it alone.” He advised Moses to keep representing the people before God and teaching them God’s decrees, but to select capable men who feared God, were trustworthy and hated dishonest gain. They should serve as officials over thousands, hundreds, fifties and tens, and bring only the difficult cases to Moses.","“If you do this and God so commands, you will be able to stand the strain, and all these people will go home satisfied.” Moses listened to his father-in-law and did everything he said. A good leader learned to listen, and a whole nation was better served because one man shared the load."],"id":"story-jethro-s-advice"},{"e":"e","t":"The Ten Commandments","r":"Exodus 19-20","p":"Moses","s":"At Mount Sinai, with thunder, smoke and a trumpet blast, God gives Israel the Ten Commandments. They begin with 'I am the Lord your God, who brought you out of Egypt'.","w":"God rescues first, then shows His people how to live as His.","q":"Which commandment is God putting His finger on in my life?","l":["Three months after leaving Egypt, the Israelites arrived at the Desert of Sinai and camped in front of the mountain. God called Moses up and gave him a message for the people. They had seen what He did to Egypt and how He carried them, as if on eagles’ wings, to Himself. If they obeyed Him and kept His covenant, they would be His treasured possession, a kingdom of priests and a holy nation. The people answered, “We will do everything the Lord has said.”","For three days the people prepared and washed their clothes. Boundaries were set around the mountain. On the third day there was thunder and lightning, a thick cloud over the mountain and a very loud trumpet blast. Everyone in the camp trembled. Mount Sinai was covered with smoke, because the Lord descended on it in fire, and the whole mountain shook violently.","Then God spoke all these words, starting with who He is: “I am the Lord your God, who brought you out of Egypt, out of the land of slavery.” Then: have no other gods before me. Do not make idols. Do not misuse the name of the Lord your God. Remember the Sabbath day by keeping it holy. Honour your father and mother. Do not murder. Do not commit adultery. Do not steal. Do not give false testimony. Do not covet.","The people were afraid and stayed at a distance. They said to Moses, “Speak to us yourself and we will listen. But do not have God speak to us or we will die.” Moses said, “Do not be afraid. God has come to test you, so that the fear of God will be with you to keep you from sinning.” Jesus later summed up the whole law: love God with all your heart, soul and mind, and love your neighbour as yourself."],"id":"story-the-ten-commandments"},{"e":"e","t":"The golden calf","r":"Exodus 32","p":"Moses; Aaron","s":"While Moses is on the mountain, the people pressure Aaron into making a golden calf and worship it. God is angry, and Moses pleads for the people. He comes down, smashes the stone tablets and grinds the calf to powder.","w":"We quickly make idols when God seems slow.","q":"What am I tempted to put in God's place when I'm waiting?","l":["Moses went up the mountain and stayed there forty days and forty nights, receiving God’s instructions. The people grew impatient. They gathered around Aaron and said, “Come, make us gods who will go before us. As for this fellow Moses who brought us up out of Egypt, we don’t know what has happened to him.”","Aaron told them to bring their gold earrings. He melted them down and shaped a calf with a tool. The people said, “These are your gods, Israel, who brought you up out of Egypt.” Aaron built an altar in front of it and announced, “Tomorrow there will be a festival to the Lord.” The next day they offered sacrifices, then sat down to eat and drink and got up to indulge in revelry.","God told Moses what was happening and said He would destroy the people and make a great nation from Moses instead. Moses pleaded with God, reminding Him of His promises to Abraham, Isaac and Israel, and the Lord relented. Moses came down carrying the two stone tablets written by the finger of God. When he saw the calf and the dancing, his anger burned. He threw the tablets down, breaking them to pieces at the foot of the mountain. He burned the calf, ground it to powder, scattered it on water and made the Israelites drink it.","Moses asked Aaron, “What did these people do to you, that you led them into such great sin?” Aaron blamed the people and said, “I threw the gold into the fire, and out came this calf!” Discipline followed. Then Moses went back to the Lord and offered himself for the people: “Please forgive their sin, but if not, then blot me out of the book you have written.” God called Moses back up the mountain, and Moses carved new tablets."],"id":"story-the-golden-calf"},{"e":"e","t":"The twelve spies","r":"Numbers 13-14","p":"Moses; Joshua; Caleb","s":"Twelve spies explore Canaan for forty days and bring back a cluster of grapes so big two men carry it on a pole. Ten say the people are giants and the cities walled. Only Joshua and Caleb say God will give them the land. The people weep and rebel, and wander forty years in the desert.","w":"Fear of the giants can keep you out of God's promises.","q":"Which report am I believing about my situation?","l":["The Lord told Moses to send men to explore the land of Canaan, one leader from each of the twelve tribes. Among them were Caleb from Judah and Hoshea son of Nun, whom Moses renamed Joshua. Moses told them to see whether the people were strong or weak, the towns walled or not, and the soil fertile or poor, and to bring back some fruit.","They explored the land for forty days. At the Valley of Eshkol they cut off a single cluster of grapes so big that two of them carried it on a pole between them, along with pomegranates and figs. When they returned, they reported, “We went into the land, and it does flow with milk and honey! Here is its fruit. But the people who live there are powerful, and the cities are fortified and very large. We even saw descendants of Anak there.”","Caleb silenced the people and said, “We should go up and take possession of the land, for we can certainly do it.” But the other ten said, “We can’t attack those people; they are stronger than we are. We seemed like grasshoppers in our own eyes, and we looked the same to them.” That night the whole community wept aloud and talked of choosing a leader to go back to Egypt. Joshua and Caleb tore their clothes and pleaded: “If the Lord is pleased with us, he will lead us into that land. Do not be afraid of the people. The Lord is with us.” The people talked about stoning them.","The glory of the Lord appeared, and God pronounced judgement: that generation would not enter the land. They would wander in the desert forty years, a year for each day of exploring, until everyone twenty and older had died, except Caleb and Joshua. The ten spies who spread the bad report died of a plague. God said of Caleb, “Because my servant Caleb has a different spirit and follows me wholeheartedly, I will bring him into the land he went to.”"],"id":"story-the-twelve-spies"},{"e":"e","t":"The bronze snake","r":"Numbers 21:4-9","p":"Moses","s":"The people complain against God again, and venomous snakes bite them. God tells Moses to make a bronze snake and put it on a pole. Anyone bitten who looks at it lives.","w":"Jesus uses this story about Himself being lifted up on the cross (John 3:14).","q":"What do I need to look to Jesus for today?","l":["Near the end of the forty years in the desert, the Israelites travelled around the land of Edom by the route to the Red Sea. The people grew impatient on the way and spoke against God and against Moses. “Why have you brought us up out of Egypt to die in the wilderness? There is no bread! There is no water! And we detest this miserable food!” They meant the manna God sent every morning.","Then the Lord sent venomous snakes among them. They bit the people, and many Israelites died. The people came to Moses and said, “We sinned when we spoke against the Lord and against you. Pray that the Lord will take the snakes away from us.” So Moses prayed for the people.","The Lord did not simply take the snakes away. He said to Moses, “Make a snake and put it up on a pole; anyone who is bitten can look at it and live.” So Moses made a bronze snake and put it up on a pole. Then when anyone was bitten by a snake and looked at the bronze snake, they lived.","Centuries later, Jesus told Nicodemus, “Just as Moses lifted up the snake in the wilderness, so the Son of Man must be lifted up, that everyone who believes may have eternal life in him.” The cure was to look in faith at what God had lifted up. Sadly, by King Hezekiah’s day the Israelites had started burning incense to that bronze snake, and he had to break it into pieces."],"id":"story-the-bronze-snake"},{"e":"e","t":"Balaam's donkey","r":"Numbers 22","p":"Balaam; Balak","s":"King Balak of Moab hires the prophet Balaam to curse Israel. On the way, Balaam's donkey sees the angel of the Lord blocking the road and refuses to go on. When Balaam beats her, the donkey speaks. In the end Balaam can only bless Israel.","w":"No one can curse what God has blessed.","q":"Where do I need to trust God's blessing over me?","l":["As Israel camped on the plains of Moab, King Balak was terrified of them. He sent messengers with payment to Balaam, a famous prophet, asking him to come and curse Israel. God told Balaam, “Do not go with them. You must not put a curse on those people, because they are blessed.” Balak sent more important officials and promised a great reward. This time God allowed Balaam to go, but only to do what God told him.","Balaam got up in the morning, saddled his donkey and went. But God was angry, and the angel of the Lord stood in the road to oppose him. The donkey saw the angel with a drawn sword and turned off into a field. Balaam beat her. Then the angel stood in a narrow path between two vineyard walls, and the donkey pressed against the wall, crushing Balaam’s foot. He beat her again. In a place too narrow to turn, the donkey lay down under Balaam, and he beat her with his staff.","Then the Lord opened the donkey’s mouth, and she said, “What have I done to you to make you beat me these three times?” Balaam answered, as if talking donkeys were normal, “You have made a fool of me! If only I had a sword in my hand, I would kill you right now.” The donkey said, “Am I not your own donkey, which you have always ridden? Have I been in the habit of doing this to you?” “No.”","Then the Lord opened Balaam’s eyes, and he saw the angel with his sword drawn, and he fell face down. The angel said the donkey had saved his life. Balaam went on to Balak, but three times, from three different hilltops, when he opened his mouth to curse Israel, only blessings came out. Balak was furious. Balaam said, “I must speak only what the Lord puts in my mouth.” His last prophecy said: “A star will come out of Jacob; a sceptre will rise out of Israel.”"],"id":"story-balaam-s-donkey"},{"e":"e","t":"Moses sees the promised land","r":"Deuteronomy 34","p":"Moses; Joshua","s":"At 120 years old, Moses climbs Mount Nebo. God shows him the whole promised land, but tells him he will not cross over. Moses dies there and God buries him. Joshua, filled with the spirit of wisdom, leads the people on.","w":"God's work is bigger than one leader's lifetime.","q":"What am I building that others will finish?","l":["After forty years in the wilderness, the Israelites camped on the plains of Moab across the Jordan from Jericho. Moses had led them all that way. But because of an earlier moment when he struck the rock in anger instead of speaking to it as God commanded, God had told him he would not enter the land. In Deuteronomy, Moses gives his final speeches, reminding the people of everything God had done and calling them to love the Lord their God with all their heart, soul and strength.","Moses blessed each of the tribes. He laid his hands on Joshua and commissioned him to lead. Then he climbed Mount Nebo from the plains of Moab to the top of Pisgah, across from Jericho. There the Lord showed him the whole land: Gilead as far as Dan, all of Naphtali, the territory of Ephraim and Manasseh, all of Judah as far as the Mediterranean Sea, the Negev and the whole region from the Valley of Jericho, the City of Palms, as far as Zoar.","The Lord said, “This is the land I promised on oath to Abraham, Isaac and Jacob. I have let you see it with your eyes, but you will not cross over into it.” Moses the servant of the Lord died there in Moab, as the Lord had said. God buried him, and to this day no one knows where his grave is. He was 120 years old, yet his eyes were not weak nor his strength gone. The Israelites grieved for him for thirty days.","Joshua was filled with the spirit of wisdom because Moses had laid his hands on him, and the people listened to him. The book ends: since then, no prophet has risen in Israel like Moses, whom the Lord knew face to face. But Moses did finally stand in the promised land. Centuries later he appeared with Elijah on the mountain of transfiguration, talking with Jesus."],"id":"story-moses-sees-the-promised-land"},{"e":"j","t":"Rahab and the spies","r":"Joshua 2","p":"Rahab; Joshua","s":"Joshua sends two spies to Jericho, and Rahab hides them on her roof under stalks of flax. She says she believes the Lord is God in heaven and on earth. She hangs a scarlet cord in her window so her family will be spared.","w":"God saves an unlikely outsider who believes; Rahab ends up in Jesus' family line.","q":"Do I write anyone off as too far from God?","l":["After Moses died, Joshua prepared to lead Israel across the Jordan. He secretly sent two spies from Shittim: “Go, look over the land, especially Jericho.” They went and entered the house of a prostitute named Rahab, which was built into the city wall, and stayed there.","The king of Jericho was told that Israelites had come to spy, and he sent orders to Rahab to bring them out. But she had taken the two men up to the roof and hidden them under stalks of flax she had laid out. She told the king’s men that yes, men had come, but they had left at dusk when the gate was about to close. “Go after them quickly. You may catch up with them.” The men set out toward the Jordan fords, and the city gate was shut.","Before the spies lay down, Rahab went up to the roof. “I know that the Lord has given you this land. We have heard how the Lord dried up the Red Sea for you. Our hearts melted in fear, for the Lord your God is God in heaven above and on the earth below.” She asked them to swear by the Lord to spare her father, mother, brothers, sisters and all who belonged to them. “Our lives for your lives!” they promised.","She let them down by a rope through the window, since her house was part of the wall, and told them to hide in the hills for three days. The spies told her to tie a scarlet cord in the window and gather her family inside. When Jericho fell, Joshua kept that promise. Rahab and her family were spared and lived among the Israelites. She married Salmon, became the mother of Boaz, and is named in Matthew 1 among the ancestors of Jesus."],"id":"story-rahab-and-the-spies"},{"e":"j","t":"Crossing the Jordan","r":"Joshua 3-4","p":"Joshua; The priests","s":"The Jordan is in flood when Israel needs to cross. The priests carrying the ark step into the water, and as their feet touch it, the river stops flowing and piles up upstream. The whole nation crosses on dry ground. Twelve men carry twelve stones from the riverbed to build a memorial.","w":"God often asks us to step in before the water moves; then we build reminders.","q":"What 'memorial stones' remind me of what God has done?","l":["Early in the morning Joshua and all the Israelites set out from Shittim and camped at the Jordan before crossing. It was harvest time, when the Jordan overflows its banks. Joshua told the people, “Consecrate yourselves, for tomorrow the Lord will do amazing things among you.” The officers told them to follow the ark of the covenant, carried by the priests, keeping a distance of about a kilometre so they could see the way.","God told Joshua, “Today I will begin to exalt you in the eyes of all Israel, so they may know that I am with you as I was with Moses.” The priests were to go and stand in the river. As soon as the priests carrying the ark reached the Jordan and their feet touched the water’s edge, the water from upstream stopped flowing. It piled up in a heap a great distance away, at a town called Adam, while the water flowing down to the Dead Sea was completely cut off.","The priests stood on dry ground in the middle of the Jordan while all Israel passed by, until the whole nation had crossed on dry ground. Joshua had chosen twelve men, one from each tribe. Each took a stone from the middle of the riverbed, from the spot where the priests stood, and carried it on his shoulder to the camp at Gilgal.","As soon as the priests came up out of the river and their feet touched dry ground, the waters returned and flooded as before. Joshua set up the twelve stones at Gilgal. In time their children would ask what the stones meant, and they were to tell them that Israel crossed the Jordan on dry ground. God did it so that all the peoples of the earth would know that His hand is powerful, and so that Israel would always fear Him."],"id":"story-crossing-the-jordan"},{"e":"j","t":"The walls of Jericho","r":"Joshua 6","p":"Joshua","s":"God tells Israel to march around Jericho once a day for six days, and seven times on the seventh. Then the priests blow trumpets, the people shout, and the walls fall down flat.","w":"God's battle plans often look strange, but obedience wins.","q":"What strange instruction is God asking me to keep obeying?","l":["Jericho was tightly shut up because of the Israelites. No one went out and no one came in. Near the city, Joshua looked up and saw a man with a drawn sword. “Are you for us or for our enemies?” “Neither, but as commander of the army of the Lord I have now come.” Joshua fell face down in reverence, and was told to take off his sandals, for the place was holy.","Then the Lord gave Joshua an unusual battle plan. The armed men were to march around the city once a day for six days, with seven priests carrying rams’ horn trumpets in front of the ark. On the seventh day they were to march around seven times with the trumpets sounding. At one long blast the whole army was to shout, and the wall would collapse.","For six days they marched around Jericho once and returned to camp. Joshua had commanded the people not to say a word until the day he told them to shout. On the seventh day they got up at daybreak and marched around the city seven times. On the seventh time, when the priests sounded the trumpet blast, Joshua commanded, “Shout! For the Lord has given you the city!”","When the trumpets sounded, the army shouted, and at the sound of the shout, the wall collapsed. Everyone charged straight in and took the city. Joshua sent the two spies to bring out Rahab and her whole family, just as they had sworn. The silver, gold and articles of bronze and iron were set aside for the Lord’s treasury. Hebrews 11 says that by faith the walls of Jericho fell, after the army had marched around them for seven days."],"id":"story-the-walls-of-jericho"},{"e":"j","t":"Achan's hidden sin","r":"Joshua 7","p":"Achan; Joshua","s":"After Jericho, Israel is unexpectedly defeated at the small town of Ai. God reveals that Achan secretly took silver, gold and a robe from Jericho and buried them in his tent. The sin is exposed and dealt with.","w":"Hidden sin affects the whole community.","q":"What am I hiding that I need to bring into the light?","l":["After Jericho, everything in the city was devoted to the Lord, and no one was to take any of it for themselves. But Achan, from the tribe of Judah, took some of the devoted things, and the Lord’s anger burned against Israel. Nobody else knew.","Joshua sent spies to the small town of Ai. They came back saying not all the army needed to go; two or three thousand men would be enough. About three thousand went up, but the men of Ai routed them, chasing them from the city gate and killing about thirty-six. The hearts of the people melted in fear. Joshua tore his clothes and fell face down before the ark until evening, asking why God had brought them across the Jordan only to be defeated.","The Lord said, “Stand up! What are you doing down on your face? Israel has sinned. They have taken some of the devoted things; they have stolen, they have lied, they have put them with their own possessions.” The next morning Joshua brought the tribes forward, then the clans, then the families, and finally Achan was singled out.","Joshua said, “My son, give glory to the Lord, and tell me what you have done; do not hide it from me.” Achan confessed: “When I saw in the plunder a beautiful robe from Babylonia, two hundred shekels of silver and a bar of gold weighing fifty shekels, I coveted them and took them. They are hidden in the ground inside my tent.” Messengers found them there. Achan and all that was his were brought to the Valley of Achor and judged. After that, Israel went up against Ai again and God gave them victory. Achan’s steps are the same as Eve’s: I saw, I coveted, I took, I hid."],"id":"story-achan-s-hidden-sin"},{"e":"j","t":"The sun stands still","r":"Joshua 10:1-15","p":"Joshua","s":"Five Amorite kings attack the town of Gibeon, Israel's ally, and Joshua marches all night to defend it. God sends huge hailstones, and at Joshua's prayer the sun stands still until the battle is won.","w":"God answers bold prayer.","q":"What bold prayer have I stopped praying?","l":["The people of Gibeon, a large city, had tricked Israel into making a peace treaty by pretending to come from a distant land. When the news spread, Adoni-Zedek, king of Jerusalem, called on four other Amorite kings: Hebron, Jarmuth, Lachish and Eglon. Together they attacked Gibeon to punish it for making peace with Israel.","The Gibeonites sent word to Joshua: “Do not abandon your servants. Come up to us quickly and save us!” Even though the treaty had been made by deceit, Israel kept its word. The Lord said to Joshua, “Do not be afraid of them; I have given them into your hand.” After an all-night march from Gilgal, Joshua took them by surprise.","The Lord threw the Amorites into confusion before Israel, who defeated them at Gibeon and chased them down the road to Beth Horon. As they fled, the Lord hurled large hailstones down on them, and more of them died from the hail than were killed by the swords of the Israelites.","On the day the Lord gave the Amorites over, Joshua said to the Lord in the presence of Israel: “Sun, stand still over Gibeon, and you, moon, over the Valley of Aijalon.” So the sun stood still, and the moon stopped, till the nation avenged itself on its enemies. The sun stopped in the middle of the sky and delayed going down about a full day. The writer adds: there has never been a day like it before or since, a day when the Lord listened to a human being. Surely the Lord was fighting for Israel!"],"id":"story-the-sun-stands-still"},{"e":"j","t":"Caleb asks for his mountain","r":"Joshua 14:6-15","p":"Caleb; Joshua","s":"Forty-five years after the spies' report, 85-year-old Caleb reminds Joshua of Moses' promise. 'I am still as strong today as the day Moses sent me out.' He asks for the hill country of Hebron, where the giants live, and Joshua blesses him.","w":"Wholehearted faith doesn't retire.","q":"What 'mountain' is God still calling me to take?","l":["The land was being divided among the tribes at Gilgal when the men of Judah came to Joshua, and Caleb son of Jephunneh spoke up. He reminded Joshua of what the Lord had said to Moses about the two of them. He was forty when Moses sent him to explore the land, and he brought back an honest report. The others made the people’s hearts melt with fear, but he had followed the Lord wholeheartedly.","Moses had sworn that the land Caleb’s feet had walked on would be his inheritance and his children’s forever. God had kept him alive for forty-five years since then, through all the wilderness wandering. “So here I am today, eighty-five years old!” He said he was still as strong as the day Moses sent him out, and just as ready for battle.","Caleb did not ask for an easy retirement plot. He asked for the hill country the Lord had promised him that day. The Anakites were there, in large fortified cities, but with the Lord’s help he would drive them out. The very giants that had terrified Israel forty-five years before were the ones Caleb wanted to face.","Joshua blessed Caleb and gave him Hebron as his inheritance. It has belonged to Caleb’s descendants ever since, the writer says, because he followed the Lord, the God of Israel, wholeheartedly. Later in Joshua 15 Caleb drives out the three sons of Anak from Hebron. Then the land had rest from war."],"id":"story-caleb-asks-for-his-mountain"},{"e":"j","t":"Choose this day","r":"Joshua 24","p":"Joshua","s":"Old Joshua gathers the tribes at Shechem and retells God's faithfulness from Abraham to the conquest. Then he challenges them to throw away their foreign gods and choose whom they will serve. 'But as for me and my household, we will serve the Lord.'","w":"Faith is a daily choice, and households choose together.","q":"What do I need to choose again today?","l":["After a long time had passed and the Lord had given Israel rest from all their enemies, Joshua, by then very old, summoned all the tribes of Israel to Shechem. They presented themselves before God: the elders, leaders, judges and officials.","Joshua spoke for the Lord and retold their whole story. Long ago their ancestors, including Terah the father of Abraham, lived beyond the Euphrates and worshipped other gods. God took Abraham and led him through Canaan and gave him Isaac. He gave Isaac Jacob and Esau. He sent Moses and Aaron and brought them out of Egypt, through the sea, through the wilderness and into the land. “I gave you a land on which you did not toil and cities you did not build; you live in them and eat from vineyards and olive groves that you did not plant.”","He called them to fear the Lord, serve Him faithfully and throw away the gods their ancestors had worshipped. If serving the Lord seemed undesirable to them, they should choose that day whom they would serve. “But as for me and my household, we will serve the Lord.”","The people answered that they would serve the Lord. Joshua pushed them: “You are not able to serve the Lord. He is a holy God; he is a jealous God.” They insisted, “No! We will serve the Lord.” Joshua made a covenant with them that day, wrote the words in the Book of the Law of God, and set up a large stone under the oak near the holy place as a witness. Not long after, Joshua died at 110 years old. Israel served the Lord throughout his lifetime and the lifetime of the elders who outlived him."],"id":"story-choose-this-day"},{"e":"j","t":"Ehud the left-handed judge","r":"Judges 3:12-30","p":"Ehud; Eglon","s":"Israel does evil and Eglon, the very fat king of Moab, oppresses them for eighteen years. They cry out, and God raises up Ehud, a left-handed man. He hides a short sword on his right thigh, gets a private audience with the king and kills him, then leads Israel to victory.","w":"God uses people the world overlooks, even in unusual ways.","q":"What do I see as a weakness that God could use?","l":["After Joshua’s generation died, a pattern began in the book of Judges: Israel did evil, God handed them over to enemies, they cried out, and God raised up a deliverer. This time the Lord gave Eglon king of Moab power over Israel. With the Ammonites and Amalekites he took possession of Jericho, the City of Palms, and the Israelites were subject to him for eighteen years.","Again the Israelites cried out to the Lord, and He gave them a deliverer: Ehud, a left-handed man from the tribe of Benjamin. Israel sent him with tribute to Eglon. Ehud had made a double-edged sword about half a metre long, which he strapped to his right thigh under his clothing, where guards checking a right-handed man would not expect a weapon. The text adds that Eglon was a very fat man.","After presenting the tribute, Ehud sent the carriers away. He turned back at the stone images near Gilgal and said, “Your Majesty, I have a secret message for you.” The king sent his attendants out. As Eglon sat alone in the upper room of his palace, Ehud said, “I have a message from God for you,” and as the king rose from his seat, Ehud reached with his left hand, drew the sword from his right thigh and plunged it into the king’s belly. Even the handle sank in after the blade, and the fat closed over it.","Ehud locked the doors and escaped. The servants found the doors locked and assumed the king was relieving himself, so they waited, to the point of embarrassment, before finding him dead. By then Ehud had reached the hill country of Ephraim, blown a trumpet and gathered Israel. “Follow me, for the Lord has given Moab, your enemy, into your hands.” They seized the fords of the Jordan and struck down about ten thousand Moabite soldiers. The land had peace for eighty years."],"id":"story-ehud-the-left-handed-judge"},{"e":"j","t":"Deborah and Jael","r":"Judges 4-5","p":"Deborah; Barak; Sisera; Jael","s":"Deborah, a prophet and judge, tells Barak to fight the army of Sisera. Barak will only go if she goes too. God routs the army, and Sisera flees to the tent of Jael, who kills him with a tent peg. Deborah and Barak sing a victory song.","w":"God raises up unexpected leaders, including women.","q":"Where is God asking me to lead or step up?","l":["After Ehud died, the Israelites again did evil, and the Lord sold them into the hands of Jabin, a king of Canaan. The commander of his army was Sisera, who had nine hundred chariots fitted with iron and cruelly oppressed Israel for twenty years. At that time Deborah, a prophet, was leading Israel. She held court under the Palm of Deborah in the hill country of Ephraim, and the Israelites went up to her to have their disputes decided.","She sent for Barak and gave him God’s command: take ten thousand men to Mount Tabor, and God would lure Sisera with his chariots to the Kishon River and give him into Barak’s hands. Barak said, “If you go with me, I will go; but if you don’t go with me, I won’t go.” Deborah agreed, but warned that because of the way he was going about this, the honour would not be his, for the Lord would deliver Sisera into the hands of a woman.","When Sisera gathered his chariots, Deborah said, “Go! This is the day the Lord has given Sisera into your hands. Has not the Lord gone ahead of you?” Barak charged down Mount Tabor, and the Lord routed Sisera and all his chariots. The song in chapter 5 suggests the Kishon River flooded and swept them away. Sisera abandoned his chariot and fled on foot.","He ran to the tent of Jael, wife of Heber the Kenite, whose clan was at peace with Jabin. Jael went out to meet him: “Come right in. Don’t be afraid.” She covered him with a blanket and gave him milk when he asked for water. He told her to stand at the door and say no one was there. Exhausted, he fell fast asleep. Jael took a tent peg and a hammer, went quietly to him and drove the peg through his temple into the ground. When Barak came by in pursuit, Jael showed him Sisera lying dead. That day God subdued Jabin, and Deborah and Barak sang a song of victory. The land had peace for forty years."],"id":"story-deborah-and-jael"},{"e":"j","t":"Gideon's 300","r":"Judges 6-7","p":"Gideon","s":"Gideon is hiding as he threshes wheat in a winepress when the angel calls him a mighty warrior. He tests God with a fleece. God cuts his army from 32,000 to 300, who rout the Midianites with trumpets, jars and torches.","w":"God uses the weak so that the glory is clearly His.","q":"Where do I feel too small for what God is calling me to?","l":["Again Israel did evil, and for seven years the Midianites swarmed over the land like locusts at harvest time, destroying crops and livestock. The Israelites hid in mountain caves. The angel of the Lord came and sat under an oak at Ophrah, where Gideon was threshing wheat in a winepress to hide it from the Midianites. “The Lord is with you, mighty warrior.” Gideon replied, “If the Lord is with us, why has all this happened to us?”","God said, “Go in the strength you have and save Israel. Am I not sending you?” Gideon protested that his clan was the weakest and he was the least in his family. “I will be with you,” God said. Gideon’s first task was to tear down his father’s altar to Baal by night. Then, before battle, he asked for a sign: if dew was only on a wool fleece and the ground dry, he would know God would save Israel. It happened. Then he asked for the reverse: the fleece dry and the ground wet. God did that too.","Thirty-two thousand men gathered, but God said, “You have too many men. Israel would boast that her own strength saved her.” Anyone afraid could go home, and twenty-two thousand left. Still too many. God had the remaining ten thousand drink at the water. Three hundred lapped with their hands to their mouths; the rest knelt down to drink. “With the three hundred men that lapped I will save you.”","That night God sent Gideon to sneak into the Midianite camp, where he overheard a soldier telling a dream of a loaf of barley bread tumbling into the camp and flattening a tent. His friend said it could only be the sword of Gideon. Gideon worshipped. He gave each of his men a trumpet and an empty jar with a torch inside. At the edge of the camp they blew the trumpets, smashed the jars, held up the torches and shouted, “A sword for the Lord and for Gideon!” The Midianites panicked and turned on each other with their swords, and fled. Israel was free."],"id":"story-gideon-s-300"},{"e":"j","t":"Samson","r":"Judges 13-16","p":"Samson; Delilah","s":"Samson is set apart to God from birth and given great strength. He keeps giving in to his own desires and finally tells Delilah his strength is in his uncut hair. The Philistines capture and blind him. In his last prayer God strengthens him to bring down their temple.","w":"Gifts without character end in tragedy, yet God still hears a broken man's prayer.","q":"Which weakness keeps pulling me back?","l":["Because Israel did evil, the Lord gave them into the hands of the Philistines for forty years. The angel of the Lord appeared to a childless woman, the wife of Manoah, and said she would have a son who would be a Nazirite, set apart to God from birth: no wine, nothing unclean, and no razor ever used on his head. He would begin to deliver Israel. The boy was named Samson, and the Spirit of the Lord began to stir him.","Samson had astonishing strength. When a young lion came roaring toward him, the Spirit came on him powerfully and he tore it apart with his bare hands. He killed a thousand Philistines with the jawbone of a donkey. He carried off the doors of the city gate of Gaza, posts and all. But he was also driven by his own desires. He demanded to marry a Philistine woman because she looked right to him, and his life was full of revenge and recklessness.","Then he fell in love with a woman named Delilah. The Philistine rulers each offered her eleven hundred shekels of silver to find out the secret of his strength. Three times Samson lied: fresh bowstrings, new ropes, his hair woven into a loom. Each time she called, “Samson, the Philistines are upon you!” and he broke free. She nagged him day after day until he was sick to death of it, and finally he told her everything: if his head were shaved, his strength would leave him. While he slept on her lap, she had his hair shaved. When she called, he woke up thinking he would shake free as before. He did not know that the Lord had left him.","The Philistines seized him, gouged out his eyes and set him grinding grain in the prison in Gaza. But the hair on his head began to grow again. At a great festival to their god Dagon, the Philistines brought Samson out to entertain them, about three thousand people on the roof alone. He asked the servant leading him to let him feel the pillars. Then he prayed, “Sovereign Lord, remember me. O God, please strengthen me just once more.” He pushed with all his might, and the temple came down on the rulers and all the people. He killed more in his death than while he lived. Hebrews 11 still lists him among the people of faith."],"id":"story-samson"},{"e":"j","t":"Ruth and Naomi","r":"Ruth 1-4","p":"Ruth; Naomi; Boaz","s":"Widowed Naomi returns to Bethlehem, and her Moabite daughter-in-law Ruth refuses to leave her: 'Where you go I will go'. Ruth gleans grain in Boaz's field, and he becomes their kinsman-redeemer. Ruth becomes King David's great-grandmother.","w":"Loyal love and a redeemer turn bitterness into blessing.","q":"Who could I show loyal, faithful love to this week?","l":["In the days when the judges ruled, there was a famine, and a man from Bethlehem named Elimelek moved to Moab with his wife Naomi and their two sons. Elimelek died, and the sons married Moabite women, Orpah and Ruth. About ten years later both sons died too, leaving Naomi with no husband and no sons. When she heard that the Lord had provided food in Judah again, she set out for home with her daughters-in-law.","On the road Naomi urged them to go back to their mothers’ homes. Orpah kissed her goodbye, but Ruth clung to her. “Don’t urge me to leave you. Where you go I will go, and where you stay I will stay. Your people will be my people and your God my God. Where you die I will die, and there I will be buried.” They arrived in Bethlehem as the barley harvest began. The town was stirred, and Naomi said, “Don’t call me Naomi (pleasant). Call me Mara (bitter), because the Almighty has made my life very bitter.”","Ruth went out to glean leftover grain behind the harvesters, as the poor were allowed to, and as it turned out, she found herself in a field belonging to Boaz, a wealthy relative of Elimelek. Boaz noticed her, told her to stay in his field, gave her water and food, and quietly told his men to drop extra stalks for her. “May the Lord repay you for what you have done,” he said, “under whose wings you have come to take refuge.” When Naomi heard whose field it was, hope came back: Boaz was one of their guardian-redeemers.","At the end of the harvest, following Naomi’s plan, Ruth went to the threshing floor at night, lay at Boaz’s feet and asked him to spread the corner of his garment over her, a way of asking him to marry her and redeem the family. Boaz was moved by her loyalty. There was a closer relative, so the next morning Boaz settled it at the town gate. The other man declined, and Boaz bought Elimelek’s land and married Ruth. They had a son, Obed. The women of Bethlehem said to Naomi, “Praise be to the Lord, who this day has not left you without a guardian-redeemer.” Obed was the father of Jesse, the father of King David."],"id":"story-ruth-and-naomi"},{"e":"k","t":"Hannah's prayer","r":"1 Samuel 1:1-2:11","p":"Hannah; Eli; Peninnah; Samuel","s":"Hannah cannot have children and is taunted by her husband's other wife. At the tabernacle she prays so deeply that the priest Eli thinks she is drunk. God gives her a son, Samuel, and she gives him back to serve the Lord.","w":"God hears the prayer of a weeping heart.","q":"What do I need to pour out to God honestly?","l":["Elkanah, from the hill country of Ephraim, had two wives, Hannah and Peninnah. Peninnah had children, but Hannah had none. Every year the family went up to worship at the tabernacle in Shiloh. Elkanah gave Hannah a double portion because he loved her, but year after year Peninnah kept provoking her to irritate her, until Hannah wept and would not eat. Elkanah asked, “Don’t I mean more to you than ten sons?”","Once, after they had eaten, Hannah stood up and went to pray. In deep anguish she wept bitterly and made a vow: “Lord Almighty, if you will only look on your servant’s misery and remember me, and give me a son, then I will give him to the Lord for all the days of his life, and no razor will ever be used on his head.” She was praying in her heart, her lips moving but no sound coming out.","Eli the priest, sitting by the doorpost, watched her mouth and thought she was drunk. “How long are you going to stay drunk? Put away your wine.” Hannah replied, “Not so, my lord. I am a woman who is deeply troubled. I was pouring out my soul to the Lord.” Eli said, “Go in peace, and may the God of Israel grant you what you have asked of him.” She went her way, ate something, and her face was no longer downcast.","The Lord remembered her, and she gave birth to a son and named him Samuel, saying, “Because I asked the Lord for him.” When he was weaned, she brought the little boy to Eli. “I prayed for this child, and the Lord has granted me what I asked. So now I give him to the Lord.” Then Hannah prayed a song of praise: “My heart rejoices in the Lord. There is no one holy like the Lord. He raises the poor from the dust.” Mary’s song in Luke 1 echoes it. Each year Hannah made Samuel a little robe, and God gave her three more sons and two daughters."],"id":"story-hannah-s-prayer"},{"e":"k","t":"God calls Samuel","r":"1 Samuel 3","p":"Samuel; Eli","s":"Young Samuel hears his name called at night and runs to Eli three times. Eli realises it is the Lord and tells him to say 'Speak, Lord, for your servant is listening'.","w":"God speaks to people who are ready to listen.","q":"Am I making space to listen to God?","l":["The boy Samuel ministered before the Lord under Eli. In those days the word of the Lord was rare, and there were not many visions. Eli’s eyes were becoming so weak that he could barely see, and his sons Hophni and Phinehas were wicked men who treated the Lord’s offering with contempt. Eli rebuked them only weakly.","One night Eli was lying down in his usual place, and Samuel was lying down in the house of the Lord, where the ark of God was. The lamp of God had not yet gone out. Then the Lord called Samuel. Samuel answered, “Here I am,” and ran to Eli. “Here I am; you called me.” Eli said, “I did not call; go back and lie down.” It happened a second time. Samuel did not yet know the Lord; the word of the Lord had not yet been revealed to him.","The third time, Eli realised the Lord was calling the boy. “Go and lie down, and if he calls you, say, ‘Speak, Lord, for your servant is listening.’” The Lord came and stood there, calling as at the other times, “Samuel! Samuel!” Samuel said, “Speak, for your servant is listening.”","God gave Samuel a heavy message: He would judge Eli’s family because his sons had blasphemed and Eli had failed to restrain them. In the morning Samuel was afraid to tell Eli, but Eli insisted, and Samuel told him everything. Eli said, “He is the Lord; let him do what is good in his eyes.” As Samuel grew up, the Lord was with him and let none of his words fall to the ground, and all Israel recognised that Samuel was a prophet of the Lord."],"id":"story-god-calls-samuel"},{"e":"k","t":"The ark is captured","r":"1 Samuel 4-6","p":"Eli; Hophni; Phinehas; The Philistines","s":"Losing to the Philistines, Israel carries the ark of the covenant into battle like a lucky charm. They lose again, the ark is captured, and old Eli falls and dies at the news. In the Philistine temple, their idol Dagon keeps falling face down before the ark, and plagues follow it from city to city until they send it back.","w":"God is not a good-luck charm to be used; He is holy.","q":"Am I treating God as a tool for my plans?","l":["Israel went out to fight the Philistines and lost about four thousand men. The elders asked why the Lord had let them be defeated, and came up with an idea: bring the ark of the covenant from Shiloh into battle, so that it would save them. Eli’s sons Hophni and Phinehas came with it. When the ark entered the camp, the Israelites raised such a shout that the ground shook, and the Philistines were afraid. But they fought harder, and Israel was routed. Thirty thousand died, Hophni and Phinehas were killed, and the ark of God was captured.","A Benjamite ran from the battle line to Shiloh with his clothes torn and dust on his head. Eli, now ninety-eight and blind, was sitting on his chair by the road, his heart fearing for the ark of God. When the messenger told him his sons were dead and the ark had been captured, Eli fell backward off his chair by the gate, broke his neck and died, for he was old and heavy. His daughter-in-law went into labour at the news and named her son Ichabod, saying, “The Glory has departed from Israel.”","The Philistines carried the ark to Ashdod and put it in the temple of their god Dagon, beside his statue, as a trophy. The next morning Dagon had fallen on his face before the ark of the Lord. They set him back in place. The following morning he had fallen again, and his head and hands had broken off and were lying on the threshold. Then the Lord’s hand was heavy on the people of Ashdod with a plague of tumours.","They moved the ark to Gath, and the plague followed. They sent it to Ekron, and the people there cried out, “They have brought the ark to us to kill us!” After seven months the Philistines put the ark on a new cart pulled by two cows that had never been yoked and had calves at home, and sent guilt offerings with it. If the cows went straight to Israel, against their nature, it was the Lord’s doing. The cows went straight up the road to Beth Shemesh, lowing all the way, not turning to the right or left. The people harvesting wheat in the valley looked up, saw the ark and rejoiced."],"id":"story-the-ark-is-captured"},{"e":"k","t":"Israel asks for a king","r":"1 Samuel 8-10","p":"Samuel; Saul","s":"Israel demands a king to be like the other nations, and Samuel warns them what a king will take. God gives them Saul, a tall young man out looking for his father's lost donkeys, and Samuel anoints him.","w":"Wanting to be like everyone else can mean rejecting God's way.","q":"Where do I want to be like everyone else instead of God's?","l":["When Samuel grew old, he appointed his sons as judges, but they did not follow his ways. They took bribes and perverted justice. So all the elders of Israel came to Samuel at Ramah and said, “You are old, and your sons do not follow your ways; now appoint a king to lead us, such as all the other nations have.”","This displeased Samuel, so he prayed. The Lord told him, “Listen to all that the people are saying to you; it is not you they have rejected, but they have rejected me as their king.” God told Samuel to warn them solemnly. Samuel described what a king would take: their sons for his army and chariots, their daughters as cooks and perfumers, the best of their fields, a tenth of their grain and flocks. “You yourselves will become his slaves.” But the people refused to listen. “We want a king over us. Then we will be like all the other nations.”","There was a Benjamite named Kish, who had a son named Saul, as handsome a young man as could be found in Israel, a head taller than anyone else. When some of his father’s donkeys were lost, Saul and a servant searched for days without finding them. The servant suggested asking the man of God in the nearby town. The day before, the Lord had told Samuel that a man from Benjamin would come, and Samuel was to anoint him ruler.","When Samuel saw Saul, the Lord said, “This is the man.” Samuel told him the donkeys had been found and gave him the place of honour at a meal. Early the next morning, outside the town, Samuel took a flask of olive oil, poured it on Saul’s head and kissed him: “Has not the Lord anointed you ruler over his inheritance?” Later Samuel gathered all Israel at Mizpah to choose the king by lot, and Saul was chosen, but he could not be found. The Lord said, “He has hidden himself among the supplies.” They brought him out, and the people shouted, “Long live the king!”"],"id":"story-israel-asks-for-a-king"},{"e":"k","t":"Saul's disobedience","r":"1 Samuel 15","p":"Saul; Samuel; Agag","s":"God tells Saul to destroy the Amalekites completely, but he spares King Agag and the best animals, claiming they are for sacrifice. Samuel tells him 'To obey is better than sacrifice', and God rejects Saul as king.","w":"Partial obedience is disobedience.","q":"Where am I half-obeying God?","l":["Saul began well, rescuing the town of Jabesh Gilead and uniting the tribes. But early on he grew impatient waiting for Samuel and offered a sacrifice himself, and Samuel warned him that his kingdom would not endure. Then Samuel brought a clear command from the Lord: attack the Amalekites, who had attacked Israel in the desert, and totally destroy everything that belonged to them.","Saul attacked and defeated the Amalekites. But he took Agag their king alive, and he and the army spared the best of the sheep and cattle, the fat calves and lambs, everything that was good. These they were unwilling to destroy completely. The Lord said to Samuel, “I regret that I have made Saul king, because he has turned away from me.” Samuel was angry, and he cried out to the Lord all that night.","Early in the morning Samuel went to meet Saul and found that he had set up a monument in his own honour. Saul greeted him cheerfully: “I have carried out the Lord’s instructions.” Samuel said, “What then is this bleating of sheep in my ears? What is this lowing of cattle that I hear?” Saul blamed the soldiers and claimed they had kept the best animals to sacrifice to the Lord.","Samuel asked whether the Lord delights in offerings as much as in obedience. “To obey is better than sacrifice.” Rebellion, he said, is as serious as divination, and arrogance as idolatry. Because Saul had rejected the word of the Lord, the Lord had rejected him as king. As Samuel turned to leave, Saul caught hold of the hem of his robe, and it tore. Samuel said, “The Lord has torn the kingdom of Israel from you today and has given it to one of your neighbours, to one better than you.” Samuel never went to see Saul again, though he mourned for him."],"id":"story-saul-s-disobedience"},{"e":"k","t":"David anointed","r":"1 Samuel 16:1-13","p":"Samuel; Jesse; David","s":"God sends Samuel to Jesse's sons to anoint the next king. One impressive son after another is rejected, because God looks at the heart. The youngest, David, is called in from the sheep and anointed.","w":"God sees potential where people see only the youngest.","q":"Do I judge myself or others by outward appearance?","l":["The Lord said to Samuel, “How long will you mourn for Saul, since I have rejected him as king? Fill your horn with oil and be on your way; I am sending you to Jesse of Bethlehem. I have chosen one of his sons to be king.” Samuel was afraid that Saul would kill him, so God told him to take a heifer and say he had come to sacrifice.","The elders of Bethlehem trembled when Samuel arrived, but he said he came in peace and invited Jesse and his sons to the sacrifice. When they arrived, Samuel saw Eliab, the oldest, tall and impressive, and thought, “Surely the Lord’s anointed stands here.” But the Lord said, “Do not consider his appearance or his height, for I have rejected him. The Lord does not look at the things people look at. People look at the outward appearance, but the Lord looks at the heart.”","Jesse called Abinadab, then Shammah. Seven of his sons passed before Samuel, and each time Samuel said, “The Lord has not chosen this one either.” Finally he asked, “Are these all the sons you have?” “There is still the youngest,” Jesse answered. “He is tending the sheep.” He had not even been invited. Samuel said, “Send for him; we will not sit down until he arrives.”","David was brought in. He was glowing with health and had a fine appearance and handsome features. The Lord said, “Rise and anoint him; this is the one.” Samuel took the horn of oil and anointed him in the presence of his brothers, and from that day on the Spirit of the Lord came powerfully upon David. It would be many years, and many hard seasons, before he actually became king. Meanwhile he went back to his sheep, and soon was called to play the lyre for the troubled King Saul."],"id":"story-david-anointed"},{"e":"k","t":"David and Goliath","r":"1 Samuel 17","p":"David; Goliath; Saul","s":"The giant Goliath mocks Israel's army for forty days. Young David is offended for God's name and goes out with a sling and stones, saying the battle is the Lord's. One stone brings the giant down.","w":"Courage comes from knowing who God is.","q":"What giant am I facing, and whose battle is it?","l":["The Philistines and Israel were camped on opposite hills with the Valley of Elah between them. A champion named Goliath, from Gath, came out from the Philistine camp. He was about three metres tall, wore a bronze helmet and a coat of scale armour weighing about 58 kilograms, and his spear’s iron point alone weighed about 7 kilograms. Every morning and evening for forty days he shouted his challenge: “Choose a man and have him come down to me. If he is able to fight and kill me, we will become your subjects.” Saul and all Israel were dismayed and terrified.","Jesse sent his youngest son David with grain, bread and cheese for his three oldest brothers in the army. David arrived just as Goliath came out shouting his defiance. He heard the men talking about the reward Saul would give, and he said, “Who is this uncircumcised Philistine that he should defy the armies of the living God?” His brother Eliab burned with anger at him: “I know how conceited you are; you came down only to watch the battle.”","David’s words reached Saul. “Let no one lose heart on account of this Philistine; your servant will go and fight him.” Saul said he was only a young man. David told him how, keeping his father’s sheep, he had struck down a lion and a bear that took a lamb. “The Lord who rescued me from the paw of the lion and the paw of the bear will rescue me from the hand of this Philistine.” Saul dressed David in his own armour, but David could not walk in it and took it off. He took his staff, chose five smooth stones from the stream, put them in his shepherd’s bag, and with his sling in hand approached the Philistine.","Goliath despised him and cursed him by his gods. David answered that Goliath came with sword, spear and javelin, but David came in the name of the Lord Almighty, the God of Israel’s armies, whom Goliath had defied. Everyone there would learn that the Lord does not save by sword or spear, “for the battle is the Lord’s.” As Goliath moved closer, David ran quickly toward the battle line, took out a stone, slung it, and struck him on the forehead. The stone sank in, and Goliath fell face down. David ran, took the giant’s own sword and cut off his head. The Philistines fled, and Israel chased them all the way to Gath."],"id":"story-david-and-goliath"},{"e":"k","t":"David and Jonathan","r":"1 Samuel 18-20","p":"David; Jonathan; Saul","s":"King Saul's son Jonathan loves David as himself and gives him his robe and sword. When Saul tries to kill David, Jonathan warns him with a signal of arrows shot in a field. They part in tears, promising loyalty.","w":"True friendship is loyal even when it costs.","q":"Who is a Jonathan in my life, and am I one to someone?","l":["After David killed Goliath and spoke with Saul, Jonathan, Saul’s son, became one in spirit with David, and he loved him as himself. Jonathan made a covenant with David, took off his own robe and gave it to him, along with his tunic, sword, bow and belt. As the king’s heir, he was handing over the symbols of his position.","David succeeded in everything Saul sent him to do. But when the women sang, “Saul has slain his thousands, and David his tens of thousands,” Saul was very angry, and from that time on kept a close eye on David. Twice, while David played the lyre, Saul hurled his spear at him. Saul even told Jonathan and his servants to kill David. Jonathan warned David and talked his father out of it for a while, but Saul’s jealousy kept returning.","David came to Jonathan and asked, “What have I done? Why is your father trying to kill me?” Jonathan found it hard to believe, so they made a plan. David would miss the king’s New Moon feast. If Saul was angry about it, David would know he was in danger. Three days later Jonathan would come to the field where David was hiding and shoot three arrows. If he told the boy collecting them, “The arrows are on this side of you,” David was safe. If he said, “The arrows are beyond you,” David must flee.","At the feast Saul noticed David’s empty seat and exploded at Jonathan, insulting him and saying that as long as David lived, Jonathan’s kingdom would never be established. He even threw a spear at his own son. The next morning Jonathan went out to the field and shot an arrow beyond the boy. “Isn’t the arrow beyond you? Hurry! Go quickly!” After the boy left, David came out from hiding and bowed down three times. Then they kissed each other and wept together, but David wept most. Jonathan said, “Go in peace, for we have sworn friendship with each other in the name of the Lord.”"],"id":"story-david-and-jonathan"},{"e":"k","t":"David spares Saul","r":"1 Samuel 24","p":"David; Saul","s":"Saul hunts David with 3,000 men and goes into the very cave where David is hiding. David's men urge him to kill Saul, but he only cuts off a corner of his robe, refusing to harm the Lord's anointed.","w":"David leaves vengeance to God.","q":"Is there someone I want to get back at?","l":["David became a fugitive, gathering around him about six hundred men who were in distress, in debt or discontented. Saul hunted him from place to place. After a break to fight the Philistines, Saul took three thousand chosen men and went to look for David near the Crags of the Wild Goats at En Gedi, by the Dead Sea.","Saul came to the sheep pens along the way, where there was a cave, and went in to relieve himself. David and his men were far back in that very cave. His men whispered, “This is the day the Lord spoke of when he said to you, ‘I will give your enemy into your hands for you to deal with as you wish.’” David crept up unnoticed and cut off a corner of Saul’s robe.","Afterwards David was conscience-stricken for even having done that. He said to his men, “The Lord forbid that I should do such a thing to my master, the Lord’s anointed, or lay my hand on him.” He rebuked his men and did not allow them to attack Saul. Saul left the cave and went his way.","Then David went out of the cave and called after him, “My lord the king!” When Saul looked behind him, David bowed down with his face to the ground. He held up the piece of robe. “See, there is no wrongdoing or rebellion in my hand. May the Lord judge between you and me. May the Lord avenge the wrongs you have done to me, but my hand will not touch you.” Saul wept aloud. “You are more righteous than I. You have treated me well, but I have treated you badly. I know that you will surely be king.” He asked David to swear not to wipe out his descendants, and David swore it."],"id":"story-david-spares-saul"},{"e":"k","t":"Abigail","r":"1 Samuel 25","p":"Abigail; Nabal; David","s":"Rich Nabal insults David and his men, and David sets out to kill every man in his household. Nabal's wife Abigail rushes out with food and wisely talks David out of bloodshed. Later, after Nabal dies, David marries her.","w":"A wise, humble word can stop a disaster.","q":"Where could a calm, wise word bring peace?","l":["In Maon lived a very wealthy man named Nabal, with a thousand goats and three thousand sheep. His wife Abigail was intelligent and beautiful, but Nabal was surly and mean. While David and his men were in the desert, they had protected Nabal’s shepherds and flocks. At sheep-shearing time, a time of feasting, David sent ten young men to greet Nabal kindly and ask for whatever he could spare.","Nabal answered with insults. “Who is this David? Many servants are breaking away from their masters these days. Why should I take my bread and water, and the meat I have slaughtered for my shearers, and give it to men coming from who knows where?” When David heard, he told four hundred of his men to put on their swords, and swore that by morning he would not leave one male alive in Nabal’s household.","One of the servants ran to Abigail, telling her how good David’s men had been to them, and how her husband had hurled insults at them. “He is such a wicked man that no one can talk to him.” Abigail acted quickly. Without telling her husband, she loaded donkeys with two hundred loaves of bread, two skins of wine, five dressed sheep, roasted grain, a hundred cakes of raisins and two hundred cakes of pressed figs, and rode out to meet David.","When she saw David, she got off her donkey, bowed down before him and took the blame on herself. She reminded him that the Lord would make him a lasting dynasty, and that when he became ruler he would not want the burden of needless bloodshed on his conscience. David said, “Praise be to the Lord, who has sent you today to meet me. May you be blessed for your good judgement and for keeping me from bloodshed.” When Abigail told Nabal the next morning, his heart failed him and he became like a stone. About ten days later the Lord struck him and he died. David sent word asking Abigail to become his wife, and she agreed."],"id":"story-abigail"},{"e":"k","t":"David brings the ark to Jerusalem","r":"2 Samuel 6","p":"David; Uzzah; Michal","s":"David's first attempt to move the ark on a cart ends when Uzzah touches it and dies. Three months later David brings it the right way, carried by Levites, dancing before the Lord with all his might. His wife Michal despises him, but David says he will become even more undignified.","w":"Worship with reverence and with abandon.","q":"Is my worship both reverent and wholehearted?","l":["After David became king over all Israel and captured Jerusalem, he wanted to bring the ark of God, the symbol of God’s throne among His people, to his new capital. He gathered thirty thousand chosen men, set the ark on a new cart, and Uzzah and Ahio guided it while David and all Israel celebrated with all their might before the Lord, with songs, harps, lyres, timbrels and cymbals.","When they came to a threshing floor, the oxen stumbled, and Uzzah reached out and took hold of the ark. The Lord’s anger burned against Uzzah because of his irreverent act, and he died there beside the ark. The law had said the ark must be carried on poles by Levites and never touched. David was angry, and also afraid of the Lord. “How can the ark of the Lord ever come to me?” He left it at the house of Obed-Edom, and for three months the Lord blessed Obed-Edom and his entire household.","When David heard about the blessing, he went to bring up the ark with rejoicing, this time the right way, carried by the Levites. When those carrying it had taken six steps, he sacrificed a bull and a fattened calf. Wearing a simple linen ephod, David was dancing before the Lord with all his might, while he and all Israel brought up the ark with shouts and the sound of trumpets.","As the ark entered the city, Michal, Saul’s daughter, watched from a window. When she saw King David leaping and dancing before the Lord, she despised him in her heart. After the ark was set in its tent, David blessed the people and gave everyone bread, dates and raisins. When he went home to bless his household, Michal came out sneering about how the king had uncovered himself before the slave girls. David replied, “It was before the Lord, who chose me rather than your father. I will celebrate before the Lord. I will become even more undignified than this, and I will be humiliated in my own eyes.”"],"id":"story-david-brings-the-ark-to-jerusalem"},{"e":"k","t":"God's promise to David","r":"2 Samuel 7","p":"David; Nathan","s":"Settled in his palace, David wants to build God a house. Through Nathan, God answers that He will build David a house: a dynasty, with a descendant whose throne will last forever. David sits before the Lord, amazed: 'Who am I, Sovereign Lord, that you have brought me this far?'","w":"God's promise to David is fulfilled in Jesus, the Son of David.","q":"Where has God brought me 'this far'?","l":["After the king was settled in his palace and the Lord had given him rest from all his enemies, David said to the prophet Nathan, “Here I am, living in a house of cedar, while the ark of God remains in a tent.” Nathan replied, “Whatever you have in mind, go ahead and do it, for the Lord is with you.”","That night the word of the Lord came to Nathan with a different message. God reminded David that He had never asked for a house of cedar. “I took you from the pasture, from tending the flock, and appointed you ruler over my people Israel. I have been with you wherever you have gone. Now I will make your name great.” Then came the surprise: “The Lord declares to you that the Lord himself will establish a house for you.”","God promised that after David died, He would raise up one of his offspring to succeed him, and that son would build a house for God’s name. “I will establish the throne of his kingdom forever. I will be his father, and he will be my son. Your house and your kingdom will endure forever before me; your throne will be established forever.” Solomon built the temple, but the forever throne points beyond him to Jesus, whom the angel told Mary would be given the throne of his father David and would reign forever.","King David went in and sat before the Lord, and said, “Who am I, Sovereign Lord, and what is my family, that you have brought me this far?” He marvelled that God had even spoken about the future of his family, and declared that there is no one like Him. David asked God simply to do as He had promised, so that His name would be great forever."],"id":"story-god-s-promise-to-david"},{"e":"k","t":"Mephibosheth at the king's table","r":"2 Samuel 9","p":"David; Mephibosheth; Ziba","s":"As king, David asks if anyone is left from Saul's family that he can show kindness to for Jonathan's sake. He finds Jonathan's son Mephibosheth, lame in both feet, gives him back Saul's land and has him eat at the king's table always.","w":"A picture of grace: brought to the King's table with nothing to offer.","q":"How does it feel that God seats me at His table?","l":["Years earlier, when news came that Saul and Jonathan had died in battle on Mount Gilboa, Jonathan’s five-year-old son was being carried by his nurse. She picked him up and fled, but in her hurry he fell and became disabled in both feet. His name was Mephibosheth. In those days a new king often wiped out the previous king’s family, so he grew up in hiding at Lo Debar, a name that may mean ‘no pasture’.","Once David was firmly established as king, he asked, “Is there anyone still left of the house of Saul to whom I can show kindness for Jonathan’s sake?” A servant of Saul’s household named Ziba was brought in. He told David that a son of Jonathan was still alive, lame in both feet, living in the house of Makir son of Ammiel in Lo Debar.","David had him brought to the palace. Mephibosheth came, bowed down to pay him honour, and must have been terrified. David said, “Don’t be afraid, for I will surely show you kindness for the sake of your father Jonathan. I will restore to you all the land that belonged to your grandfather Saul, and you will always eat at my table.” Mephibosheth bowed down and said, “What is your servant, that you should notice a dead dog like me?”","David gave all of Saul’s land to Mephibosheth and appointed Ziba and his fifteen sons and twenty servants to farm it for him. So Mephibosheth lived in Jerusalem, because he always ate at the king’s table like one of the king’s sons, and he was lame in both feet. The story ends with his feet hidden under the king’s table: grace does not deny our weakness, but it seats us at the table anyway."],"id":"story-mephibosheth-at-the-king-s-table"},{"e":"k","t":"David and Bathsheba","r":"2 Samuel 11-12","p":"David; Bathsheba; Uriah; Nathan","s":"David sleeps with Bathsheba and arranges for her husband Uriah to die in battle. The prophet Nathan tells him a story of a rich man stealing a poor man's only lamb. When David is angry, Nathan says 'You are the man!' David repents (see Psalm 51).","w":"Even a man after God's heart can fall, and real repentance is possible.","q":"Is there something I need to stop excusing?","l":["In the spring, at the time when kings go off to war, David sent Joab and the whole army out, but David remained in Jerusalem. One evening he got up from his bed and walked around on the roof of the palace. From there he saw a very beautiful woman bathing. He sent someone to find out about her: she was Bathsheba, wife of Uriah the Hittite, one of his own mighty men. David sent messengers to get her, and he slept with her. Later she sent word: “I am pregnant.”","David tried to cover it up. He called Uriah home from the battle and told him to go home to his wife. But Uriah slept at the palace entrance with the servants. “The ark and Israel and Judah are staying in tents, and my commander Joab and my lord’s men are camped in the open country. How could I go to my house to eat and drink and make love to my wife?” Even when David got him drunk, Uriah would not go home. So David wrote a letter to Joab, and sent it with Uriah himself: put Uriah where the fighting is fiercest, then withdraw so he is struck down. Uriah died. After Bathsheba’s time of mourning, David brought her to his house and she became his wife. But the thing David had done displeased the Lord.","The Lord sent Nathan to David. Nathan told him about a rich man with very many sheep and cattle, and a poor man who had nothing except one little ewe lamb, which grew up with his children, shared his food and slept in his arms like a daughter. When a traveller came to the rich man, he took the poor man’s lamb and prepared it for his guest. David burned with anger: “As surely as the Lord lives, the man who did this must die!”","Nathan said to David, “You are the man!” He spelled out God’s word: God had given David everything, and he had struck down Uriah and taken his wife. The sword would never depart from his house. David said, “I have sinned against the Lord.” Nathan replied, “The Lord has taken away your sin. You are not going to die.” But the child would die. David fasted and pleaded for the child, and when he died, David got up, washed, worshipped and ate. Psalm 51 is David’s prayer from this time: “Create in me a pure heart, O God, and renew a steadfast spirit within me.” Later Bathsheba gave birth to Solomon, and the Lord loved him."],"id":"story-david-and-bathsheba"},{"e":"k","t":"Absalom's rebellion","r":"2 Samuel 15-18","p":"Absalom; David; Joab","s":"David's son Absalom wins the people's hearts and leads a revolt, and David flees Jerusalem. In the battle, Absalom's hair catches in a tree and Joab kills him. David weeps: 'O my son Absalom!'","w":"Family wounds left unhealed can become rebellion; a father's heart still grieves.","q":"Is there a relationship I need to work on before it breaks?","l":["Trouble in David’s family grew after Bathsheba. His son Amnon assaulted his half-sister Tamar, and David did nothing. Two years later Tamar’s full brother Absalom had Amnon killed and fled. After three years away, Absalom was allowed back to Jerusalem, but David refused to see him for two more years. When they finally met, David kissed him, but the damage remained.","Absalom was the most handsome man in Israel, admired from head to foot, with thick hair he cut once a year. He got himself a chariot and horses with fifty men to run ahead of him. Each morning he stood by the city gate, and whenever someone came with a complaint for the king, he said, “Look, your claims are valid, but there is no representative of the king to hear you. If only I were appointed judge in the land!” When people bowed to him, he took them by the hand and kissed them. So he stole the hearts of the people of Israel.","After four years Absalom went to Hebron and had himself proclaimed king, and the conspiracy gained strength. A messenger told David, “The hearts of the people of Israel are with Absalom.” David said, “Come! We must flee,” and left Jerusalem barefoot, weeping as he climbed the Mount of Olives with his head covered. Even so, he sent the ark back to the city, saying that if he found favour in the Lord’s eyes, God would bring him back. Psalm 3 is his prayer from this time.","In the battle in the forest of Ephraim, David asked his commanders, “Be gentle with the young man Absalom for my sake.” Absalom, riding his mule, went under the thick branches of a large oak, and his head got caught in the tree. He was left hanging in mid-air while the mule kept going. Joab took three javelins and plunged them into Absalom’s heart. When a messenger brought the news, the king was shaken. He went up to the room over the gateway and wept: “O my son Absalom! My son, my son Absalom! If only I had died instead of you, O Absalom, my son, my son!”"],"id":"story-absalom-s-rebellion"},{"e":"k","t":"Solomon asks for wisdom","r":"1 Kings 3","p":"Solomon","s":"God tells young King Solomon to ask for anything. Solomon asks for a discerning heart to govern well. God is pleased and gives wealth and honour too. Soon two women claim the same baby, and Solomon's wisdom reveals the real mother.","w":"Asking for wisdom pleases God.","q":"What situation do I need God's wisdom for?","l":["Before David died, he made sure Solomon was crowned king. Solomon showed his love for the Lord by walking according to the instructions of his father David. He went to Gibeon to offer sacrifices, offering a thousand burnt offerings on the altar there.","At Gibeon the Lord appeared to Solomon during the night in a dream, and God said, “Ask for whatever you want me to give you.” Solomon thanked God for His kindness to his father David. He admitted he felt like a little child who did not know how to carry out his duties. So he asked for a discerning heart, to govern God’s people and to tell right from wrong.","The Lord was pleased that Solomon had asked for this. Because he had asked for this, and not for long life, riches or the death of his enemies, God would give him a wise and discerning heart. He would also give him what he had not asked for: wealth and honour. If he walked in obedience, God would also give him a long life.","Soon two prostitutes came before the king. Both had given birth in the same house, three days apart. One baby had died in the night, and each woman claimed the living child was hers. Solomon said, “Bring me a sword. Cut the living child in two and give half to one and half to the other.” The real mother was deeply moved and cried, “Please, my lord, give her the living baby! Don’t kill him!” The other said, “Neither I nor you shall have him. Cut him in two!” Solomon said, “Give the living baby to the first woman. She is his mother.” When all Israel heard the verdict, they held the king in awe, because they saw that he had wisdom from God."],"id":"story-solomon-asks-for-wisdom"},{"e":"k","t":"Solomon dedicates the temple","r":"1 Kings 8","p":"Solomon","s":"After seven years of building, the ark is brought into the temple, and the cloud of God's glory fills it so the priests cannot stand to serve. Solomon prays that even though the heavens cannot contain God, He would hear every prayer made toward this place, including those of foreigners.","w":"God chooses to dwell with His people; now we are His temple.","q":"How do I make room for God's presence in my life?","l":["In the 480th year after Israel came out of Egypt, Solomon began to build the temple of the Lord in Jerusalem, on Mount Moriah. It took seven years. The stones were dressed at the quarry, so that no hammer, chisel or iron tool was heard at the building site. The inside was panelled with cedar and overlaid with gold, and two great cherubim of olive wood stood in the Most Holy Place.","When it was finished, Solomon gathered the elders of Israel, and the priests carried the ark of the covenant into the inner sanctuary, beneath the wings of the cherubim. Nothing was in the ark except the two stone tablets Moses had placed there at Horeb. When the priests withdrew, a cloud filled the temple of the Lord, and the priests could not perform their service because of the cloud, for the glory of the Lord filled His temple.","Solomon stood before the altar in front of the whole assembly, spread out his hands toward heaven and prayed: “Will God really dwell on earth? The heavens, even the highest heaven, cannot contain you. How much less this temple I have built!” He asked that God’s eyes would be open toward this place night and day. When people sinned and repented, when there was drought, famine or plague, when they went out to battle or were carried into exile, if they prayed toward this house, would God hear from heaven, His dwelling place, and forgive.","He even prayed for the foreigner who comes from a distant land because of God’s great name: “Hear from heaven and do whatever the foreigner asks of you, so that all the peoples of the earth may know your name and fear you.” Then Solomon blessed the people, and they celebrated for fourteen days. Centuries later, the New Testament says that believers themselves are now God’s temple, and God’s Spirit dwells in us."],"id":"story-solomon-dedicates-the-temple"},{"e":"k","t":"The Queen of Sheba","r":"1 Kings 10:1-13","p":"Solomon; The Queen of Sheba","s":"The Queen of Sheba travels to test Solomon with hard questions. He answers them all, and when she sees his wisdom and his kingdom she is overwhelmed and praises his God.","w":"God's wisdom in His people can point outsiders to Him.","q":"Does my life make anyone curious about God?","l":["Solomon’s fame spread far beyond Israel. The queen of Sheba, a kingdom probably in southern Arabia, heard about his fame and his relationship to the Lord, and came to test Solomon with hard questions.","She arrived at Jerusalem with a very great caravan, camels carrying spices, large quantities of gold and precious stones. She came to Solomon and talked with him about all that she had on her mind. Solomon answered all her questions; nothing was too hard for the king to explain to her.","When she saw all the wisdom of Solomon and the palace he had built, the food on his table, the seating of his officials, the attending servants in their robes, his cupbearers and the burnt offerings he made at the temple, she was overwhelmed. She said, “The report I heard in my own country about your achievements and your wisdom is true. But I did not believe these things until I came and saw with my own eyes. Indeed, not even half was told me.”","“Praise be to the Lord your God, who has delighted in you and placed you on the throne of Israel. Because of the Lord’s eternal love for Israel, he has made you king to maintain justice and righteousness.” She gave the king about four tonnes of gold and an unmatched quantity of spices, and Solomon gave her all she desired. Jesus later said the Queen of the South came from the ends of the earth to listen to Solomon’s wisdom, “and now something greater than Solomon is here.”"],"id":"story-the-queen-of-sheba"},{"e":"d","t":"The kingdom splits","r":"1 Kings 12","p":"Rehoboam; Jeroboam","s":"Solomon's son Rehoboam ignores the old advisers and listens to his young friends, promising even heavier burdens. Ten tribes break away under Jeroboam, who sets up golden calves so they won't go to Jerusalem to worship.","w":"Pride and bad counsel can split God's people.","q":"Whose advice am I listening to?","l":["Solomon reigned forty years, but in his old age his many foreign wives turned his heart after other gods. God told him the kingdom would be torn away from his son, leaving only one tribe for David’s sake. When Solomon died, his son Rehoboam went to Shechem to be made king. The northern tribes sent for Jeroboam, a capable official who had fled to Egypt, and together they came to Rehoboam with a request: “Your father put a heavy yoke on us, but now lighten the harsh labour, and we will serve you.”","Rehoboam asked for three days to decide. First he consulted the elders who had served Solomon. They said, “If today you will be a servant to these people and serve them, they will always be your servants.” But he rejected their advice and consulted the young men who had grown up with him. They told him to say, “My little finger is thicker than my father’s waist. My father scourged you with whips; I will scourge you with scorpions.”","On the third day Rehoboam answered the people harshly, following the young men’s advice. The writer says this turn of events was from the Lord, to fulfil His word. When Israel saw the king would not listen, they answered, “What share do we have in David? To your tents, Israel!” Ten tribes made Jeroboam their king. Only Judah, with Benjamin, stayed with Rehoboam. When Rehoboam prepared to fight, a prophet told him not to fight his brothers, and he obeyed.","Jeroboam now worried that if his people kept going to the temple in Jerusalem to worship, their hearts would turn back to Rehoboam. So he made two golden calves and said, “It is too much for you to go up to Jerusalem. Here are your gods, Israel, who brought you up out of Egypt.” He set one in Bethel and the other in Dan, built shrines and appointed his own priests. This became the sin that the northern kingdom never shook off. From here on, the Bible tells two stories: Israel in the north and Judah in the south."],"id":"story-the-kingdom-splits"},{"e":"d","t":"Elijah, the ravens and the widow","r":"1 Kings 17","p":"Elijah; The widow of Zarephath","s":"Elijah announces a drought, and God feeds him by ravens at a brook. Then God sends him to a poor widow who has only enough flour and oil for one last meal. She feeds Elijah first, and her jar and jug never run out. Later Elijah raises her son.","w":"God provides in unexpected ways, often through generosity.","q":"Where is God asking me to give from the little I have?","l":["Ahab king of Israel did more evil than all the kings before him. He married Jezebel, a Sidonian princess, and built a temple to Baal in Samaria. Baal was worshipped as the god of rain and fertility. Then, without introduction, Elijah the Tishbite appeared before Ahab: “As the Lord, the God of Israel, lives, whom I serve, there will be neither dew nor rain in the next few years except at my word.”","God told Elijah to hide in the Kerith Ravine, east of the Jordan. “You will drink from the brook, and I have directed the ravens to supply you with food there.” The ravens brought him bread and meat in the morning and evening, and he drank from the brook. Some time later the brook dried up because there had been no rain.","Then God sent him to Zarephath in Sidon, Jezebel’s home territory, where He had directed a widow to supply him with food. At the town gate Elijah found a widow gathering sticks. He asked for water, and then for a piece of bread. She told him she had no bread, only a handful of flour in a jar and a little oil in a jug. She was gathering sticks to cook a last meal for herself and her son before they died. Elijah said, “Don’t be afraid. First make a small loaf for me, then make something for yourself and your son. The jar of flour will not be used up and the jug of oil will not run dry until the day the Lord sends rain.” She did, and there was food every day.","Later her son became ill and stopped breathing. In her grief she said, “Did you come to remind me of my sin and kill my son?” Elijah carried the boy up to his room, laid him on his bed and cried out to the Lord. He stretched himself out on the boy three times and prayed, “Lord my God, let this boy’s life return to him!” The Lord heard Elijah’s cry, and the boy lived. Elijah brought him down to his mother. “Look, your son is alive!” She said, “Now I know that you are a man of God and that the word of the Lord from your mouth is the truth.” Jesus later mentioned this widow in His first sermon in Nazareth."],"id":"story-elijah-the-ravens-and-the-widow"},{"e":"d","t":"Elijah on Mount Carmel","r":"1 Kings 18","p":"Elijah; Ahab; The prophets of Baal","s":"Elijah challenges 450 prophets of Baal: the god who answers by fire is God. Baal's prophets shout all day with no answer. Elijah soaks his altar with water, prays a simple prayer, and fire falls from heaven.","w":"The Lord is God, and He answers.","q":"Where am I wavering between two opinions?","l":["In the third year of the drought, God sent Elijah back to Ahab. The famine was severe in Samaria. When Ahab saw Elijah, he said, “Is that you, you troubler of Israel?” Elijah answered, “I have not made trouble for Israel. But you and your father’s family have. You have abandoned the Lord’s commands and followed the Baals.” He told Ahab to gather all Israel to Mount Carmel, with the 450 prophets of Baal and the 400 prophets of Asherah who ate at Jezebel’s table.","Elijah said to the people, “How long will you waver between two opinions? If the Lord is God, follow him; but if Baal is God, follow him.” The people said nothing. He proposed a test: two bulls, two altars, no fire lit. “You call on the name of your god, and I will call on the name of the Lord. The god who answers by fire, he is God.” The people agreed.","The prophets of Baal went first. From morning till noon they shouted, “Baal, answer us!” and danced around the altar. There was no response. At noon Elijah began to taunt them: “Shout louder! Surely he is a god! Perhaps he is deep in thought, or busy, or travelling. Maybe he is sleeping and must be awakened.” They shouted louder and cut themselves with swords and spears until their blood flowed. Midday passed, and they continued until evening. But there was no response, no one answered, no one paid attention.","Then Elijah repaired the broken-down altar of the Lord with twelve stones, one for each tribe. He dug a trench around it, arranged the wood and the bull, and had the people pour four large jars of water over it, three times, until the water ran down and even filled the trench. At the time of the evening sacrifice he prayed a short prayer: “Lord, let it be known today that you are God in Israel and that I am your servant. Answer me, so these people will know that you, Lord, are God, and that you are turning their hearts back again.” Then the fire of the Lord fell and burned up the sacrifice, the wood, the stones and the soil, and licked up the water in the trench. The people fell prostrate and cried, “The Lord, he is God!” Then Elijah prayed seven times on the top of Carmel until a cloud as small as a man’s hand rose from the sea, and heavy rain came."],"id":"story-elijah-on-mount-carmel"},{"e":"d","t":"The gentle whisper","r":"1 Kings 19","p":"Elijah; Jezebel","s":"After the victory, Queen Jezebel threatens Elijah, and he runs into the desert, wanting to die. An angel lets him sleep and gives him food. At Mount Horeb God isn't in the wind, earthquake or fire, but in a gentle whisper.","w":"God cares for burned-out servants and speaks quietly.","q":"Do I need rest, food and a quiet place to hear God?","l":["Ahab told Jezebel everything Elijah had done, including how he had killed the prophets of Baal. Jezebel sent a messenger: “May the gods deal with me, be it ever so severely, if by this time tomorrow I do not make your life like that of one of them.” Elijah, the hero of Carmel, was afraid and ran for his life.","He left his servant in Beersheba and went a day’s journey into the wilderness. He sat down under a broom bush and prayed that he might die. “I have had enough, Lord. Take my life; I am no better than my ancestors.” Then he lay down and fell asleep. An angel touched him: “Get up and eat.” There by his head was bread baked over hot coals and a jar of water. He ate, drank and lay down again. The angel came back a second time: “Get up and eat, for the journey is too much for you.” Strengthened by that food, he travelled forty days and forty nights to Horeb, the mountain of God.","He went into a cave and spent the night. The word of the Lord came: “What are you doing here, Elijah?” He replied, “I have been very zealous for the Lord God Almighty. The Israelites have rejected your covenant, torn down your altars and put your prophets to death. I am the only one left, and now they are trying to kill me too.” The Lord said, “Go out and stand on the mountain in the presence of the Lord, for the Lord is about to pass by.”","Then a great and powerful wind tore the mountains apart and shattered the rocks, but the Lord was not in the wind. After the wind there was an earthquake, but the Lord was not in the earthquake. After the earthquake came a fire, but the Lord was not in the fire. And after the fire came a gentle whisper. When Elijah heard it, he pulled his cloak over his face and stood at the mouth of the cave. God gave him new work: anoint new kings, and anoint Elisha to succeed him. And He corrected Elijah’s loneliness: “I reserve seven thousand in Israel, all whose knees have not bowed down to Baal.” Elijah found Elisha ploughing with twelve yoke of oxen, threw his cloak around him, and Elisha left everything to follow him."],"id":"story-the-gentle-whisper"},{"e":"d","t":"Naboth's vineyard","r":"1 Kings 21","p":"Ahab; Jezebel; Naboth; Elijah","s":"King Ahab sulks because Naboth won't sell him his family vineyard. Jezebel arranges false witnesses, and Naboth is stoned. Elijah confronts Ahab with God's judgement.","w":"God sees injustice done by the powerful.","q":"Do I stand up for those who are treated unfairly?","l":["Naboth owned a vineyard in Jezreel, right next to King Ahab’s palace. Ahab said to him, “Let me have your vineyard to use for a vegetable garden. In exchange I will give you a better vineyard or pay you whatever it is worth.” Naboth replied, “The Lord forbid that I should give you the inheritance of my ancestors.” God’s law said family land was to stay in the family.","Ahab went home sullen and angry. He lay on his bed sulking and refused to eat. When Jezebel heard why, she said, “Is this how you act as king over Israel? Get up and eat! Cheer up. I’ll get you the vineyard of Naboth.” She wrote letters in Ahab’s name, sealed them with his seal and sent them to the elders of Naboth’s city.","The letters said: proclaim a day of fasting and seat Naboth in a prominent place. Seat two scoundrels opposite him and have them testify that he cursed both God and the king. Then take him out and stone him to death. The elders did exactly as she wrote. Naboth was stoned. Jezebel told Ahab, “Get up and take possession of the vineyard. Naboth is no longer alive, but dead.” Ahab went down to take it.","Then the word of the Lord came to Elijah: go and meet Ahab in Naboth’s vineyard. Ahab said to Elijah, “So you have found me, my enemy!” Elijah said, “I have found you, because you have sold yourself to do evil in the eyes of the Lord.” He announced judgement on Ahab’s house and on Jezebel. When Ahab heard these words, he tore his clothes, put on sackcloth and fasted. And God, astonishingly, said to Elijah, “Have you noticed how Ahab has humbled himself before me? Because he has humbled himself, I will not bring this disaster in his day.” Even for Ahab, there was mercy when he humbled himself."],"id":"story-naboth-s-vineyard"},{"e":"d","t":"Jehoshaphat's singers","r":"2 Chronicles 20:1-30","p":"Jehoshaphat; Jahaziel","s":"A vast army from Moab, Ammon and Edom marches on Judah. King Jehoshaphat calls a fast and prays, 'We do not know what to do, but our eyes are on you.' God answers through Jahaziel: 'The battle is not yours, but God's.' Jehoshaphat sends singers ahead of the army, and as they praise, the enemy armies turn on each other.","w":"Praise can be the front line.","q":"What could I thank God for before I see the answer?","l":["Jehoshaphat was a good king of Judah who sent officials and priests throughout the land to teach the people God’s law. One day messengers came: “A vast army is coming against you from Edom, from the other side of the Dead Sea. It is already in En Gedi.” The Moabites, Ammonites and some Meunites had joined together to attack.","Alarmed, Jehoshaphat resolved to inquire of the Lord, and proclaimed a fast for all Judah. People came from every town to seek help from the Lord. Standing in the temple courtyard, the king prayed, remembering God’s power and His promises, and ended with these words: “We have no power to face this vast army that is attacking us. We do not know what to do, but our eyes are on you.” All the men of Judah stood there before the Lord, with their wives and children and little ones.","Then the Spirit of the Lord came on Jahaziel, a Levite, as he stood in the assembly. He told the king and all Judah not to be afraid or discouraged because of the vast army. “For the battle is not yours, but God’s.” They were to march out the next day, take up their positions, stand firm and watch the Lord deliver them. They would not have to fight. Jehoshaphat bowed down with his face to the ground, and all the people fell down in worship.","Early in the morning they left for the Desert of Tekoa. Jehoshaphat appointed men to sing to the Lord and to praise Him for the splendour of His holiness as they went out at the head of the army, saying, “Give thanks to the Lord, for his love endures forever.” As they began to sing and praise, the Lord set ambushes against the invaders, and they turned on one another and destroyed each other. When Judah came to the place overlooking the desert, they saw only dead bodies. It took them three days to collect the plunder. On the fourth day they gathered in a valley and praised the Lord, and named it the Valley of Berakah, which means praise or blessing."],"id":"story-jehoshaphat-s-singers"},{"e":"d","t":"Elijah taken up to heaven","r":"2 Kings 2:1-15","p":"Elijah; Elisha","s":"Elisha refuses to leave Elijah's side and asks for a double portion of his spirit. A chariot of fire appears, and Elijah goes up to heaven in a whirlwind. Elisha picks up his cloak, strikes the Jordan, and the water parts.","w":"Hunger for more of God, and the work carries on to the next generation.","q":"Who am I learning from, and who is learning from me?","l":["When the Lord was about to take Elijah up to heaven in a whirlwind, Elijah and Elisha were on their way from Gilgal. Elijah said, “Stay here; the Lord has sent me to Bethel.” Elisha said, “As surely as the Lord lives and as you live, I will not leave you.” It happened again at Bethel and again at Jericho. Each time the company of the prophets told Elisha that his master would be taken today, and each time Elisha said, “Yes, I know, so be quiet,” and refused to leave Elijah’s side.","Fifty of the prophets watched from a distance as the two stopped at the Jordan. Elijah took his cloak, rolled it up and struck the water with it. The water divided to the right and to the left, and the two of them crossed over on dry ground. Then Elijah said, “Tell me, what can I do for you before I am taken from you?” Elisha replied, “Let me inherit a double portion of your spirit,” the share given to a firstborn son and heir.","“You have asked a difficult thing,” Elijah said, “yet if you see me when I am taken from you, it will be yours.” As they were walking along and talking together, suddenly a chariot of fire and horses of fire appeared and separated the two of them, and Elijah went up to heaven in a whirlwind. Elisha saw it and cried out, “My father! My father! The chariots and horsemen of Israel!” He tore his clothes in two.","Elisha picked up the cloak that had fallen from Elijah, went back and stood on the bank of the Jordan. He struck the water with it and asked, “Where now is the Lord, the God of Elijah?” The water divided, and he crossed over. The prophets from Jericho said, “The spirit of Elijah is resting on Elisha,” and bowed to the ground before him. The Bible records roughly twice as many miracles through Elisha as through Elijah."],"id":"story-elijah-taken-up-to-heaven"},{"e":"d","t":"The widow's oil","r":"2 Kings 4:1-7","p":"Elisha; A prophet's widow","s":"A widow's creditor is coming to take her two sons as slaves. Elisha tells her to borrow as many empty jars as she can and pour her little oil into them. The oil keeps flowing until the last jar is full, and she sells it to pay her debt.","w":"God's provision matches the room we make for it.","q":"How many 'empty jars' am I bringing to God?","l":["The wife of a man from the company of the prophets cried out to Elisha, “Your servant my husband is dead, and you know that he revered the Lord. But now his creditor is coming to take my two boys as his slaves.” In those days a debt could be paid by forced labour.","Elisha replied, “How can I help you? Tell me, what do you have in your house?” She said, “Your servant has nothing there at all, except a small jar of olive oil.” Elisha said, “Go around and ask all your neighbours for empty jars. Don’t ask for just a few. Then go inside and shut the door behind you and your sons. Pour oil into all the jars, and as each is filled, put it to one side.”","She left him and shut the door behind her and her sons. They brought the jars to her and she kept pouring. When all the jars were full, she said to her son, “Bring me another one.” But he replied, “There is not a jar left.” Then the oil stopped flowing.","She went and told the man of God, and he said, “Go, sell the oil and pay your debts. You and your sons can live on what is left.” The miracle happened behind a closed door, in an ordinary kitchen, and it was as big as the faith that went around borrowing jars."],"id":"story-the-widow-s-oil"},{"e":"d","t":"The Shunammite's son","r":"2 Kings 4:8-37","p":"Elisha; The Shunammite woman; Gehazi","s":"A woman in Shunem builds a room for Elisha, and God gives her a son. Years later the boy dies in her lap. She goes straight to Elisha, who prays and stretches himself over the boy, and he comes back to life.","w":"Bring your deepest grief straight to God.","q":"What do I need to bring to God rather than carry alone?","l":["Elisha often passed through Shunem, where a well-to-do woman urged him to stay for a meal. She said to her husband, “This man who often comes our way is a holy man of God. Let’s make a small room on the roof and put in it a bed and a table, a chair and a lamp for him.” Wanting to thank her, Elisha asked what he could do for her. His servant Gehazi pointed out that she had no son, and her husband was old. Elisha told her, “About this time next year, you will hold a son in your arms.” “No, my lord,” she objected. “Please, man of God, don’t mislead your servant!” But a year later she had a son.","Years later the boy went out to his father among the reapers and said, “My head! My head!” A servant carried him to his mother, and he sat on her lap until noon, and then he died. She carried him up and laid him on the bed of the man of God, shut the door, and set out at once for Mount Carmel to find Elisha. When her husband asked why, she said only, “That’s all right.” When Gehazi ran to meet her and asked if all was well, she said, “Everything is all right.”","But when she reached Elisha, she took hold of his feet. “Did I ask you for a son, my lord? Didn’t I tell you, ‘Don’t raise my hopes’?” Elisha sent Gehazi ahead with his staff to lay on the boy’s face, but she said, “As surely as the Lord lives and as you live, I will not leave you.” So Elisha went with her. Gehazi came back to meet them: the boy had not awakened.","When Elisha reached the house, the boy was lying dead on his couch. He went in, shut the door and prayed to the Lord. Then he lay on the boy, mouth to mouth, eyes to eyes, hands to hands. As he stretched himself out on him, the boy’s body grew warm. Elisha walked back and forth in the room, then stretched out on him once more. The boy sneezed seven times and opened his eyes. Elisha called the mother: “Take your son.” She came in, fell at his feet and bowed to the ground. Then she took her son and went out."],"id":"story-the-shunammite-s-son"},{"e":"d","t":"Naaman healed","r":"2 Kings 5","p":"Naaman; Elisha; A servant girl; Gehazi","s":"Naaman, an Aramean army commander, has leprosy. A captured Israelite servant girl tells his wife about Elisha. Elisha tells him to wash seven times in the Jordan, and Naaman storms off insulted, until his servants persuade him. His skin becomes like a child's.","w":"Pride nearly costs Naaman his healing; God's ways are humble.","q":"Is pride keeping me from doing a simple thing God asks?","l":["Naaman was commander of the army of the king of Aram. He was a great man, highly regarded, a valiant soldier, but he had leprosy. Aramean raiders had taken captive a young girl from Israel, and she served Naaman’s wife. She said to her mistress, “If only my master would see the prophet who is in Samaria! He would cure him of his leprosy.”","The king of Aram sent Naaman with a letter to the king of Israel, along with silver, gold and ten sets of clothing. The king of Israel read it, tore his robes and said, “Am I God? Can I kill and bring back to life? He is trying to pick a quarrel with me!” When Elisha heard, he sent word: “Have the man come to me and he will know that there is a prophet in Israel.”","Naaman went with his horses and chariots and stopped at the door of Elisha’s house. Elisha didn’t even come out. He sent a messenger: “Go, wash yourself seven times in the Jordan, and your flesh will be restored.” Naaman went away angry. He had expected the prophet to come out, call on his God, wave his hand over the spot and cure him. And weren’t the rivers of Damascus better than any water in Israel? He turned and went off in a rage.","His servants went to him: “My father, if the prophet had told you to do some great thing, would you not have done it? How much more, then, when he tells you, ‘Wash and be cleansed’!” So he went down and dipped himself in the Jordan seven times, and his flesh was restored and became clean like that of a young boy. He went back to Elisha and said, “Now I know that there is no God in all the world except in Israel.” Elisha refused any gift, but his servant Gehazi secretly ran after Naaman and lied to get silver and clothing for himself. Naaman’s leprosy clung to Gehazi from then on. The outsider was cleansed by humble obedience; the insider was struck by greed."],"id":"story-naaman-healed"},{"e":"d","t":"The floating axe head","r":"2 Kings 6:1-7","p":"Elisha","s":"The prophets are cutting trees by the Jordan when a borrowed iron axe head flies off into the water. Elisha throws a stick in, and the axe head floats to the surface.","w":"God cares about small, everyday troubles too.","q":"What small worry can I hand to God today?","l":["The company of prophets who met with Elisha said, “Look, the place where we meet with you is too small for us. Let us go to the Jordan, where each of us can get a pole; and let us build a place there for us to meet.” Elisha said, “Go.” One of them asked him to come along, and he agreed.","At the Jordan they began to cut down trees. As one of them was cutting down a tree, the iron axe head fell into the water. “Oh no, my lord!” he cried out. “It was borrowed!” Iron tools were expensive, and he had no way to repay it.","The man of God asked, “Where did it fall?” When he showed him the place, Elisha cut a stick and threw it there, and made the iron float. “Lift it out,” he said. Then the man reached out his hand and took it.","It is one of the smallest miracles in the Bible. No army was defeated and no one was raised from the dead. A poor student lost a borrowed tool and was worried about paying it back, and God cared enough to bring it back. Nothing that worries you is too small to bring to Him."],"id":"story-the-floating-axe-head"},{"e":"d","t":"Horses and chariots of fire","r":"2 Kings 6:8-23","p":"Elisha; His servant","s":"The king of Aram surrounds the town of Dothan to capture Elisha. His servant panics, but Elisha prays for his eyes to be opened, and he sees the hills full of horses and chariots of fire. The enemy army is struck blind, led away, fed and sent home.","w":"Those with us are more than those against us.","q":"What would I see if God opened my eyes in my situation?","l":["The king of Aram was at war with Israel. Each time he planned where to set up camp and ambush the Israelites, Elisha warned the king of Israel, who avoided those places. This happened over and over. The king of Aram was enraged and demanded to know which of his officers was a traitor. One of them said, “None of us, my lord the king, but Elisha, the prophet who is in Israel, tells the king of Israel the very words you speak in your bedroom.”","The king found out that Elisha was in Dothan and sent horses, chariots and a strong force there. They went by night and surrounded the city. When Elisha’s servant got up early the next morning and went out, an army with horses and chariots had surrounded the city. “Oh no, my lord! What shall we do?”","“Don’t be afraid,” Elisha answered. “Those who are with us are more than those who are with them.” And Elisha prayed, “Open his eyes, Lord, so that he may see.” Then the Lord opened the servant’s eyes, and he looked and saw the hills full of horses and chariots of fire all around Elisha.","As the enemy came down, Elisha prayed that God would strike them with blindness, and He did. Elisha told them, “This is not the road and this is not the city. Follow me, and I will lead you to the man you are looking for.” He led them into Samaria, the capital of Israel. There God opened their eyes, and they found themselves surrounded. The king of Israel asked, “Shall I kill them, my father?” Elisha said no: “Set food and water before them so that they may eat and drink and then go back to their master.” The king prepared a great feast for them and sent them home. And the raiding bands from Aram stopped invading Israel’s territory."],"id":"story-horses-and-chariots-of-fire"},{"e":"d","t":"Four lepers at the gate","r":"2 Kings 6:24-7:20","p":"Elisha; Four men with leprosy","s":"Samaria is starving under siege. Elisha promises food will be cheap by tomorrow, and an officer scoffs. Four men with leprosy decide they might as well go to the enemy camp. They find it deserted, because God made the Arameans hear a great army. After feasting, they say, 'This is a day of good news and we are keeping it to ourselves.'","w":"Good news is meant to be shared.","q":"What good news am I keeping to myself?","l":["Ben-Hadad king of Aram besieged Samaria for so long that there was a terrible famine. A donkey’s head sold for eighty shekels of silver, and things got so desperate that people were doing unthinkable things. The king of Israel blamed Elisha. Elisha said, “Hear the word of the Lord. About this time tomorrow, a seah of the finest flour will sell for a shekel at the gate of Samaria.” The officer on whose arm the king was leaning scoffed: “Even if the Lord should open the floodgates of the heavens, could this happen?” Elisha replied, “You will see it with your own eyes, but you will not eat any of it!”","Four men with leprosy were sitting at the entrance of the city gate, outside the walls. They said to each other, “Why stay here until we die? If we go into the city, we will die; if we stay here, we will die. So let’s go over to the camp of the Arameans. If they spare us, we live; if they kill us, then we die.” At dusk they went to the camp. When they reached the edge of it, no one was there.","The Lord had caused the Arameans to hear the sound of chariots and horses and a great army, and they thought Israel had hired the Hittite and Egyptian kings to attack them. They ran for their lives at dusk, leaving their tents, horses and donkeys, and the camp just as it was. The four men went into one tent and ate and drank, and carried away silver, gold and clothes and hid them. Then they did it again in another tent.","Then they said to each other, “What we’re doing is not right. This is a day of good news and we are keeping it to ourselves. If we wait until daylight, punishment will overtake us. Let’s go at once and report this to the royal palace.” They told the gatekeepers. The king sent scouts, who found the road all the way to the Jordan strewn with clothing and equipment thrown away in their headlong flight. The people plundered the camp, and flour sold for a shekel, just as the Lord had said. The scoffing officer was in charge of the gate, and the people trampled him in the rush, and he died, just as Elisha had said."],"id":"story-four-lepers-at-the-gate"},{"e":"d","t":"Joash the boy king","r":"2 Kings 11-12","p":"Joash; Jehosheba; Jehoiada; Athaliah","s":"Wicked Queen Athaliah murders the royal family to seize the throne. Baby Joash is rescued by his aunt Jehosheba and hidden in the temple for six years. Then the priest Jehoiada crowns the seven-year-old king, and Athaliah is overthrown. Joash repairs the temple while Jehoiada guides him.","w":"God preserves His promise to David through one hidden baby and a brave aunt.","q":"Who is a Jehoiada in my life, helping me walk with God?","l":["When Athaliah, mother of King Ahaziah of Judah, saw that her son was dead, she set out to destroy the whole royal family and seized the throne for herself. She was the daughter of Ahab and Jezebel, and she brought Baal worship into Judah. If she succeeded, the line of David, the line of God’s promise, would end.","But Jehosheba, the daughter of King Jehoram and sister of Ahaziah, took Ahaziah’s baby son Joash and stole him away from among the royal princes who were about to be murdered. She hid him and his nurse in a bedroom. Jehosheba was married to the high priest Jehoiada, and Joash remained hidden with them at the temple of the Lord for six years while Athaliah ruled the land.","In the seventh year Jehoiada brought together the commanders of the royal guard and showed them the king’s son. He made a covenant with them and organised the guards in shifts around the temple and the boy, each man with his weapon in his hand. Then he brought out the seven-year-old, put the crown on him, presented him with a copy of the covenant and proclaimed him king. They anointed him, and the people clapped their hands and shouted, “Long live the king!”","When Athaliah heard the noise, she rushed to the temple and saw the king standing by the pillar, with trumpeters and all the people rejoicing. She tore her robes and called out, “Treason! Treason!” Jehoiada ordered her taken out, and she was put to death outside the temple. Jehoiada made a covenant that the king and people would be the Lord’s, and the people tore down the temple of Baal. Joash did what was right in the eyes of the Lord all the years Jehoiada the priest instructed him. He set up a chest with a hole in its lid beside the altar to collect money, and the temple was repaired. Sadly, after Jehoiada died, Joash turned away and listened to bad advisers."],"id":"story-joash-the-boy-king"},{"e":"d","t":"Jonah","r":"Jonah 1-4","p":"Jonah","s":"God sends Jonah to preach to Nineveh, Israel's enemy, and he runs the other way by ship. In a storm he is thrown overboard and swallowed by a great fish. He prays, is spat onto land and preaches, and Nineveh repents. Jonah sulks because God was merciful.","w":"God's mercy is wider than our prejudices.","q":"Who do I secretly think doesn't deserve God's mercy?","l":["The word of the Lord came to Jonah son of Amittai: “Go to the great city of Nineveh and preach against it, because its wickedness has come up before me.” Nineveh was the capital of Assyria, Israel’s brutal enemy. Jonah ran away from the Lord. He went down to Joppa, found a ship bound for Tarshish, at the opposite end of the known world, paid the fare and went aboard.","The Lord sent a great wind on the sea, and the storm was so violent that the ship threatened to break up. The sailors were afraid; each cried out to his own god and threw cargo overboard. Jonah had gone below deck and fallen into a deep sleep. The captain woke him: “How can you sleep? Get up and call on your god!” The sailors cast lots to find out who was responsible, and the lot fell on Jonah. He confessed that he worshipped the Lord, the God of heaven, who made the sea and the dry land, and that he was running from Him. “Pick me up and throw me into the sea, and it will become calm.” The sailors tried hard to row back to land, but finally they threw him overboard, and the raging sea grew calm. The pagan sailors greatly feared the Lord and made vows to Him.","The Lord provided a huge fish to swallow Jonah, and Jonah was in the belly of the fish three days and three nights. From inside the fish he prayed: “In my distress I called to the Lord, and he answered me. Salvation comes from the Lord.” And the Lord commanded the fish, and it vomited Jonah onto dry land. Then the word of the Lord came to Jonah a second time: go to Nineveh. This time Jonah went. It was a very large city, taking three days to walk through. Jonah began by going a day’s journey into the city, proclaiming, “Forty more days and Nineveh will be overthrown.” The Ninevites believed God. From the greatest to the least, they put on sackcloth and fasted, and even the king took off his royal robes and sat in the dust. When God saw that they turned from their evil ways, He relented and did not bring on them the destruction He had threatened.","But to Jonah this seemed very wrong, and he became angry. “Isn’t this what I said when I was still at home? That is what I tried to forestall by fleeing to Tarshish. I knew that you are a gracious and compassionate God, slow to anger and abounding in love.” He asked God to take his life. Jonah went outside the city to sit and see what would happen. God made a leafy plant grow up to shade him, and Jonah was very happy about it. At dawn God sent a worm to chew it so it withered, and a scorching east wind, and Jonah wanted to die. God pointed out that Jonah cared about a plant he had not planted or grown. Should God not care about the great city of Nineveh, with more than a hundred and twenty thousand people who could not tell their right hand from their left, and many animals as well? The book ends with God’s question hanging in the air."],"id":"story-jonah"},{"e":"d","t":"Hosea and Gomer","r":"Hosea 1-3","p":"Hosea; Gomer","s":"God tells the prophet Hosea to marry Gomer, who will be unfaithful to him, as a living picture of Israel's unfaithfulness to God. She leaves him for other lovers. Then God tells Hosea to go and love her again, and he buys her back.","w":"God's love pursues and redeems the unfaithful.","q":"Where do I need to hear that God still pursues me?","l":["Hosea prophesied in the northern kingdom of Israel in its last decades, a time of prosperity on the surface and idolatry underneath. The people kept worshipping the Lord in name while chasing Baal for good harvests. God gave Hosea one of the hardest assignments any prophet received: “Go, marry a promiscuous woman and have children with her, for like an adulterous wife this land is guilty of unfaithfulness to the Lord.”","So he married Gomer daughter of Diblaim, and she had a son. God told Hosea to name him Jezreel, as a sign of coming judgement on the royal house. Then Gomer had a daughter, and God said to name her Lo-Ruhamah, which means ‘not loved’ or ‘no mercy’. Then another son, Lo-Ammi, meaning ‘not my people’. Each child’s name was a sermon. Yet straight after, God promised a day when the people who were called ‘not my people’ would be called ‘children of the living God’. Paul and Peter both quote that promise about the church.","Gomer left Hosea for other lovers. In chapter 2 God speaks of His people the same way: she chased her lovers, not realising that it was God who gave her the grain, the new wine and the oil. Yet after describing the judgement, God says, “Therefore I am now going to allure her; I will lead her into the wilderness and speak tenderly to her. I will betroth you to me forever in righteousness and justice, in love and compassion.”","Then the Lord said to Hosea, “Go, show your love to your wife again, though she is loved by another man and is an adulteress. Love her as the Lord loves the Israelites, though they turn to other gods.” Gomer had apparently ended up enslaved or in debt, because Hosea bought her back for fifteen shekels of silver and about a homer and a lethek of barley. He told her she would live with him and be faithful to him, and he to her. The prophet’s broken heart became a living picture of God’s heart: a love that pays the price to bring an unfaithful bride home."],"id":"story-hosea-and-gomer"},{"e":"d","t":"Isaiah's vision","r":"Isaiah 6","p":"Isaiah","s":"Isaiah sees the Lord on a high throne, with seraphim calling 'Holy, holy, holy'. He cries out that he is ruined, a man of unclean lips. A seraph touches his lips with a live coal. When God asks who will go, Isaiah says 'Here am I. Send me!'","w":"Seeing God's holiness leads to cleansing, then to calling.","q":"What would 'Here am I, send me' look like for me?","l":["In the year that King Uzziah died, after a long and mostly prosperous reign that ended badly, Isaiah saw the Lord, high and exalted, seated on a throne, and the train of His robe filled the temple. Above Him were seraphim, each with six wings: with two they covered their faces, with two they covered their feet, and with two they were flying.","They were calling to one another: “Holy, holy, holy is the Lord Almighty; the whole earth is full of his glory.” At the sound of their voices the doorposts and thresholds shook and the temple was filled with smoke.","“Woe to me!” Isaiah cried. “I am ruined! For I am a man of unclean lips, and I live among a people of unclean lips, and my eyes have seen the King, the Lord Almighty.” Then one of the seraphim flew to him with a live coal in his hand, which he had taken with tongs from the altar. He touched Isaiah’s mouth with it and said, “See, this has touched your lips; your guilt is taken away and your sin atoned for.”","Then Isaiah heard the voice of the Lord saying, “Whom shall I send? And who will go for us?” And he said, “Here am I. Send me!” God warned him that the people would hear but not understand, and that judgement would come until the land was devastated. Yet, like a tree cut down whose stump remains, a holy seed would be left in the land. John’s Gospel says that Isaiah saw Jesus’ glory and spoke about Him."],"id":"story-isaiah-s-vision"},{"e":"d","t":"Hezekiah spreads out the letter","r":"2 Kings 19","p":"Hezekiah; Isaiah; Sennacherib","s":"The Assyrian king Sennacherib sends Hezekiah a letter mocking God and threatening Jerusalem. Hezekiah takes the letter to the temple and spreads it out before the Lord in prayer. God answers through Isaiah, and the Assyrian army is struck down overnight.","w":"Take the threat to God and lay it out before Him.","q":"What 'letter' do I need to spread out before God?","l":["Hezekiah was one of the best kings of Judah. He trusted in the Lord, removed the high places and even broke the old bronze snake of Moses, which people had started worshipping. But the Assyrian empire had already destroyed the northern kingdom of Israel, and now King Sennacherib had captured all the fortified cities of Judah and sent his field commander to Jerusalem with a large army.","The field commander stood near the city wall and shouted in Hebrew so that all the people could hear. He mocked Hezekiah’s trust in God: “Do not let Hezekiah persuade you to trust in the Lord. Has the god of any nation ever delivered his land from the hand of the king of Assyria? How then can the Lord deliver Jerusalem?” The people kept silent, as the king had commanded. Hezekiah tore his clothes, put on sackcloth and sent to the prophet Isaiah, who answered, “Do not be afraid of what you have heard.”","Then Sennacherib sent Hezekiah a letter repeating his threats. Hezekiah received the letter from the messengers and read it. Then he went up to the temple of the Lord and spread it out before the Lord. He prayed to the Lord as the only God over all the kingdoms of the earth. He asked God to see and hear how Sennacherib had ridiculed the living God, and to deliver them, so that every kingdom would know that the Lord alone is God.","Isaiah sent a message: because Hezekiah had prayed, God had heard. The king of Assyria would not enter the city or shoot an arrow there. “I will defend this city and save it, for my sake and for the sake of David my servant.” That night the angel of the Lord went out and put to death 185,000 in the Assyrian camp. When the people got up the next morning, there were all the dead bodies. Sennacherib broke camp and went home to Nineveh. Some time later, while he was worshipping in the temple of his god, two of his own sons killed him with the sword."],"id":"story-hezekiah-spreads-out-the-letter"},{"e":"d","t":"Hezekiah's fifteen extra years","r":"2 Kings 20:1-11; Isaiah 38","p":"Hezekiah; Isaiah","s":"Isaiah tells King Hezekiah to put his house in order, because he will die. Hezekiah turns his face to the wall, prays and weeps bitterly. Before Isaiah has even left the palace courtyard, God sends him back: 'I have heard your prayer and seen your tears; I will add fifteen years to your life.' As a sign, the shadow on the stairway goes back ten steps.","w":"God hears prayers and sees tears.","q":"What do I need to bring to God with honest tears?","l":["In those days Hezekiah became ill and was at the point of death. The prophet Isaiah went to him and said, “This is what the Lord says: Put your house in order, because you are going to die; you will not recover.”","Hezekiah turned his face to the wall and prayed to the Lord, “Remember, Lord, how I have walked before you faithfully and with wholehearted devotion and have done what is good in your eyes.” And Hezekiah wept bitterly.","Before Isaiah had left the middle court, the word of the Lord came to him. He was to go back and tell Hezekiah that the Lord had heard his prayer and seen his tears. God would heal him. On the third day he would go up to the temple, and God would add fifteen years to his life and defend the city from the king of Assyria. Isaiah told them to prepare a poultice of figs and apply it to the boil, and Hezekiah recovered.","Hezekiah asked for a sign that the Lord would heal him. Isaiah asked whether the shadow on the stairway built by King Ahaz should go forward ten steps or back ten steps. Hezekiah said, “It is a simple matter for the shadow to go forward ten steps. Rather, have it go back ten steps.” Isaiah called on the Lord, and the Lord made the shadow go back the ten steps it had gone down. Isaiah 38 records the song Hezekiah wrote after his recovery: “The living, the living, they praise you, as I am doing today.” Sadly, soon afterwards he proudly showed all his treasures to envoys from Babylon, and Isaiah warned that one day all of it would be carried off to Babylon."],"id":"story-hezekiah-s-fifteen-extra-years"},{"e":"d","t":"Josiah finds the Book of the Law","r":"2 Kings 22-23","p":"Josiah; Hilkiah","s":"Josiah becomes king at eight years old. While the temple is being repaired, the priest Hilkiah finds the Book of the Law. When it is read to him, Josiah tears his robes, renews the covenant, removes the idols and celebrates the Passover.","w":"Rediscovering God's Word changes a nation.","q":"What changes when I take God's Word seriously?","l":["After Hezekiah, his son Manasseh became king and led Judah into deeper evil than the nations God had driven out, even sacrificing his own son in the fire. Manasseh’s son Amon followed him and was assassinated after two years. Josiah was eight years old when he became king. He did what was right in the eyes of the Lord and followed completely the ways of his father David, not turning aside to the right or to the left.","In the eighteenth year of his reign, Josiah sent his secretary Shaphan to the temple to arrange payment for the workers who were repairing it. There Hilkiah the high priest said, “I have found the Book of the Law in the temple of the Lord.” It had been lost, ignored for so long that no one knew what it said. Shaphan read it, then went to the king and read it aloud to him.","When the king heard the words of the Book of the Law, he tore his robes. He realised how far the nation had drifted, and how great the Lord’s anger must be because their ancestors had not obeyed. He sent officials to inquire of the Lord. They went to the prophet Huldah, who said that judgement would come on Jerusalem, but because Josiah’s heart was responsive and he humbled himself and wept, it would not come in his lifetime.","Josiah called together all the elders, priests, prophets and people, from the least to the greatest, and read in their hearing all the words of the Book of the Covenant. He stood by the pillar and renewed the covenant in the presence of the Lord, to follow Him with all his heart and soul, and all the people pledged themselves too. Then he cleared out every idol and pagan shrine in Judah, and even in the north at Bethel, where Jeroboam’s altar stood. He celebrated a Passover like none held since the days of the judges. Neither before nor after Josiah, the writer says, was there a king like him who turned to the Lord with all his heart and with all his soul and with all his strength."],"id":"story-josiah-finds-the-book-of-the-law"},{"e":"d","t":"The potter's house","r":"Jeremiah 18:1-12","p":"Jeremiah","s":"God sends Jeremiah to watch a potter at his wheel. When a pot is marred, the potter reshapes it into another pot. God says Israel is like clay in His hands.","w":"God can reshape a spoiled life.","q":"Where do I need to let God reshape me?","l":["Jeremiah was called as a young man, around the time of Josiah, to warn Judah that judgement was coming unless they turned back to God. He prophesied for about forty years, through the reigns of several kings, and few listened. He is sometimes called the weeping prophet.","One day the word of the Lord came to him: “Go down to the potter’s house, and there I will give you my message.” So Jeremiah went down and saw the potter working at his wheel. The pot he was shaping from the clay was marred in his hands, so the potter formed it into another pot, shaping it as seemed best to him.","Then God spoke: “Can I not do with you, Israel, as this potter does? Like clay in the hand of the potter, so are you in my hand.” If God announced that a nation would be uprooted, and that nation repented of its evil, He would relent. If He announced that a nation would be built up, and it did evil, He would reconsider the good He had intended.","God told Jeremiah to tell the people to turn from their evil ways and reform. But they replied, “It’s no use. We will continue with our own plans.” In the next chapter God sends Jeremiah to buy a hardened clay jar and smash it in front of the elders, as a sign that a hardened people would be broken. Clay that is still soft can be reshaped; the warning is not to let the heart harden."],"id":"story-the-potter-s-house"},{"e":"d","t":"Jeremiah in the cistern","r":"Jeremiah 38","p":"Jeremiah; Zedekiah; Ebed-Melek","s":"During the siege of Jerusalem, officials say Jeremiah's warnings are discouraging the soldiers. With the king's permission they lower him into a muddy cistern to die. Ebed-Melek, an Ethiopian official, pleads for him and pulls him out with ropes, padding them with old rags so they won't hurt him.","w":"Speaking God's truth can cost you; one brave outsider can make all the difference.","q":"Who is sinking that I could help pull out?","l":["Nebuchadnezzar’s Babylonian army was besieging Jerusalem. Jeremiah kept telling the people what God had said: whoever stayed in the city would die by sword, famine or plague, but whoever surrendered to the Babylonians would live. The city would certainly be handed over to Babylon.","Several officials heard what Jeremiah was saying and went to King Zedekiah. “This man should be put to death. He is discouraging the soldiers who are left in this city, as well as all the people. This man is not seeking the good of these people but their ruin.” The weak king answered, “He is in your hands. The king can do nothing to oppose you.” So they took Jeremiah and lowered him by ropes into the cistern of Malkijah. It had no water in it, only mud, and Jeremiah sank down into the mud.","Ebed-Melek, a Cushite (Ethiopian) official in the royal palace, heard what they had done. He went to the king, who was sitting at the Benjamin Gate. “My lord the king, these men have acted wickedly in all they have done to Jeremiah the prophet. They have thrown him into a cistern, where he will starve to death when there is no longer any bread in the city.” The king ordered him to take thirty men and lift Jeremiah out before he died.","Ebed-Melek went to a room under the treasury in the palace and took some old rags and worn-out clothes from there. He let them down with ropes to Jeremiah and said, “Put these old rags and worn-out clothes under your arms to pad the ropes.” Jeremiah did so, and they pulled him up out of the cistern. Afterwards the king secretly asked Jeremiah for advice, but was too afraid to follow it. When Jerusalem fell, God sent a special promise to Ebed-Melek: “I will save you; you will not fall by the sword but will escape with your life, because you trust in me.”"],"id":"story-jeremiah-in-the-cistern"},{"e":"x","t":"Daniel's diet","r":"Daniel 1","p":"Daniel; Hananiah; Mishael; Azariah","s":"Taken to Babylon as young men, Daniel and his three friends refuse the king's food. They ask to be tested on vegetables and water for ten days, and end up healthier than everyone else. God gives them unusual wisdom.","w":"Faithfulness in small things in a foreign culture.","q":"Where do I need to quietly stand out?","l":["In the third year of King Jehoiakim of Judah, Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon besieged Jerusalem, and the Lord delivered Jehoiakim into his hand. Nebuchadnezzar took some of the articles from the temple to the temple of his god in Babylon. He also ordered his chief official to bring young men from Israel’s royal family and nobility: handsome, without physical defect, showing aptitude for every kind of learning, to be trained for three years in the language and literature of the Babylonians and then serve in the king’s court.","Among them were Daniel, Hananiah, Mishael and Azariah, from the tribe of Judah. The official gave them new Babylonian names: Daniel became Belteshazzar, Hananiah became Shadrach, Mishael became Meshach, and Azariah became Abednego. Their Hebrew names honoured the God of Israel; their new names honoured Babylonian gods. They were assigned a daily share of food and wine from the king’s table.","But Daniel resolved not to defile himself with the royal food and wine, and asked permission not to. God had caused the official to show favour and compassion to Daniel, but the official was afraid: if the king saw them looking worse than the other young men, it would cost the official his head. So Daniel went to the guard and suggested a test: “Please test your servants for ten days. Give us nothing but vegetables to eat and water to drink. Then compare our appearance with that of the young men who eat the royal food.”","At the end of the ten days they looked healthier and better nourished than any of the young men who ate the royal food. So the guard took away their choice food and wine and gave them vegetables instead. To these four young men God gave knowledge and understanding of all kinds of literature and learning, and Daniel could understand visions and dreams of all kinds. When the king interviewed them, he found them ten times better than all the magicians and enchanters in his whole kingdom. Daniel remained in royal service for about seventy years."],"id":"story-daniel-s-diet"},{"e":"x","t":"Nebuchadnezzar's dream","r":"Daniel 2","p":"Daniel; Nebuchadnezzar","s":"King Nebuchadnezzar demands that his wise men tell him both his dream and its meaning, or die. Daniel and his friends pray, and God reveals it: a huge statue of gold, silver, bronze and iron, smashed by a rock cut out without human hands, which becomes a mountain filling the earth.","w":"Kingdoms rise and fall, but God's kingdom will last forever.","q":"What do I need to pray about with friends before I act?","l":["In the second year of his reign, Nebuchadnezzar had dreams that troubled him so much he could not sleep. He summoned the magicians, enchanters, sorcerers and astrologers. But he would not tell them the dream. “If you do not tell me what my dream was and interpret it, I will have you cut into pieces. But if you tell me the dream and explain it, you will receive gifts and rewards.” They protested that no king had ever asked such a thing and that only the gods could reveal it. The king became so furious that he ordered the execution of all the wise men of Babylon, which included Daniel and his friends.","When the commander of the guard came to carry out the order, Daniel spoke to him with wisdom and tact and asked the king for time. Then Daniel went home and explained the matter to Hananiah, Mishael and Azariah, and urged them to plead for mercy from the God of heaven. During the night the mystery was revealed to Daniel in a vision. Daniel praised God: “He changes times and seasons; he deposes kings and raises up others. He gives wisdom to the wise. He reveals deep and hidden things.”","Daniel was brought before the king. “No wise man can explain this mystery, but there is a God in heaven who reveals mysteries.” The king had seen a huge, dazzling statue. Its head was pure gold, its chest and arms silver, its belly and thighs bronze, its legs iron, and its feet partly iron and partly baked clay. A rock was cut out, but not by human hands. It struck the statue on its feet and smashed them. The whole statue was broken to pieces and became like chaff on a threshing floor in summer, blown away without trace. But the rock became a huge mountain and filled the whole earth.","Daniel explained: “You, Your Majesty, are that head of gold.” After him would come other kingdoms, each inferior, ending with a kingdom strong as iron but divided. “In the time of those kings, the God of heaven will set up a kingdom that will never be destroyed. It will crush all those kingdoms and bring them to an end, but it will itself endure forever.” Nebuchadnezzar fell prostrate before Daniel and said, “Surely your God is the God of gods and the Lord of kings.” He made Daniel ruler over the province of Babylon, and at Daniel’s request appointed his three friends as administrators. Christians have long seen the rock as Christ and His kingdom."],"id":"story-nebuchadnezzar-s-dream"},{"e":"x","t":"The fiery furnace","r":"Daniel 3","p":"Shadrach; Meshach; Abednego; Nebuchadnezzar","s":"King Nebuchadnezzar orders everyone to bow to a gold statue. Three friends refuse, saying God can save them, 'but even if he does not' they won't bow. They are thrown into the furnace, and the king sees a fourth man walking with them in the fire.","w":"Faith that holds even if God doesn't rescue, and God is with us in the fire.","q":"Do I trust God 'even if'?","l":["King Nebuchadnezzar made a gold statue about 27 metres high and 2.7 metres wide and set it up on the plain of Dura in Babylon. He summoned all the officials of his empire to its dedication. A herald proclaimed: “As soon as you hear the sound of the horn, flute, zither, lyre, harp, pipe and all kinds of music, you must fall down and worship the image of gold. Whoever does not will immediately be thrown into a blazing furnace.” When the music played, all the peoples fell down and worshipped.","Some astrologers came forward and denounced Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego, Jews the king had set over the affairs of Babylon, who would not serve his gods or worship the statue. Furious with rage, Nebuchadnezzar summoned them and gave them one more chance. “If you do not worship it, you will be thrown immediately into a blazing furnace. Then what god will be able to rescue you from my hand?”","They said they did not need to defend themselves. The God they served was able to rescue them from the furnace and from the king’s hand. “But even if he does not,” they would still not serve his gods or worship the gold statue. The king’s face changed. He ordered the furnace heated seven times hotter than usual and had his strongest soldiers tie them up, fully dressed, and throw them in. The furnace was so hot that the flames killed the soldiers who threw them in.","Then King Nebuchadnezzar leaped to his feet in amazement. “Weren’t there three men that we tied up and threw into the fire? Look! I see four men walking around in the fire, unbound and unharmed, and the fourth looks like a son of the gods.” He called them out. The officials crowded around and saw that the fire had not harmed their bodies, not a hair of their heads was singed, their robes were not scorched, and there was no smell of fire on them. Nebuchadnezzar praised the God of Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego, who had sent His angel to rescue servants who trusted Him and would rather give up their lives than worship any other god."],"id":"story-the-fiery-furnace"},{"e":"x","t":"Nebuchadnezzar humbled","r":"Daniel 4","p":"Nebuchadnezzar; Daniel","s":"Nebuchadnezzar dreams of a great tree cut down. Daniel warns him to turn from his pride. A year later, admiring Babylon from his palace roof, the king boasts, 'Is not this the great Babylon I have built?' He loses his mind and lives like an animal for seven periods of time, until he looks up to heaven and praises God.","w":"Those who walk in pride God is able to humble.","q":"Where am I taking credit that belongs to God?","l":["This chapter is written as a letter from King Nebuchadnezzar himself to all the peoples of the earth, telling what the Most High God had done for him. He was at home in his palace, contented and prosperous, when he had a dream that made him afraid. None of the wise men could interpret it, so finally he told it to Daniel.","He had seen an enormous tree in the middle of the land. It grew large and strong, its top touched the sky, and it was visible to the ends of the earth. Its leaves were beautiful and its fruit abundant; animals rested under it and birds lived in its branches. Then a holy messenger came down from heaven and gave orders. He ordered the tree cut down, but the stump and roots left in the ground, bound with iron and bronze. The man it stood for would live with the animals, drenched with dew, with the mind of an animal, until seven periods of time had passed. Then everyone would know that the Most High rules over all kingdoms.","Daniel was greatly perplexed and terrified, but he told the king the truth: “Your Majesty, you are that tree.” The king would be driven away from people and live with the wild animals until he acknowledged that the Most High is sovereign. The stump meant his kingdom would be restored once he did. Daniel pleaded, “Renounce your sins by doing what is right, and your wickedness by being kind to the oppressed. It may be that then your prosperity will continue.”","Twelve months later, as the king was walking on the roof of the royal palace of Babylon, he said, “Is not this the great Babylon I have built as the royal residence, by my mighty power and for the glory of my majesty?” The words were still on his lips when a voice came from heaven, and immediately it happened. He was driven away from people and ate grass like the ox; his body was drenched with dew, his hair grew like the feathers of an eagle and his nails like the claws of a bird. At the end of that time, he raised his eyes toward heaven, his sanity was restored, and he praised the Most High. His honour and kingdom were restored. His letter ends: “Now I, Nebuchadnezzar, praise and exalt and glorify the King of heaven, because everything he does is right and all his ways are just. And those who walk in pride he is able to humble.”"],"id":"story-nebuchadnezzar-humbled"},{"e":"x","t":"The writing on the wall","r":"Daniel 5","p":"Belshazzar; Daniel","s":"King Belshazzar throws a feast and drinks from the goblets taken from God's temple. A hand appears and writes on the wall. Daniel reads it: God has weighed the king and found him wanting. That night Belshazzar is killed.","w":"Mocking God has consequences.","q":"What do I treat lightly that is holy to God?","l":["Years later Belshazzar ruled in Babylon. He gave a great banquet for a thousand of his nobles and drank wine with them. While he was drinking, he ordered the gold and silver goblets that Nebuchadnezzar had taken from the temple in Jerusalem to be brought in, so that he and his nobles, wives and concubines could drink from them. As they drank, they praised the gods of gold and silver, bronze, iron, wood and stone.","Suddenly the fingers of a human hand appeared and wrote on the plaster of the wall, near the lampstand in the royal palace. The king watched the hand as it wrote. His face turned pale, he was so frightened that his legs became weak and his knees were knocking together. He called for the enchanters and promised that whoever read the writing would be clothed in purple, have a gold chain placed around his neck and be made the third highest ruler in the kingdom. None of them could read it.","The queen, hearing the noise, came into the banquet hall and reminded him of Daniel, in whom there was the spirit of the holy gods, who had served Nebuchadnezzar. Daniel was brought in, now an old man. He told the king to keep his gifts. Then he reminded Belshazzar how God had humbled his predecessor Nebuchadnezzar until he acknowledged that the Most High is sovereign. Belshazzar knew all this, Daniel said, and still had not humbled himself. He had set himself up against the Lord of heaven and praised gods that cannot see or hear, instead of honouring the God who held his life in His hand.","Daniel read the inscription: MENE, MENE, TEKEL, PARSIN. Mene: God has numbered the days of your reign and brought it to an end. Tekel: you have been weighed on the scales and found wanting. Peres: your kingdom is divided and given to the Medes and Persians. Belshazzar kept his promise and clothed Daniel in purple. That very night Belshazzar, king of the Babylonians, was killed, and Darius the Mede took over the kingdom. Historians record that Babylon fell to the Persians in 539 BC."],"id":"story-the-writing-on-the-wall"},{"e":"x","t":"Daniel in the lions' den","r":"Daniel 6","p":"Daniel; Darius","s":"Jealous officials trick King Darius into a law that no one may pray to anyone but the king. Daniel keeps praying three times a day with his windows open toward Jerusalem. He is thrown to the lions, and God shuts their mouths.","w":"A steady prayer habit holds under pressure.","q":"Is my prayer life a habit that would hold under pressure?","l":["Darius appointed 120 regional governors over his kingdom, with three administrators over them, one of whom was Daniel. Daniel so distinguished himself by his exceptional qualities that the king planned to set him over the whole kingdom. The other administrators and governors tried to find grounds for charges against him, but they could find no corruption in him, because he was trustworthy and neither corrupt nor negligent. They said, “We will never find any basis for charges against this man unless it has something to do with the law of his God.”","So they went as a group to the king and proposed a decree: for thirty days, anyone who prayed to any god or human being except the king would be thrown into the lions’ den. They flattered Darius into signing it into law, a law of the Medes and Persians, which could not be repealed.","When Daniel learned that the decree had been published, he went home to his upstairs room where the windows opened toward Jerusalem. Three times a day he got down on his knees and prayed, giving thanks to his God, just as he had done before. The men went as a group and found him praying. They reported him to the king, who was greatly distressed and tried until sunset to rescue Daniel, but the law could not be changed. Daniel was thrown into the lions’ den. The king said, “May your God, whom you serve continually, rescue you!” A stone was placed over the mouth of the den, sealed with the king’s own ring.","The king spent the night without eating and could not sleep. At first light he hurried to the den and called in an anguished voice, “Daniel, servant of the living God, has your God, whom you serve continually, been able to rescue you from the lions?” Daniel answered, “May the king live forever! My God sent his angel, and he shut the mouths of the lions. They have not hurt me, because I was found innocent in his sight.” The king was overjoyed and had Daniel lifted out, and no wound was found on him, because he had trusted in his God. The men who had falsely accused Daniel were thrown into the den instead. Darius wrote to all the nations: “In every part of my kingdom people must fear and reverence the God of Daniel. For he is the living God and he endures forever.”"],"id":"story-daniel-in-the-lions-den"},{"e":"x","t":"Ezekiel and the dry bones","r":"Ezekiel 37:1-14","p":"Ezekiel","s":"God shows Ezekiel a valley full of dry bones and tells him to prophesy to them. The bones rattle together, flesh covers them, and when he prophesies to the breath, they stand up as a vast army.","w":"God brings life where everything seems dead.","q":"What feels dead that I need to invite God's breath into?","l":["Ezekiel was a priest carried off to Babylon in an early wave of exiles. There, by the Kebar River, God called him to be a prophet. For years he warned that Jerusalem would fall, and then the news came: the city had been destroyed and the temple burned. The exiles said, “Our bones are dried up and our hope is gone; we are cut off.”","The hand of the Lord was on Ezekiel, and He brought him out by the Spirit and set him in the middle of a valley full of bones. God led him back and forth among them. There were a great many bones on the floor of the valley, bones that were very dry. God asked him, “Son of man, can these bones live?” Ezekiel answered, “Sovereign Lord, you alone know.”","God said, “Prophesy to these bones and say to them, ‘Dry bones, hear the word of the Lord! I will make breath enter you, and you will come to life.’” So Ezekiel prophesied as he was commanded. As he was prophesying, there was a noise, a rattling sound, and the bones came together, bone to bone. Tendons and flesh appeared on them and skin covered them, but there was no breath in them.","Then God said, “Prophesy to the breath; prophesy, son of man, and say, ‘Come, breath, from the four winds and breathe into these slain, that they may live.’” In Hebrew the same word means breath, wind and spirit. Ezekiel prophesied, and breath entered them; they came to life and stood up on their feet, a vast army. God explained: these bones are the whole house of Israel. “I will open your graves and bring you up from them. I will put my Spirit in you and you will live, and I will settle you in your own land. Then you will know that I the Lord have spoken, and I have done it.”"],"id":"story-ezekiel-and-the-dry-bones"},{"e":"x","t":"The exiles return","r":"Ezra 1; 3","p":"Cyrus; Zerubbabel; Jeshua","s":"Seventy years after the exile, Persian king Cyrus issues a decree that the Jews may go home and rebuild the temple. About 50,000 return. When the foundation is laid, the young shout for joy, while the old men who had seen Solomon's temple weep aloud, and no one can tell the joy from the weeping.","w":"God keeps His promises of restoration, even after discipline.","q":"What is God rebuilding in my life?","l":["Long before, Jeremiah had prophesied that the exile in Babylon would last seventy years, and Isaiah had even named a coming king called Cyrus who would rebuild Jerusalem. In the first year of Cyrus king of Persia, in order to fulfil the word of the Lord spoken by Jeremiah, the Lord moved the heart of Cyrus to make a proclamation throughout his realm and put it in writing.","He declared that the Lord, the God of heaven, had given him all the kingdoms of the earth and appointed him to build a temple in Jerusalem. Any of God’s people who wished could go up and build it. Cyrus even brought out the gold and silver articles Nebuchadnezzar had carried away from the temple, 5,400 of them, and returned them. Neighbours gave silver, gold, goods and livestock. About 50,000 people returned, led by Zerubbabel, of David’s royal line, and Jeshua the priest.","When the seventh month came, the people assembled together as one in Jerusalem. Even though they were afraid of the peoples around them, they rebuilt the altar on its old foundation and began to offer sacrifices again, morning and evening. They celebrated the Festival of Tabernacles. Then they hired masons and carpenters and bought cedar logs from Lebanon.","In the second year, when the builders laid the foundation of the temple of the Lord, the priests in their vestments with trumpets and the Levites with cymbals took their places to praise the Lord, as King David had prescribed. They sang, “He is good; his love toward Israel endures forever.” All the people gave a great shout of praise. But many of the older priests, Levites and family heads, who had seen the former temple, wept aloud when they saw the foundation of this temple, while many others shouted for joy. No one could distinguish the sound of the shouts of joy from the sound of weeping, because the people made so much noise. The sound was heard far away."],"id":"story-the-exiles-return"},{"e":"x","t":"Esther becomes queen","r":"Esther 2","p":"Esther; Mordecai; Xerxes","s":"After Queen Vashti is removed, King Xerxes searches the empire for a new queen. Hadassah, an orphan raised by her cousin Mordecai, is taken to the palace under her Persian name Esther. She wins everyone's favour and is crowned queen, keeping her Jewish identity secret. Mordecai overhears a plot to kill the king and reports it through Esther.","w":"God was positioning people long before the crisis came.","q":"How might God be preparing me now for something later?","l":["Xerxes ruled the Persian empire, 127 provinces stretching from India to Cush. In the third year of his reign he gave a banquet lasting 180 days to display his wealth, followed by a seven-day feast. On the last day, in high spirits from wine, he commanded Queen Vashti to come wearing her royal crown to display her beauty to the people and nobles. She refused. The king was furious, and on his advisers’ suggestion Vashti was removed as queen.","Later the king’s attendants proposed a search for beautiful young women throughout the empire, so that the one who pleased the king would become queen. In the citadel of Susa lived a Jew named Mordecai, of the tribe of Benjamin, whose family had been carried into exile. He had a cousin named Hadassah, whom he had brought up because she had neither father nor mother. This young woman, also known as Esther, had a lovely figure and was beautiful.","Esther was taken to the king’s palace. She pleased Hegai, who was in charge of the women, and won his favour, and he gave her special care. Esther had not revealed her nationality or family background, because Mordecai had forbidden her to. Every day Mordecai walked back and forth near the courtyard to find out how she was. When her turn came to go to the king, she asked for nothing other than what Hegai suggested, and she won the favour of everyone who saw her.","The king was attracted to Esther more than to any of the other women. He set a royal crown on her head and made her queen instead of Vashti, and gave a great banquet in her honour. Around that time, while Mordecai was sitting at the king’s gate, he learned of a plot by two of the king’s officers to assassinate Xerxes. He told Queen Esther, who reported it to the king, giving credit to Mordecai. The plot was confirmed, the two officials were executed, and the event was recorded in the king’s annals. It seemed forgotten, but it would matter later."],"id":"story-esther-becomes-queen"},{"e":"x","t":"Esther","r":"Esther 4-7","p":"Esther; Mordecai; Haman; Xerxes","s":"Haman plots to wipe out the Jews in Persia. Mordecai urges Queen Esther to go to the king: 'Who knows if you have come to your royal position for such a time as this?' After three days of fasting she risks her life, and Haman's plot is exposed.","w":"God places people where they are for a purpose.","q":"Why might God have placed me exactly where I am?","l":["After these events, King Xerxes honoured Haman, elevating him above all the other nobles. All the royal officials at the king’s gate knelt and paid honour to Haman, but Mordecai would not. When Haman learned that Mordecai was a Jew, he scorned the idea of killing only Mordecai, and looked for a way to destroy all Mordecai’s people throughout the empire. Lots (purim) were cast to choose the day. Haman told the king that a certain people did not obey the king’s laws and offered ten thousand talents of silver for the cost. The king gave Haman his signet ring, and a decree went out to annihilate all the Jews, young and old, women and children, on a single day.","Mordecai tore his clothes, put on sackcloth and ashes and wailed loudly and bitterly. Esther sent a servant to find out why, and Mordecai sent back a copy of the decree and urged her to go to the king and plead for her people. Esther replied that anyone who approached the king without being summoned would be put to death unless the king extended his gold sceptre, and she had not been called for thirty days. Mordecai answered that she should not imagine that she alone would escape because she lived in the palace. If she kept silent, help for the Jews would come from somewhere else, but she and her family would perish. “And who knows but that you have come to your royal position for such a time as this?”","Esther asked him to gather all the Jews in Susa to fast for her for three days, as she and her attendants would. Then she would go to the king, even though it was against the law. “And if I perish, I perish.” On the third day she put on her royal robes and stood in the inner court. When the king saw her, he was pleased and held out the gold sceptre. She invited the king and Haman to a banquet, and then to a second one. Haman went home happy, but was enraged to see Mordecai, so on his wife’s advice he built a gallows over twenty metres high to hang Mordecai on.","That night the king could not sleep, so he had the record of his reign read to him, and heard how Mordecai had exposed the assassination plot and had never been rewarded. In the morning Haman arrived to ask permission to hang Mordecai, and the king asked him, “What should be done for the man the king delights to honour?” Thinking he meant himself, Haman described a royal robe, a royal horse and a public procession. The king said, “Go at once. Do just as you have suggested for Mordecai the Jew.” At the second banquet Esther revealed that she and her people had been sold for destruction. “Who is he? Where is he?” the king demanded. “An adversary and enemy! This vile Haman!” Haman was hanged on the gallows he had built for Mordecai. A new decree allowed the Jews to defend themselves, Mordecai was honoured, and the festival of Purim still celebrates the deliverance. God’s name is never mentioned in the book of Esther, yet His hand is on every page."],"id":"story-esther"},{"e":"x","t":"Nehemiah rebuilds the wall","r":"Nehemiah 1-6","p":"Nehemiah; Artaxerxes; Sanballat","s":"Nehemiah, cupbearer to the Persian king, weeps and prays when he hears Jerusalem's walls are in ruins. The king sends him to rebuild. Despite mocking and threats, the people work with a tool in one hand and a weapon in the other, finishing in 52 days.","w":"Prayer and hard work go together.","q":"What broken thing is God stirring me to help rebuild?","l":["Nehemiah was cupbearer to King Artaxerxes of Persia, a position of great trust. Some men came from Judah, and he asked about the Jews who had returned and about Jerusalem. They told him the survivors were in great trouble and disgrace, the wall of Jerusalem was broken down and its gates burned with fire. When Nehemiah heard this, he sat down and wept. For some days he mourned, fasted and prayed, confessing the sins of Israel, his own included, and reminding God of His promise to gather His people if they returned to Him.","Four months later, as he served the king wine, the king noticed his sadness. “Why does your face look so sad when you are not ill? This can be nothing but sadness of heart.” Nehemiah was very much afraid, but he told the king the city of his ancestors lay in ruins. The king asked, “What is it you want?” Nehemiah writes, “Then I prayed to the God of heaven, and I answered the king.” He asked to be sent to rebuild the city, and for letters guaranteeing safe passage and timber from the king’s forest. Because the gracious hand of his God was on him, the king granted his requests.","In Jerusalem Nehemiah inspected the walls secretly by night. Then he told the officials, “You see the trouble we are in. Come, let us rebuild the wall of Jerusalem.” They replied, “Let us start rebuilding,” and began the good work. Chapter 3 lists family after family each repairing the section of wall near their own house. Sanballat, Tobiah and Geshem ridiculed them: “What are those feeble Jews doing? If even a fox climbed up on it, he would break down their wall of stones!” Nehemiah prayed and the people kept working, because they worked with all their heart.","When the enemies plotted to attack, Nehemiah posted people by families with swords, spears and bows. “Don’t be afraid of them. Remember the Lord, who is great and awesome, and fight for your families.” From then on half the men did the work while the other half were armed. Those who carried materials did their work with one hand and held a weapon in the other. The trumpeter stayed beside Nehemiah to rally them. The enemies tried to lure Nehemiah away to harm him, but he answered, “I am carrying on a great project and cannot go down.” The wall was completed in fifty-two days. When the surrounding nations heard, they lost their self-confidence, because they realised that this work had been done with the help of God."],"id":"story-nehemiah-rebuilds-the-wall"},{"e":"g","t":"The birth of Jesus","r":"Luke 2:1-20","p":"Mary; Joseph; The shepherds","s":"A census takes Mary and Joseph to Bethlehem, where Jesus is born and laid in a manger. Angels announce the good news to shepherds in the fields, who hurry to find Him and then tell everyone.","w":"God came as a baby, and the news went first to ordinary workers.","q":"Who could I tell about Jesus this week?","l":["Caesar Augustus ordered a census of the whole Roman world, and everyone had to go to their own town to register. Joseph, a descendant of David, travelled from Nazareth in Galilee to Bethlehem, David’s town, with Mary, who was engaged to him and expecting a baby. Months earlier the angel Gabriel had told Mary she would conceive by the Holy Spirit and give birth to the Son of God, and an angel had told Joseph in a dream to take her as his wife.","While they were in Bethlehem, Mary gave birth to her firstborn son. She wrapped Him in cloths and laid Him in a feeding trough for animals, because there was no guest room available. The King of kings arrived not in a palace, but in the humblest place imaginable, fulfilling Micah’s prophecy that the ruler of Israel would come from little Bethlehem.","That night, shepherds were out in the nearby fields keeping watch over their flocks. Shepherds were near the bottom of society, yet they were the first to hear. An angel appeared and the glory of the Lord shone around them, and they were terrified. The angel told them not to be afraid, because he brought good news of great joy for all people: a Saviour had been born in David’s town, the Messiah, the Lord. The sign would be a baby wrapped in cloths and lying in a manger. Suddenly a huge company of angels appeared, praising God and announcing glory to God and peace on earth.","The shepherds hurried off and found Mary, Joseph and the baby, just as they had been told. Then they spread the word about everything they had heard, and everyone who heard was amazed. Mary treasured all these things and thought about them deeply. The shepherds went back to their sheep, glorifying and praising God. The first evangelists were ordinary working men who simply told what they had seen."],"id":"story-the-birth-of-jesus"},{"e":"g","t":"The boy Jesus in the temple","r":"Luke 2:41-52","p":"Jesus; Mary; Joseph","s":"At twelve, Jesus stays behind in Jerusalem after the Passover. After three days of searching, His parents find Him in the temple, listening and asking questions. He says 'Didn't you know I had to be in my Father's house?'","w":"Even as a boy, Jesus knew who His Father was.","q":"Do I delight in being where my Father is?","l":["Every year Jesus’ parents went to Jerusalem for the Passover festival. When He was twelve, they went as usual. After the festival, Mary and Joseph started home, travelling with a large group of relatives and friends. Without their realising it, Jesus stayed behind in Jerusalem. They assumed He was somewhere in the group and travelled for a whole day before they began looking for Him.","When they couldn’t find Him, they went back to Jerusalem. After three days of searching, they found Him in the temple courts, sitting among the teachers, listening to them and asking questions. Everyone who heard Him was amazed at His understanding and His answers.","His parents were astonished. Mary asked Him why He had treated them like this, and told Him how anxiously they had been searching. Jesus asked why they had been searching: didn’t they know He had to be in His Father’s house? They didn’t understand what He meant. These are the first words of Jesus recorded in the Gospels, and they are about His Father.","Then He went down to Nazareth with them and was obedient to them. His mother treasured all these things in her heart. Luke sums up the next eighteen years in one sentence: Jesus grew in wisdom and stature, and in favour with God and people. Even the Son of God spent most of His life in ordinary, hidden faithfulness."],"id":"story-the-boy-jesus-in-the-temple"},{"e":"g","t":"Jesus is baptised","r":"Matthew 3","p":"John the Baptist; Jesus","s":"John the Baptist preaches repentance by the Jordan, wearing camel hair and eating locusts and wild honey. When Jesus comes to be baptised, John protests that he should be baptised by Jesus. As Jesus comes up from the water, heaven opens, the Spirit descends like a dove, and the Father says He is pleased with His Son.","w":"Jesus begins His ministry identified with us and affirmed by the Father.","q":"What does it mean to me that the Father calls me His child?","l":["John, son of the priest Zechariah and Mary’s relative Elizabeth, appeared in the wilderness of Judea preaching a simple message: repent, because the kingdom of heaven has come near. He was the voice Isaiah had foretold, calling people to prepare the way for the Lord. He wore clothes of camel hair with a leather belt, and ate locusts and wild honey. Crowds came from Jerusalem and all Judea, confessed their sins and were baptised by him in the Jordan River.","When religious leaders came, John warned them not to rely on being Abraham’s descendants, but to produce fruit that showed real repentance. He said someone more powerful was coming after him, whose sandals he was not worthy to carry. John baptised with water, but this One would baptise with the Holy Spirit and fire.","Then Jesus came from Galilee to the Jordan to be baptised by John. John tried to stop Him, saying he needed to be baptised by Jesus, not the other way around. Jesus answered that it was right to do this to fulfil all righteousness. Jesus had no sin to confess, but He stepped into the water alongside sinners, taking His place with us.","As soon as Jesus came up out of the water, heaven was opened, and He saw the Spirit of God descending like a dove and resting on Him. A voice from heaven said that this was His beloved Son, with whom He was well pleased. Father, Son and Holy Spirit are all present in this moment. Before Jesus had preached a sermon or done a miracle, the Father declared His love and delight."],"id":"story-jesus-is-baptised"},{"e":"g","t":"Jesus is tempted","r":"Matthew 4:1-11","p":"Jesus; The devil","s":"After forty days of fasting in the wilderness, Jesus is tempted three times: to turn stones into bread, to throw Himself off the temple, and to worship the devil in exchange for the world's kingdoms. Each time He answers with Scripture from Deuteronomy.","w":"Jesus overcame temptation where Israel and Adam failed, with God's Word.","q":"Which verse do I need ready for my temptation?","l":["Straight after His baptism, the Spirit led Jesus into the wilderness to be tempted by the devil. After fasting forty days and forty nights, He was hungry. The tempter came and said that if He really was the Son of God, He should tell these stones to become bread. Jesus answered from Deuteronomy that people do not live on bread alone, but on every word that comes from the mouth of God.","Then the devil took Him to the holy city and had Him stand on the highest point of the temple. If He was the Son of God, he said, He should throw Himself down, and this time the devil quoted Scripture too, from Psalm 91, saying the angels would catch Him. Jesus answered with another verse: it is also written, do not put the Lord your God to the test.","Finally the devil took Him to a very high mountain and showed Him all the kingdoms of the world and their splendour. He offered all of it, if Jesus would bow down and worship him. It was a shortcut to the throne that skipped the cross. Jesus said, “Away from me, Satan!” and quoted Deuteronomy once more: worship the Lord your God, and serve Him only.","Then the devil left Him, and angels came and attended Him. Israel had been tested forty years in the wilderness and failed; Adam had been tested in a garden and failed. Jesus was tested in a desert, hungry and alone, and stood firm, using the same weapon available to us: God’s Word, known and believed. Hebrews says that because He was tempted in every way, yet without sin, He can sympathise with our weaknesses."],"id":"story-jesus-is-tempted"},{"e":"g","t":"Water into wine","r":"John 2:1-11","p":"Jesus; Mary; The servants","s":"At a wedding in Cana the wine runs out. Mary tells the servants 'Do whatever he tells you'. Jesus has them fill six stone jars with water, and it becomes the best wine of the feast.","w":"Jesus' first sign brings joy, and the servants' obedience comes first.","q":"What is Jesus telling me to simply do?","l":["On the third day there was a wedding at Cana in Galilee. Jesus’ mother was there, and Jesus and His disciples had also been invited. A village wedding could last a week, and running out of wine would bring deep shame on the family. The wine ran out.","Mary said to Jesus, “They have no more wine.” He replied that His hour had not yet come. But Mary simply turned to the servants and told them to do whatever He told them. Nearby stood six stone water jars, the kind used for Jewish ceremonial washing, each holding about eighty to a hundred and twenty litres.","Jesus told the servants to fill the jars with water, and they filled them to the brim. Then He told them to draw some out and take it to the master of the banquet. The master tasted the water that had become wine. He did not know where it had come from, though the servants knew. He called the bridegroom aside and said that everyone brings out the best wine first and the cheaper wine later, but this bridegroom had saved the best till now.","John says this was the first of the signs through which Jesus revealed His glory, and His disciples believed in Him. His first miracle was not in a temple but at a party, rescuing a family from embarrassment, and it produced an abundance of the very best. It also hints at what He came to do: replace water for ritual washing with the new wine of His kingdom."],"id":"story-water-into-wine"},{"e":"g","t":"Nicodemus at night","r":"John 3:1-21","p":"Jesus; Nicodemus","s":"Nicodemus, a Pharisee and teacher, comes to Jesus at night. Jesus tells him he must be born again by the Spirit. Here Jesus says 'For God so loved the world that he gave his one and only Son'.","w":"New life is a gift of the Spirit, not a religious achievement.","q":"What does being 'born again' mean in my life now?","l":["Nicodemus was a Pharisee and a member of the Jewish ruling council, a respected teacher of Israel. He came to Jesus at night, perhaps to avoid being seen. He said they knew Jesus was a teacher come from God, because no one could perform such signs unless God was with him.","Jesus went straight to the heart of it: no one can see the kingdom of God unless they are born again, or born from above. Nicodemus took it literally and asked how a grown man could enter his mother’s womb a second time. Jesus explained that He meant being born of water and the Spirit. Flesh gives birth to flesh, but the Spirit gives birth to spirit. Like the wind, you can’t see where the Spirit comes from or where He is going, but you see His effects.","Nicodemus asked how this could be. Jesus was surprised that Israel’s teacher did not understand. Then He pointed back to the bronze snake: just as Moses lifted up the snake in the wilderness, the Son of Man must be lifted up, so that everyone who believes may have eternal life. Then comes the most famous verse in the Bible, John 3:16: God loved the world so much that He gave His one and only Son, so that whoever believes in Him will not perish but have eternal life. God did not send His Son to condemn the world, but to save it.","We don’t hear what Nicodemus decided that night. But later he defended Jesus before the council, and after the crucifixion he came with Joseph of Arimathea, bringing about thirty-five kilograms of myrrh and aloes to bury Jesus’ body. The man who came in the dark ended up standing with Jesus in the open."],"id":"story-nicodemus-at-night"},{"e":"g","t":"The woman at the well","r":"John 4:1-42","p":"Jesus; The Samaritan woman","s":"At noon Jesus asks a Samaritan woman for water and offers her living water. He knows about her five husbands, yet doesn't reject her. She runs back to tell her town, and many believe.","w":"Jesus crosses every barrier and knows us completely.","q":"What would I tell others about what Jesus knows about me?","l":["Jesus was travelling from Judea to Galilee, and He had to go through Samaria. Most Jews avoided Samaria, because Jews and Samaritans despised each other. Tired from the journey, Jesus sat down by Jacob’s well near the town of Sychar. It was about noon, the hottest part of the day. His disciples had gone into town to buy food.","A Samaritan woman came to draw water, alone and at noon, perhaps to avoid the other women. Jesus asked her for a drink. She was astonished that a Jewish man would ask a Samaritan woman for anything. Jesus answered that if she knew who was asking, she would have asked Him, and He would have given her living water. Whoever drank from the well would be thirsty again, but the water He gives becomes a spring welling up to eternal life. She said, “Sir, give me this water.”","Jesus told her to go and call her husband. She said she had no husband. Jesus agreed: she had had five husbands, and the man she was with now was not her husband. She called Him a prophet and quickly turned to a religious debate about where to worship. Jesus said a time was coming when true worshippers would worship the Father in Spirit and in truth. She said she knew the Messiah was coming. Jesus said, “I, the one speaking to you, I am he.” It is the first time in John that Jesus says this so plainly, and He says it to a Samaritan woman with a messy past.","She left her water jar, went back to the town and told people to come and see a man who told her everything she ever did. Could this be the Messiah? Many Samaritans believed because of her testimony. They asked Jesus to stay, and He stayed two days. Then they told her that now they believed not just because of what she said, but because they had heard Him themselves and knew He really was the Saviour of the world."],"id":"story-the-woman-at-the-well"},{"e":"g","t":"The great catch of fish","r":"Luke 5:1-11","p":"Jesus; Simon Peter; James; John","s":"Jesus teaches from Simon's boat, then tells him to let down the nets in deep water. Simon has fished all night with nothing, but obeys. The nets fill until they begin to break. Simon falls at Jesus' knees: 'Go away from me, Lord; I am a sinful man!' Jesus says from now on he will catch people.","w":"Obedience when it makes no sense, and a call that starts with mercy.","q":"Where is Jesus telling me to 'go out deeper'?","l":["One day Jesus was standing by the Lake of Gennesaret (the Sea of Galilee), with crowds pressing around Him to hear the word of God. He saw two boats at the water’s edge, left by fishermen who were washing their nets. He got into the one belonging to Simon, asked him to push out a little from shore, then sat down and taught the people from the boat.","When He had finished speaking, He told Simon to go out into deep water and let down the nets for a catch. Simon was the professional fisherman; Jesus was a carpenter turned teacher. Simon explained that they had worked hard all night and caught nothing. But, he said, because Jesus said so, he would let down the nets.","When they did, they caught such a large number of fish that their nets began to break. They signalled to their partners in the other boat, James and John, sons of Zebedee, to come and help, and they filled both boats so full that they began to sink.","When Simon Peter saw this, he fell at Jesus’ knees and begged Jesus to go away from him, because he was a sinful man. He and all his companions were astonished. But Jesus said to Simon not to be afraid; from now on he would fish for people. So they pulled their boats up on shore, left everything and followed Him. Peter’s call began with a miracle that showed him both who Jesus was and who he himself was, and Jesus’ answer to his unworthiness was not rejection but a mission."],"id":"story-the-great-catch-of-fish"},{"e":"g","t":"Through the roof","r":"Mark 2:1-12","p":"Jesus; A paralysed man; His four friends","s":"The house in Capernaum is so packed that four friends dig through the roof to lower their paralysed friend to Jesus. Jesus first forgives his sins, then tells him to pick up his mat and walk.","w":"Friends who carry each other to Jesus.","q":"Who do I need to carry to Jesus in prayer?","l":["A few days after Jesus returned to Capernaum, word spread that He was home. So many people gathered that there was no room left, not even outside the door, and He preached the word to them. Pharisees and teachers of the law from all over had come to listen.","Four men came carrying a paralysed man on a mat. Since they could not get him to Jesus because of the crowd, they climbed onto the flat roof, made an opening above Jesus by digging through it, and lowered the mat with the man lying on it. Imagine dust and bits of roof falling on the crowd while Jesus was teaching.","When Jesus saw their faith, the faith of the friends, He said to the paralysed man, “Son, your sins are forgiven.” Some teachers of the law sitting there thought to themselves that this was blasphemy: who can forgive sins but God alone? Jesus knew what they were thinking, and asked them which was easier: to say that someone’s sins are forgiven, or to tell him to get up and walk? Anyone can say the first, because no one can check. So to show that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins, He said to the man, “Get up, take your mat and go home.”","The man got up, took his mat and walked out in full view of them all. Everyone was amazed and praised God, saying they had never seen anything like this. The man needed healing, but Jesus dealt first with his deepest need, forgiveness. And none of it would have happened without four friends who refused to give up."],"id":"story-through-the-roof"},{"e":"g","t":"The centurion's faith","r":"Luke 7:1-10","p":"Jesus; A Roman centurion","s":"A Roman officer's valued servant is dying. He sends Jewish elders to ask Jesus for help, then sends friends to say he isn't worthy to have Jesus under his roof: 'Just say the word.' Jesus is amazed and says He hasn't found such great faith even in Israel. The servant is healed.","w":"Faith trusts the authority of Jesus' word.","q":"Do I believe Jesus' word is enough?","l":["When Jesus had finished teaching, He entered Capernaum. There was a Roman centurion there, an officer in charge of about a hundred soldiers, whose servant, whom he valued highly, was sick and about to die. The centurion heard about Jesus and sent some Jewish elders to ask Him to come and heal the servant.","The elders pleaded earnestly with Jesus, saying this man deserved it, because he loved their nation and had built their synagogue. So Jesus went with them. Romans were the occupying power, and most Jews resented them, yet this soldier had won the town’s respect.","Jesus was not far from the house when the centurion sent friends with another message. He told Jesus not to trouble Himself, because he did not deserve to have Jesus come under his roof; that was why he hadn’t even come himself. Just say the word, and my servant will be healed. He explained that he understood authority: he was under authority himself, with soldiers under him. When he told one to go, he went, and when he told another to come, he came.","When Jesus heard this, He was amazed at him. Turning to the crowd following Him, He said He had not found such great faith even in Israel. Then the men who had been sent returned to the house and found the servant well. Only twice do the Gospels say Jesus was amazed: once at the great faith of this Gentile soldier, and once at the unbelief in His own hometown."],"id":"story-the-centurion-s-faith"},{"e":"g","t":"The woman who anointed Jesus' feet","r":"Luke 7:36-50","p":"Jesus; Simon the Pharisee; A sinful woman","s":"At a Pharisee's dinner, a woman known for her sinful life weeps over Jesus' feet, wipes them with her hair and pours perfume on them. Simon thinks Jesus should know what kind of woman she is. Jesus tells a parable of two debtors and says her many sins are forgiven, because she loved much.","w":"Those who know they are forgiven much, love much.","q":"How much do I realise I've been forgiven?","l":["A Pharisee named Simon invited Jesus to dinner, so He went to his house and reclined at the table. Guests lay on couches with their feet stretched away from the table. A woman in that town who had lived a sinful life learned that Jesus was eating there, and she came with an alabaster jar of perfume.","She stood behind Him at His feet, weeping. Her tears fell on His feet, and she wiped them with her hair, kissed them and poured perfume on them. Simon thought to himself that if Jesus were really a prophet, He would know who was touching Him and what kind of woman she was.","Jesus answered his unspoken thought with a story. Two people owed money to a moneylender: one owed five hundred denarii, about two years’ wages, and the other fifty. Neither could pay, so he forgave both debts. Which would love him more? Simon said he supposed the one who had the bigger debt forgiven. Jesus said he had judged correctly. Then He turned toward the woman and pointed out that Simon had given Him no water for His feet, no kiss of greeting, and no oil for His head, the basic courtesies of a host. She had washed His feet with tears, kissed them and anointed them with perfume.","Jesus said that her many sins had been forgiven, as her great love showed. But whoever has been forgiven little loves little. Then He said to her, “Your sins are forgiven.” The other guests began asking among themselves who this was who even forgave sins. Jesus said to the woman, “Your faith has saved you; go in peace.” She walked out with a new name: not ‘that sinful woman’, but forgiven."],"id":"story-the-woman-who-anointed-jesus-feet"},{"e":"g","t":"Calming the storm","r":"Mark 4:35-41","p":"Jesus; The disciples","s":"A furious storm hits the boat while Jesus sleeps on a cushion. The terrified disciples wake Him. He says to the wind and waves 'Quiet! Be still!' and it is completely calm.","w":"The storm obeys Jesus, and He is in the boat with us.","q":"What storm do I need to hear Him speak 'be still' over?","l":["That day, when evening came, Jesus said to His disciples that they should go over to the other side of the lake. Leaving the crowd behind, they took Him along in the boat, just as He was, and there were other boats with Him.","A furious squall came up. The Sea of Galilee sits low between hills, and sudden storms can sweep down on it. The waves broke over the boat so that it was nearly swamped. Several of the disciples were experienced fishermen, and even they were terrified. Jesus was in the stern, sleeping on a cushion.","The disciples woke Him and asked whether He didn’t care if they drowned. He got up, rebuked the wind and said to the waves, “Quiet! Be still!” The wind died down and it was completely calm. He asked them why they were so afraid, and whether they still had no faith.","They were terrified, now of Him rather than the storm, and asked each other who this was, that even the wind and the waves obey Him. Psalm 107 describes sailors in a storm crying out to the Lord, who stills the storm to a whisper. In the boat that night, the disciples saw that the Lord of the psalm was asleep on a cushion beside them."],"id":"story-calming-the-storm"},{"e":"g","t":"The man called Legion","r":"Mark 5:1-20","p":"Jesus; Legion","s":"Across the lake, a man possessed by many demons lives among the tombs, breaking every chain. Jesus sends the demons into a herd of pigs, which rush into the lake. The townspeople find the man dressed and in his right mind, and beg Jesus to leave. The man wants to go with Jesus, but Jesus sends him home to tell his family.","w":"No one is too far gone for Jesus, and our story is our testimony.","q":"Who could I tell what the Lord has done for me?","l":["Jesus and His disciples crossed the lake to the region of the Gerasenes, Gentile territory. As soon as Jesus got out of the boat, a man with an impure spirit came from the tombs to meet Him. He lived among the burial caves, and no one could bind him any more, not even with a chain. He had often been chained hand and foot, but he tore the chains apart and broke the irons on his feet. Night and day he cried out and cut himself with stones.","When he saw Jesus from a distance, he ran and fell on his knees in front of Him, and shouted at the top of his voice, asking what Jesus wanted with him and begging Him not to torture him. Jesus asked his name. He replied, “My name is Legion, for we are many.” A Roman legion was about five thousand soldiers. The demons begged Jesus not to send them out of the area, but into a large herd of pigs feeding on the nearby hillside.","Jesus gave them permission, and the demons came out and went into the pigs. The herd, about two thousand in number, rushed down the steep bank into the lake and drowned. The herders ran off and reported this in the town and countryside, and people came out to see. When they found the man sitting there, dressed and in his right mind, they were afraid. Then they began to plead with Jesus to leave their region.","As Jesus was getting into the boat, the man who had been demon-possessed begged to go with Him. Jesus did not let him, but told him to go home to his own people and tell them how much the Lord had done for him and how He had mercy on him. So the man went away and began to tell in the Decapolis, ten Greek cities, how much Jesus had done for him. And all the people were amazed. The first missionary to that region was a man who had lived among the tombs."],"id":"story-the-man-called-legion"},{"e":"g","t":"Feeding the five thousand","r":"John 6:1-14","p":"Jesus; Philip; Andrew; A boy","s":"A huge crowd has nothing to eat. Andrew finds a boy with five small barley loaves and two fish. Jesus gives thanks and feeds everyone, with twelve baskets left over.","w":"Jesus multiplies what we give Him.","q":"What 'small lunch' could I put in Jesus' hands?","l":["Jesus crossed to the far shore of the Sea of Galilee, and a great crowd followed Him because they had seen His signs of healing. He went up on a mountainside and sat down with His disciples. The Passover festival was near.","When Jesus looked up and saw the great crowd coming, He asked Philip where they could buy bread for all these people to eat. He asked this only to test him, because He already knew what He was going to do. Philip calculated that more than half a year’s wages would not buy enough for each person to have one bite.","Andrew, Simon Peter’s brother, said there was a boy with five small barley loaves and two small fish, but asked what that was among so many. Barley loaves were the bread of the poor. Jesus told the disciples to have the people sit down. There was plenty of grass, and about five thousand men sat down, besides women and children. Jesus took the loaves, gave thanks, and distributed them to those seated, as much as they wanted. He did the same with the fish.","When they had all had enough to eat, He told His disciples to gather the pieces that were left over so that nothing would be wasted. They filled twelve baskets with the leftovers from the five barley loaves. The people began to say that surely this was the Prophet who was to come into the world, and wanted to make Him king by force, so Jesus withdrew again to a mountain by Himself. The next day He told them that He Himself is the bread of life, and whoever comes to Him will never go hungry."],"id":"story-feeding-the-five-thousand"},{"e":"g","t":"Walking on water","r":"Matthew 14:22-33","p":"Jesus; Peter","s":"In the night Jesus walks to the disciples across the lake. Peter asks to come to Him and walks on the water, until he looks at the wind and starts to sink. Jesus immediately reaches out and catches him.","w":"Keep your eyes on Jesus, and He catches you when you sink.","q":"What am I looking at instead of Jesus?","l":["Immediately after feeding the five thousand, Jesus made the disciples get into the boat and go ahead of Him to the other side, while He dismissed the crowd. After He had dismissed them, He went up on a mountainside by Himself to pray. Later that night, He was there alone.","The boat was already a considerable distance from land, buffeted by the waves because the wind was against it. Shortly before dawn, Jesus went out to them, walking on the lake. When the disciples saw Him walking on the water, they were terrified and cried out in fear that it was a ghost. But Jesus immediately told them to take courage: it was He; they should not be afraid.","Peter replied that if it was really the Lord, He should tell him to come to Him on the water. Jesus said, “Come.” Peter got down out of the boat, walked on the water and came toward Jesus. But when he saw the wind, he was afraid and, beginning to sink, cried out, “Lord, save me!” Immediately Jesus reached out His hand and caught him. He asked Peter why he doubted, calling him a man of little faith.","When they climbed into the boat, the wind died down. Then those in the boat worshipped Him, saying that truly He was the Son of God. Peter is often remembered for sinking, but he was the only one who got out of the boat. And when he sank, the hand of Jesus was right there."],"id":"story-walking-on-water"},{"e":"g","t":"Jairus and the woman who touched His cloak","r":"Mark 5:21-43","p":"Jesus; Jairus; The bleeding woman","s":"On the way to Jairus's dying daughter, a woman bleeding for twelve years touches Jesus' cloak and is healed. Jesus stops to call her 'daughter'. Then news comes that the girl has died, but Jesus says 'Don't be afraid; just believe', and raises her.","w":"No one is an interruption to Jesus.","q":"Do I believe Jesus has time for me?","l":["When Jesus crossed back over the lake, a large crowd gathered. Jairus, one of the leaders of the local synagogue, fell at His feet and pleaded with Him: his little daughter was dying. He begged Jesus to come and put His hands on her so she would be healed and live. Jesus went with him, and a large crowd followed and pressed around Him.","In the crowd was a woman who had been bleeding for twelve years. She had suffered a great deal under many doctors and spent everything she had, yet instead of getting better she grew worse. Her condition also made her ritually unclean, cut off from worship and community. She thought that if she could just touch His clothes, she would be healed. She came up behind Him and touched His cloak, and immediately the bleeding stopped and she felt in her body that she was freed from her suffering.","At once Jesus realised that power had gone out from Him. He turned around and asked who had touched His clothes. His disciples thought it was a strange question with the crowd pushing against Him from every side. But Jesus kept looking around. The woman, trembling with fear, came and fell at His feet and told Him the whole truth. He said to her, “Daughter, your faith has healed you. Go in peace and be freed from your suffering.” She went from hidden and unclean to publicly restored and called ‘daughter’.","While Jesus was still speaking, people came from Jairus’ house: his daughter was dead, so why bother the teacher any more? Overhearing, Jesus told Jairus not to be afraid, but just believe. He took only Peter, James and John, and at the house found people crying and wailing loudly. When He said the child was not dead but asleep, they laughed at Him. He put them all out, took the girl’s parents and His three disciples in, took her by the hand and said, “Talitha koum!” which means “Little girl, I say to you, get up!” Immediately the twelve-year-old stood up and began to walk around, and they were completely astonished. Jesus told them to give her something to eat."],"id":"story-jairus-and-the-woman-who-touched-his-cloak"},{"e":"g","t":"The transfiguration","r":"Matthew 17:1-13","p":"Jesus; Peter; James; John; Moses; Elijah","s":"Jesus takes Peter, James and John up a high mountain, where His face shines like the sun and His clothes become white as light. Moses and Elijah appear, talking with Him. Peter offers to build shelters, and a bright cloud covers them as the Father says, 'This is my Son. Listen to him!'","w":"Jesus is greater than the Law and the Prophets; listen to Him.","q":"What is Jesus saying that I need to listen to?","l":["About a week after Peter declared that Jesus is the Messiah, and Jesus began telling them that He must suffer and die, Jesus took Peter, James and John up a high mountain by themselves. Luke says He went there to pray.","There He was transfigured before them. His face shone like the sun, and His clothes became as white as the light. Just then Moses and Elijah appeared before them, talking with Jesus. Luke adds that they spoke about His departure, literally His ‘exodus’, which He was about to bring to fulfilment at Jerusalem. Moses represented the Law and Elijah the Prophets, and both pointed to Jesus.","Peter, not knowing what to say, offered to put up three shelters, one for Jesus, one for Moses and one for Elijah. While he was still speaking, a bright cloud covered them, and a voice from the cloud said that this was His Son, whom He loves and with whom He is well pleased, and that they should listen to Him. When the disciples heard this, they fell face down to the ground, terrified.","Jesus came and touched them, and told them to get up and not be afraid. When they looked up, they saw no one except Jesus. As they came down the mountain, Jesus told them not to tell anyone what they had seen until the Son of Man had been raised from the dead. Years later Peter wrote that they had been eyewitnesses of His majesty on the sacred mountain. The glory they saw was a glimpse of who Jesus always is, given to strengthen them for the cross that was coming."],"id":"story-the-transfiguration"},{"e":"g","t":"The good Samaritan","r":"Luke 10:25-37","p":"Jesus; An expert in the law; The Samaritan","s":"A lawyer asks who his neighbour is. Jesus tells of a man beaten by robbers on the road to Jericho. A priest and a Levite pass by on the other side. A Samaritan, from a despised people, stops, bandages his wounds, takes him to an inn and pays for his care. 'Go and do likewise.'","w":"Love doesn't ask who qualifies; it becomes a neighbour.","q":"Who is the person on my road this week?","l":["An expert in the law stood up to test Jesus, asking what he must do to inherit eternal life. Jesus asked what was written in the Law. The man answered well: love the Lord your God with all your heart, soul, strength and mind, and love your neighbour as yourself. Jesus told him he had answered correctly; do this and he would live. But the man wanted to justify himself, so he asked, “And who is my neighbour?”","Jesus replied with a story. A man was going down the steep, lonely road from Jerusalem to Jericho, when he was attacked by robbers. They stripped him of his clothes, beat him and went away, leaving him half dead. A priest happened to be going down the same road, and when he saw the man, he passed by on the other side. So too a Levite, a temple assistant, came to the place, saw him and passed by on the other side.","But a Samaritan, as he travelled, came where the man was, and when he saw him, he took pity on him. To Jesus’ listeners the Samaritan was the last person they would expect to be the hero. He went to him and bandaged his wounds, pouring on oil and wine. Then he put the man on his own donkey, brought him to an inn and took care of him. The next day he gave the innkeeper two silver coins, about two days’ wages, and promised to repay any extra expense when he returned.","Jesus asked which of the three had been a neighbour to the man who fell into the hands of robbers. The expert could not even bring himself to say ‘the Samaritan’; he answered, “The one who had mercy on him.” Jesus told him, “Go and do likewise.” The lawyer asked who qualifies as his neighbour. Jesus turned the question around: the real question is whether I will be a neighbour to whoever is in need."],"id":"story-the-good-samaritan"},{"e":"g","t":"The lost sheep and the lost coin","r":"Luke 15:1-10","p":"Jesus; The Pharisees","s":"Religious leaders grumble that Jesus welcomes sinners. He tells of a shepherd who leaves ninety-nine sheep to find one that is lost, and a woman who sweeps the whole house for one lost coin. Both call their friends to celebrate. There is joy in heaven over one sinner who repents.","w":"God searches for the lost and throws a party when they are found.","q":"Who is the 'one' God has put on my heart?","l":["Tax collectors and sinners were all gathering around to hear Jesus. The Pharisees and teachers of the law muttered that this man welcomes sinners and eats with them. So Jesus told them three stories in a row, all about something lost and found.","Suppose one of you has a hundred sheep and loses one of them. Doesn’t he leave the ninety-nine in the open country and go after the lost sheep until he finds it? When he finds it, he joyfully puts it on his shoulders and goes home. Then he calls his friends and neighbours together and asks them to rejoice with him, because he has found his lost sheep. Jesus said there will be more rejoicing in heaven over one sinner who repents than over ninety-nine righteous people who do not need to repent.","Or suppose a woman has ten silver coins and loses one. Each coin was worth about a day’s wages. Doesn’t she light a lamp, sweep the house and search carefully until she finds it? When she finds it, she calls her friends and neighbours together to celebrate. In the same way, Jesus said, there is rejoicing in the presence of the angels of God over one sinner who repents. The third story, the lost son, follows straight after. In each one, God is the one who searches, and the story ends in a party."],"id":"story-the-lost-sheep-and-the-lost-coin"},{"e":"g","t":"The prodigal son","r":"Luke 15:11-32","p":"The father; The younger son; The older brother","s":"A younger son demands his inheritance and wastes it, ending up feeding pigs. He comes home to beg to be a servant, but his father runs to him, hugs him and throws a feast. The older brother refuses to celebrate.","w":"The Father runs to meet returning children, whichever brother we are.","q":"Am I more like the younger or the older brother right now?","l":["A man had two sons. The younger one said to his father, “Give me my share of the estate.” In that culture it was like saying he wished his father were dead. The father divided his property between them. Not long after, the younger son got together all he had, set off for a distant country and squandered his wealth in wild living. After he had spent everything, a severe famine struck, and he began to be in need. He hired himself out to a local man who sent him to feed pigs, the most shameful job imaginable for a Jew. He longed to eat the pigs’ food, but no one gave him anything.","When he came to his senses, he realised his father’s hired servants had food to spare while he was starving. He decided to go back and say that he had sinned against heaven and against his father, was no longer worthy to be called his son, and should be made like one of the hired servants. He got up and went. But while he was still a long way off, his father saw him and was filled with compassion. He ran to his son, threw his arms around him and kissed him. Respectable older men in that culture did not run; the father ran anyway. The son started his speech, but the father interrupted, calling for the best robe, a ring for his finger and sandals for his feet, and the fattened calf for a feast. This son of his was dead and is alive again; he was lost and is found.","Meanwhile the older son was in the field. When he heard music and dancing and found out why, he became angry and refused to go in. His father came out and pleaded with him. The son said he had slaved for his father all these years and never disobeyed, yet never got even a young goat to celebrate with his friends. But when this son of yours, who wasted your property, comes home, you kill the fattened calf! The father said, “My son, you are always with me, and everything I have is yours. But we had to celebrate and be glad.” The story ends without telling us whether the older brother went in. Jesus told it to religious people who were grumbling about Him eating with sinners."],"id":"story-the-prodigal-son"},{"e":"g","t":"Mary and Martha","r":"Luke 10:38-42","p":"Jesus; Mary; Martha","s":"Martha is busy with all the preparations while her sister Mary sits at Jesus' feet listening. Martha complains, and Jesus gently says Mary has chosen what is better.","w":"Being with Jesus matters more than being busy for Him.","q":"Where am I being Martha when I could be Mary?","l":["As Jesus and His disciples travelled, they came to a village where a woman named Martha opened her home to Him. Hosting Jesus and His disciples meant a lot of cooking and serving. Martha had a sister called Mary, who sat at the Lord’s feet listening to what He said. Sitting at a teacher’s feet was the place of a disciple, normally taken by men.","Martha was distracted by all the preparations that had to be made. She came to Jesus and asked whether He didn’t care that her sister had left her to do the work by herself. Tell her to help me!","Jesus answered her gently, saying her name twice: “Martha, Martha.” He told her she was worried and upset about many things, but few things are needed, or indeed only one. Mary had chosen what was better, and it would not be taken away from her. Jesus did not scold Martha for serving. He invited her to the one thing her busyness was crowding out: simply being with Him. Later, at Lazarus’s tomb, it is Martha who makes one of the clearest confessions of faith in the Gospels."],"id":"story-mary-and-martha"},{"e":"g","t":"The ten lepers","r":"Luke 17:11-19","p":"Jesus; A Samaritan","s":"Ten men with leprosy call to Jesus from a distance. He tells them to show themselves to the priests, and on the way they are healed. Only one comes back, praising God loudly and throwing himself at Jesus' feet, and he is a Samaritan. 'Were not all ten cleansed? Where are the other nine?'","w":"Gratitude turns back to Jesus.","q":"What have I received from God that I haven't thanked Him for?","l":["On His way to Jerusalem, Jesus travelled along the border between Samaria and Galilee. As He was going into a village, ten men who had leprosy met Him. They stood at a distance, as the law required of people with skin diseases, who had to live outside the community. They called out in a loud voice, “Jesus, Master, have pity on us!”","When He saw them, He told them to go and show themselves to the priests, who were the ones who could declare someone clean and allowed back into society. They had to set off still diseased, trusting His word. And as they went, they were cleansed.","One of them, when he saw he was healed, came back, praising God in a loud voice. He threw himself at Jesus’ feet and thanked Him, and he was a Samaritan. Jesus asked whether all ten had not been cleansed. Where were the other nine? Was no one found to return and give praise to God except this foreigner? Then He told him to rise and go, because his faith had made him well. All ten were healed in body; the one who came back received something more, a personal encounter with Jesus."],"id":"story-the-ten-lepers"},{"e":"g","t":"The rich young man","r":"Mark 10:17-27","p":"Jesus; A rich man","s":"A man runs up and kneels before Jesus, asking what he must do to inherit eternal life. He says he has kept the commandments since boyhood. Jesus looks at him and loves him, and tells him to sell everything, give to the poor and follow. The man goes away sad, because he has great wealth.","w":"Jesus asks for the one thing we hold tightest.","q":"What would be hardest for me to let go of for Jesus?","l":["As Jesus started on His way, a man ran up to Him and fell on his knees before Him. Luke calls him a ruler, and Matthew says he was young. He asked, “Good teacher, what must I do to inherit eternal life?” Jesus asked why he called Him good, since no one is good except God alone. Then He listed commandments: do not murder, do not commit adultery, do not steal, do not give false testimony, do not defraud, honour your father and mother.","The man declared that he had kept all these since he was a boy. Mark adds a tender detail: Jesus looked at him and loved him. Then He said one thing was lacking. Go, sell everything you have and give to the poor, and you will have treasure in heaven. Then come, follow Me. At this the man’s face fell. He went away sad, because he had great wealth. Jesus didn’t chase after him or lower the cost.","Jesus looked around and told His disciples how hard it is for the rich to enter the kingdom of God. They were amazed, because wealth was seen as a sign of God’s blessing. He said it is easier for a camel to go through the eye of a needle than for someone who is rich to enter the kingdom of God. The disciples were even more amazed and asked who then could be saved. Jesus looked at them and said that with man this is impossible, but not with God; all things are possible with God."],"id":"story-the-rich-young-man"},{"e":"g","t":"Zacchaeus","r":"Luke 19:1-10","p":"Jesus; Zacchaeus","s":"Zacchaeus, a rich chief tax collector in Jericho, is too short to see Jesus, so he climbs a sycamore-fig tree. Jesus calls him by name and invites Himself to his house. Zacchaeus gives half his goods to the poor and repays anyone he cheated four times over.","w":"The Son of Man came to seek and save the lost.","q":"What changes when Jesus calls me by name?","l":["Jesus entered Jericho and was passing through. A man there by the name of Zacchaeus was a chief tax collector, and he was wealthy. Tax collectors worked for Rome and were known for collecting more than they should and keeping the difference. A chief tax collector was the most hated man in town.","He wanted to see who Jesus was, but because he was short he could not see over the crowd. So he ran ahead and climbed a sycamore-fig tree to see Him, since Jesus was coming that way. When Jesus reached the spot, He looked up, called him by name and told him to come down immediately, because He must stay at his house today. Zacchaeus came down at once and welcomed Him gladly. All the people began to mutter that Jesus had gone to be the guest of a sinner.","Zacchaeus stood up and said to the Lord that here and now he would give half of his possessions to the poor, and if he had cheated anybody out of anything, he would pay back four times the amount. The law only required paying back the amount plus a fifth. Jesus said that today salvation had come to this house, because this man too was a son of Abraham. For the Son of Man came to seek and to save the lost. Jesus didn’t demand that Zacchaeus change before He came to his house; His welcome is what changed him."],"id":"story-zacchaeus"},{"e":"g","t":"Lazarus raised","r":"John 11","p":"Jesus; Martha; Mary; Lazarus","s":"Jesus' friend Lazarus dies, and Jesus arrives four days later. He tells Martha 'I am the resurrection and the life'. Seeing Mary weep, Jesus weeps too. Then He calls 'Lazarus, come out!' and Lazarus walks out of the tomb.","w":"Jesus grieves with us and has power over death.","q":"Where do I need Jesus to weep with me and also bring life?","l":["Lazarus lived in Bethany, near Jerusalem, with his sisters Mary and Martha, and Jesus loved all three of them. When Lazarus fell ill, the sisters sent word to Jesus: the one He loved was sick. Jesus said this sickness would not end in death, but was for God’s glory. Yet, strangely, John says that because Jesus loved them, He stayed where He was two more days. Then He told the disciples they were going back to Judea, even though people there had recently tried to stone Him. Thomas said, “Let us also go, that we may die with him.”","When Jesus arrived, Lazarus had already been in the tomb four days. Martha went out to meet Him and said that if He had been there, her brother would not have died. Jesus told her that her brother would rise again. She said she knew he would rise at the resurrection on the last day. Jesus said, “I am the resurrection and the life. The one who believes in me will live, even though they die.” He asked if she believed this, and she said she believed He was the Messiah, the Son of God. Then Mary came and fell at His feet, saying the same words, weeping. When Jesus saw her weeping, and the Jews who had come with her weeping too, He was deeply moved and troubled. Jesus wept. The people said, “See how he loved him!”","At the tomb, a cave with a stone across the entrance, Jesus told them to take away the stone. Practical Martha warned that by this time there would be a bad smell. Jesus reminded her that if she believed, she would see the glory of God. They took away the stone. Jesus looked up and thanked the Father for hearing Him, then called in a loud voice, “Lazarus, come out!” The dead man came out, his hands and feet wrapped with strips of linen and a cloth around his face. Jesus told them to take off the grave clothes and let him go. Many who saw it believed, but from that day the religious leaders plotted to kill Jesus. Raising Lazarus led directly to the cross."],"id":"story-lazarus-raised"},{"e":"g","t":"Blind Bartimaeus","r":"Mark 10:46-52","p":"Jesus; Bartimaeus","s":"Blind Bartimaeus shouts 'Son of David, have mercy on me!' outside Jericho, and people tell him to be quiet. He shouts louder. Jesus calls him and asks 'What do you want me to do for you?' He receives his sight and follows Jesus.","w":"Persistent faith, and a Jesus who asks what we want.","q":"If Jesus asked me today what I want, what would I say?","l":["Jesus and His disciples came to Jericho, on the way up to Jerusalem for the last time. As they were leaving the city with a large crowd, a blind man, Bartimaeus son of Timaeus, was sitting by the roadside begging. When he heard that it was Jesus of Nazareth passing by, he began to shout, “Jesus, Son of David, have mercy on me!” Son of David was a title for the Messiah.","Many rebuked him and told him to be quiet, but he shouted all the more. Jesus stopped and told them to call him. So they called to the blind man, telling him to cheer up and get on his feet, because Jesus was calling him. Throwing his cloak aside, probably his most valuable possession and the place he collected coins, he jumped to his feet and came to Jesus.","Jesus asked him, “What do you want me to do for you?” It was the same question He had just asked James and John, who wanted the best seats in His kingdom. The blind man said, “Rabbi, I want to see.” Jesus told him to go, because his faith had healed him. Immediately he received his sight and followed Jesus along the road, the road that led to Jerusalem and the cross."],"id":"story-blind-bartimaeus"},{"e":"g","t":"The triumphal entry","r":"Luke 19:28-44","p":"Jesus; The disciples","s":"Jesus sends two disciples for a colt no one has ridden. He rides into Jerusalem as crowds spread their cloaks on the road and praise God: 'Blessed is the king who comes in the name of the Lord!' When Pharisees tell Him to quiet them, He says the stones would cry out. Then, seeing the city, He weeps over it.","w":"The King comes humbly, and weeps over those who miss Him.","q":"Is Jesus welcomed as King in my life?","l":["As Jesus approached Bethphage and Bethany at the Mount of Olives, He sent two disciples ahead to the village. They would find a colt tied there that no one had ever ridden. If anyone asked why they were untying it, they should say the Lord needed it. They found it just as He had told them. Zechariah had prophesied that Jerusalem’s king would come to her righteous and victorious, yet humble and riding on a donkey, on a colt.","They threw their cloaks on the colt and put Jesus on it. As He went along, people spread their cloaks on the road, the way you would welcome a king, and John adds that they waved palm branches. When He came near the place where the road goes down the Mount of Olives, the whole crowd of disciples began joyfully to praise God in loud voices for all the miracles they had seen, calling out blessings on the king who comes in the name of the Lord, and peace in heaven and glory in the highest.","Some of the Pharisees in the crowd told Jesus to rebuke His disciples. He replied that if they kept quiet, the stones would cry out. As He approached Jerusalem and saw the city, He wept over it. He said that if they had only known on this day what would bring them peace, but now it was hidden from their eyes. He foretold that enemies would surround the city and not leave one stone on another, because they did not recognise the time of God’s coming to them. Within the week, some in the same crowds would shout for His crucifixion. About forty years later, in AD 70, Roman armies destroyed Jerusalem."],"id":"story-the-triumphal-entry"},{"e":"g","t":"The widow's two coins","r":"Mark 12:41-44","p":"Jesus; A poor widow","s":"Jesus sits opposite the temple treasury, watching people give. Many rich people throw in large amounts. A poor widow puts in two very small copper coins. Jesus tells His disciples she has given more than all the others, because she gave all she had to live on.","w":"God measures the heart and the cost, not the amount.","q":"What does my giving say about my trust in God?","l":["In the last week before the cross, Jesus was teaching in the temple courts. He warned the crowds about teachers of the law who liked to walk around in flowing robes, be greeted with respect in the marketplaces, and have the most important seats. They devoured widows’ houses and, for a show, made lengthy prayers. Such men, He said, would be punished most severely.","Then Jesus sat down opposite the place where the offerings were put and watched the crowd putting their money into the temple treasury. Many rich people threw in large amounts. But a poor widow came and put in two very small copper coins, the smallest coins in circulation, worth only a fraction of a day’s wage.","Calling His disciples to Him, Jesus said that this poor widow had put more into the treasury than all the others. They all gave out of their wealth; but she, out of her poverty, put in everything, all she had to live on. Jesus measured not the size of the gift, but how much was left over afterwards and how much trust it took. She gave her last coins, trusting God to provide."],"id":"story-the-widow-s-two-coins"},{"e":"g","t":"Jesus washes the disciples' feet","r":"John 13:1-17","p":"Jesus; Peter; The disciples","s":"During the evening meal, Jesus takes off His outer clothes, wraps a towel around His waist and washes His disciples' feet, the job of the lowest servant. Peter refuses at first, then asks to be washed all over. Jesus says He has set an example: they should wash one another's feet.","w":"Greatness in God's kingdom looks like serving.","q":"Whose 'feet' could I wash this week?","l":["It was just before the Passover festival. Jesus knew that the hour had come for Him to leave this world and go to the Father. John says that having loved His own who were in the world, He loved them to the end. The evening meal was in progress, and the devil had already prompted Judas Iscariot to betray Him.","Jesus knew that the Father had put all things under His power, and that He had come from God and was returning to God. Knowing all that, He got up from the meal, took off His outer clothing and wrapped a towel around His waist. Then He poured water into a basin and began to wash His disciples’ feet, drying them with the towel. Roads were dusty and people wore sandals, so foot washing was a job for the lowest household servant. None of the disciples had offered to do it; Luke says they were arguing about which of them was the greatest.","When He came to Simon Peter, Peter protested that Jesus would never wash his feet. Jesus answered that unless He washed him, Peter had no part with Him. Then Peter swung the other way and asked Him to wash his hands and head as well. Jesus said those who have had a bath only need to wash their feet; they were clean, though not every one of them, because He knew who was going to betray Him. He even washed Judas’s feet. When He had finished, He put His clothes back on and asked if they understood what He had done. They called Him Teacher and Lord, and rightly so. Now that He, their Lord and Teacher, had washed their feet, they also should wash one another’s feet. He had set them an example, and they would be blessed if they did these things."],"id":"story-jesus-washes-the-disciples-feet"},{"e":"g","t":"The Last Supper","r":"Luke 22:7-23","p":"Jesus; The twelve disciples","s":"At the Passover meal Jesus takes bread, saying 'This is my body given for you', and the cup, 'the new covenant in my blood'. He tells them to do this in remembrance of Him.","w":"Communion remembers what His death bought for us.","q":"How do I prepare my heart for Communion?","l":["The day of Unleavened Bread came, when the Passover lamb had to be sacrificed. Jesus sent Peter and John to prepare the meal, telling them they would meet a man carrying a jar of water, who would lead them to a house with a large upstairs room. They found things just as He had told them.","When the hour came, Jesus and His apostles reclined at the table. He told them He had eagerly desired to eat this Passover with them before He suffered. At Passover, Jewish families remembered how God rescued them from Egypt by the blood of the lamb. Jesus took bread, gave thanks, broke it and gave it to them, saying that this was His body given for them, and that they should do this in remembrance of Him. In the same way, after the supper He took the cup, saying that this cup was the new covenant in His blood, poured out for them. Jeremiah had promised a new covenant in which God would forgive sins and write His law on hearts.","Then Jesus said that the hand of the one who would betray Him was with Him on the table. The Son of Man would go as it had been decreed, but woe to the man who betrayed Him. The disciples began to question among themselves which of them it might be. Soon after, they argued about which of them was the greatest. Jesus told them that among His followers the greatest should be like the youngest, and the leader like the one who serves, because He was among them as one who serves. Every time the church shares Communion, we remember His body broken and His blood poured out for us."],"id":"story-the-last-supper"},{"e":"g","t":"Gethsemane and the arrest","r":"Luke 22:39-53","p":"Jesus; Judas; Peter","s":"Jesus prays in anguish, 'not my will, but yours'. Judas arrives with a crowd and betrays Him with a kiss. One disciple cuts off a servant's ear, and Jesus heals it.","w":"Jesus chose the Father's will, and showed mercy even in His arrest.","q":"Where do I need to pray 'not my will'?","l":["After the meal, Jesus went out as usual to the Mount of Olives, and His disciples followed. At the place called Gethsemane, which means ‘olive press’, He told them to pray that they would not fall into temptation. He withdrew about a stone’s throw beyond them, knelt down and prayed, asking the Father, if He was willing, to take this cup from Him; yet not His will, but the Father’s be done.","An angel from heaven appeared to Him and strengthened Him. Being in anguish, He prayed more earnestly, and Luke, the doctor, records that His sweat was like drops of blood falling to the ground. When He rose from prayer and went back to the disciples, He found them asleep, exhausted from sorrow. He asked why they were sleeping and told them to get up and pray so that they would not fall into temptation.","While He was still speaking, a crowd came up, led by Judas, one of the Twelve. He approached Jesus to kiss Him, the agreed signal. Jesus asked, “Judas, are you betraying the Son of Man with a kiss?” When the disciples saw what was going to happen, one of them, John tells us it was Peter, struck the high priest’s servant and cut off his right ear. Jesus said, “No more of this!” and He touched the man’s ear and healed him. Then He told the chief priests and officers that this was their hour, when darkness reigns. His last miracle before the cross was healing an enemy who had come to arrest Him."],"id":"story-gethsemane-and-the-arrest"},{"e":"g","t":"Peter's denial","r":"Luke 22:54-62","p":"Peter; Jesus","s":"Peter follows at a distance into the high priest's courtyard. Three times people say he was with Jesus, and three times he denies it. The rooster crows, Jesus turns and looks at Peter, and Peter goes out and weeps bitterly.","w":"Even the boldest fail, and Jesus' look is not the end of the story.","q":"Where have I been following Jesus at a distance?","l":["Earlier that evening Peter had insisted he was ready to go with Jesus to prison and to death. Jesus told him that before the rooster crowed that day, Peter would deny three times that he knew Him. Jesus also said He had prayed for Peter, that his faith would not fail, and that when he had turned back he should strengthen his brothers.","After the arrest, they led Jesus to the house of the high priest. Peter followed at a distance. When some there had kindled a fire in the middle of the courtyard and sat down together, Peter sat down with them. A servant girl saw him seated in the firelight, looked closely at him and said this man had been with Him. Peter denied it, saying, “Woman, I don’t know him.” A little later someone else saw him and said he was one of them. “Man, I am not!” Peter replied. About an hour later another asserted that certainly this fellow was with Him, because he was a Galilean, recognisable by his accent. Peter replied, “Man, I don’t know what you’re talking about!”","Just as he was speaking, the rooster crowed. The Lord turned and looked straight at Peter. Then Peter remembered the word the Lord had spoken to him. He went outside and wept bitterly. Peter’s failure was real and public, but Jesus had already prayed for him and already spoken of his turning back. After the resurrection, Jesus would restore him by another charcoal fire."],"id":"story-peter-s-denial"},{"e":"g","t":"The crucifixion","r":"Luke 23:26-49","p":"Jesus; Simon of Cyrene; The two criminals; The centurion","s":"Simon of Cyrene carries the cross. On it Jesus prays 'Father, forgive them', promises a dying criminal 'Today you will be with me in paradise', and commits His spirit to the Father. The temple curtain tears, and a centurion praises God.","w":"The heart of the whole Bible: Jesus dies in our place.","q":"What do I want to say to Jesus about the cross today?","l":["After Jesus had been tried before the high priest, Pilate and Herod, and the crowd had chosen to release the murderer Barabbas instead, Pilate handed Jesus over. As the soldiers led Him away, they seized Simon from Cyrene, in North Africa, who was on his way in from the country, and made him carry the cross behind Jesus. A large number of people followed, including women who mourned and wailed for Him. Two criminals were also led out with Him to be executed.","When they came to the place called the Skull, they crucified Him there, with the criminals, one on His right and the other on His left. Jesus said, “Father, forgive them, for they do not know what they are doing.” The soldiers divided up His clothes by casting lots. The rulers sneered that He saved others, so let Him save Himself if He is God’s Messiah. The soldiers mocked Him too, and a sign above Him read: this is the King of the Jews. One of the criminals hurled insults at Him. But the other rebuked him, saying they were being punished justly, but this man had done nothing wrong. Then he said, “Jesus, remember me when you come into your kingdom.” Jesus answered that truly, today he would be with Him in paradise.","It was now about noon, and darkness came over the whole land until three in the afternoon, for the sun stopped shining. The curtain of the temple, which separated the Most Holy Place from everyone else, was torn in two. Jesus called out with a loud voice, “Father, into your hands I commit my spirit.” When He had said this, He breathed His last. The centurion, seeing what had happened, praised God and said, “Surely this was a righteous man.” Joseph of Arimathea, a member of the council who had not agreed with their decision, asked Pilate for the body, wrapped it in linen and laid it in a new tomb cut in the rock. The women who had followed Jesus from Galilee saw where He was laid, then went home to prepare spices, and rested on the Sabbath."],"id":"story-the-crucifixion"},{"e":"g","t":"The empty tomb","r":"John 20:1-18","p":"Mary Magdalene; Peter; John; Jesus","s":"Mary Magdalene finds the tomb empty and weeps outside. She thinks the man standing there is the gardener, until He says her name, 'Mary'. She runs to tell the disciples 'I have seen the Lord!'","w":"He is risen, and He knows us by name.","q":"How does the resurrection change my week?","l":["Early on the first day of the week, while it was still dark, Mary Magdalene went to the tomb and saw that the stone had been removed from the entrance. She ran to Simon Peter and the other disciple, the one Jesus loved, and told them that they had taken the Lord out of the tomb and she didn’t know where they had put Him.","Peter and the other disciple ran to the tomb. The other disciple outran Peter and reached it first. He bent over and looked in at the strips of linen lying there, but did not go in. Then Simon Peter came along behind him and went straight into the tomb. He saw the linen lying there, and the cloth that had been around Jesus’ head folded up by itself, separate from the linen. Grave robbers would not have unwrapped the body and tidied up. Then the other disciple went inside too; he saw and believed. They still did not understand from Scripture that Jesus had to rise from the dead, and they went back home.","Mary stood outside the tomb crying. As she wept, she bent over to look in and saw two angels in white, seated where Jesus’ body had been. They asked why she was crying, and she said they had taken her Lord away. Then she turned around and saw Jesus standing there, but she did not realise it was Him. He asked why she was crying and who she was looking for. Thinking He was the gardener, she asked Him to tell her where He had put the body. Jesus said to her, “Mary.” She turned toward Him and cried out in Aramaic, “Rabboni!” which means Teacher. Jesus told her not to hold on to Him, but to go to His brothers and tell them He was returning to His Father and their Father, to His God and their God. Mary Magdalene went to the disciples with the news: “I have seen the Lord!” The first witness of the resurrection was a woman whose testimony would not even have counted in a court of the time."],"id":"story-the-empty-tomb"},{"e":"g","t":"The road to Emmaus","r":"Luke 24:13-35","p":"Jesus; Cleopas","s":"Two discouraged disciples walk to Emmaus, and the risen Jesus joins them, unrecognised. He explains everything the Scriptures say about Him. When He breaks bread they recognise Him, and say 'Were not our hearts burning within us?'","w":"Jesus opens the Scriptures, and they all point to Him.","q":"Has my heart burned while reading God's Word lately?","l":["That same day two of Jesus’ followers were going to a village called Emmaus, about eleven kilometres from Jerusalem. They were talking with each other about everything that had happened. As they talked and discussed these things, Jesus Himself came up and walked along with them, but they were kept from recognising Him.","He asked what they were discussing. They stood still, their faces downcast. One of them, named Cleopas, asked if He was the only visitor to Jerusalem who didn’t know what had happened there. They told Him about Jesus of Nazareth, a prophet powerful in word and deed, how the chief priests and rulers had handed Him over to be crucified. They said sadly, “We had hoped that he was the one who was going to redeem Israel.” Some women had gone to the tomb that morning, found it empty and reported seeing angels who said He was alive. Jesus said they were foolish and slow to believe all that the prophets had spoken. Did not the Messiah have to suffer these things and then enter His glory? Beginning with Moses and all the Prophets, He explained to them what was said in all the Scriptures concerning Himself.","As they approached the village, Jesus continued on as if He were going farther. But they urged Him strongly to stay with them, because it was nearly evening. When He was at the table with them, He took bread, gave thanks, broke it and began to give it to them. Then their eyes were opened and they recognised Him, and He disappeared from their sight. They asked each other whether their hearts had not been burning within them while He talked with them on the road and opened the Scriptures to them. They got up at once and walked all the way back to Jerusalem that night, found the Eleven and told them what had happened on the way, and how Jesus was recognised by them when He broke the bread."],"id":"story-the-road-to-emmaus"},{"e":"g","t":"Thomas believes","r":"John 20:24-29","p":"Thomas; Jesus","s":"Thomas wasn't there when the risen Jesus appeared, and says he won't believe unless he touches the nail marks. A week later Jesus appears again and invites him to touch His wounds. Thomas answers, 'My Lord and my God!' Jesus blesses those who have not seen and yet believe.","w":"Jesus meets honest doubt; and we are the blessed ones who believe without seeing.","q":"What doubt can I bring honestly to Jesus?","l":["On the evening of that first day of the week, the disciples were together with the doors locked for fear of the Jewish leaders. Jesus came and stood among them and said, “Peace be with you!” He showed them His hands and side, and the disciples were overjoyed when they saw the Lord. He breathed on them and said, “Receive the Holy Spirit.”","But Thomas, one of the Twelve, also called Didymus or ‘the twin’, was not with them when Jesus came. When the other disciples told him they had seen the Lord, he said that unless he saw the nail marks in His hands and put his finger where the nails were, and put his hand into His side, he would not believe. This is why he is often called ‘doubting Thomas’, though earlier he was the one ready to go and die with Jesus.","A week later His disciples were in the house again, and Thomas was with them. Though the doors were locked, Jesus came and stood among them and said, “Peace be with you!” Then He spoke straight to Thomas, offering exactly what Thomas had asked for: put your finger here, see My hands, reach out your hand and put it into My side. Stop doubting and believe. Thomas said to Him, “My Lord and my God!” It is the strongest statement of who Jesus is in all the Gospels. Then Jesus told him that because he had seen Him, he believed; blessed are those who have not seen and yet have believed. That blessing is for us. John adds that he wrote these things so that we may believe that Jesus is the Messiah, the Son of God, and that by believing we may have life in His name."],"id":"story-thomas-believes"},{"e":"g","t":"Breakfast on the beach","r":"John 21","p":"Jesus; Peter; John","s":"After a night of catching nothing, the disciples obey a man on the shore and cast the net on the right side, catching 153 fish. Jesus has breakfast ready on a fire. Three times He asks Peter 'Do you love me?' and three times recommissions him.","w":"Jesus restores us after failure and gives us work to do.","q":"Where do I need Jesus to restore me?","l":["Afterwards Jesus appeared again to His disciples by the Sea of Galilee. Simon Peter, Thomas, Nathanael, the sons of Zebedee and two other disciples were together. Peter said he was going out to fish, and the others went with him. They went out and got into the boat, but that night they caught nothing.","Early in the morning, Jesus stood on the shore, but the disciples did not realise it was Jesus. He called out, asking if they had any fish. When they said no, He told them to throw the net on the right side of the boat and they would find some. When they did, they were unable to haul the net in because of the large number of fish. Then the disciple whom Jesus loved said to Peter, “It is the Lord!” As soon as Peter heard that, he wrapped his outer garment around him and jumped into the water, about a hundred metres from shore. The others followed in the boat, towing the net full of fish. When they landed, they saw a fire of burning coals with fish on it, and some bread. Jesus asked them to bring some of the fish they had just caught. The net held 153 large fish, and even with so many it was not torn. Jesus said, “Come and have breakfast,” and gave them bread and fish.","When they had finished eating, Jesus asked Simon son of John whether he loved Him more than these. Peter said yes, Lord, You know that I love You. Jesus said, “Feed my lambs.” He asked a second time, and Peter answered the same, and Jesus said, “Take care of my sheep.” The third time Jesus asked, Peter was hurt, because it was the third time, and said, “Lord, you know all things; you know that I love you.” Jesus said, “Feed my sheep.” Three denials by a charcoal fire were met by three questions by another charcoal fire. Jesus told Peter he would one day glorify God by his death. Then He said to him the same words He had said by that lake at the very beginning: “Follow me!”"],"id":"story-breakfast-on-the-beach"},{"e":"g","t":"The great commission","r":"Matthew 28:16-20","p":"Jesus; The eleven disciples","s":"On a mountain in Galilee, the risen Jesus meets His disciples. Some worship and some doubt. He says all authority has been given to Him, and sends them to make disciples of all nations, baptising and teaching them. He promises to be with them always, to the very end of the age.","w":"Every disciple is sent, and never sent alone.","q":"Who am I helping to follow Jesus?","l":["After the resurrection, the eleven disciples went to Galilee, to the mountain where Jesus had told them to go. When they saw Him, they worshipped Him, but some doubted. Matthew is honest: even at this moment, faith and hesitation were mixed in the group.","Then Jesus came to them and said that all authority in heaven and on earth had been given to Him. On the mountain of temptation, the devil had offered Him all the kingdoms of the world in exchange for worship. Now, having gone the way of the cross, He has been given all authority by the Father.","Therefore, He said, go and make disciples of all nations, baptising them in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit, and teaching them to obey everything He had commanded. The task is not just converts, but disciples: people who follow, learn and obey. Then He gave His promise: “And surely I am with you always, to the very end of the age.” Matthew’s Gospel began with the name Immanuel, ‘God with us’, and it ends with Jesus promising to be with us always. This is the commission every church, JoshGen included, is built around."],"id":"story-the-great-commission"},{"e":"c","t":"Jesus goes up to heaven","r":"Acts 1:1-11","p":"Jesus; The apostles","s":"Jesus promises the apostles power when the Holy Spirit comes, to be His witnesses to the ends of the earth. Then He is taken up into a cloud. Two men in white say He will come back the same way.","w":"Jesus is reigning and coming back, and His Spirit empowers us now.","q":"Where are my 'ends of the earth'?","l":["Luke begins his second book by reminding his friend Theophilus that his Gospel covered everything Jesus began to do and teach. After His suffering, Jesus showed Himself alive to the apostles over a period of forty days, with many convincing proofs, and spoke to them about the kingdom of God. Once, while eating with them, He told them not to leave Jerusalem but to wait for the gift the Father had promised: in a few days they would be baptised with the Holy Spirit.","The apostles asked whether He was now going to restore the kingdom to Israel. Jesus answered that the times and dates were set by the Father’s authority and were not for them to know. What they would receive was power, when the Holy Spirit came on them, and they would be His witnesses in Jerusalem, in all Judea and Samaria, and to the ends of the earth. That one sentence is the outline of the whole book of Acts.","After He said this, He was taken up before their eyes, and a cloud hid Him from their sight. They were still staring into the sky when two men dressed in white stood beside them and asked why they were standing there looking up. This same Jesus, they said, would come back in the same way they had seen Him go. The apostles returned to Jerusalem and, with the women, Mary the mother of Jesus and His brothers, about a hundred and twenty people in all, joined together constantly in prayer."],"id":"story-jesus-goes-up-to-heaven"},{"e":"c","t":"Pentecost","r":"Acts 2","p":"Peter; The apostles","s":"The believers are together when a sound like wind fills the house and tongues of fire rest on them. They speak in other languages, and Peter preaches to the crowd. About 3,000 people are baptised.","w":"The church is born by the power of the Spirit.","q":"Am I asking to be filled with the Spirit?","l":["Fifty days after Passover came the feast of Pentecost, and the believers were all together in one place. Suddenly a sound like a violent rushing wind came from heaven and filled the whole house. They saw what looked like tongues of fire that separated and came to rest on each of them. All of them were filled with the Holy Spirit and began to speak in other languages as the Spirit enabled them.","Jerusalem was full of God-fearing Jews from every nation. A crowd gathered at the sound, bewildered, because each one heard their own native language being spoken by these Galileans, declaring the wonders of God. Some were amazed and asked what it meant. Others made fun of them and said they had had too much wine. Peter stood up with the Eleven and said these people were not drunk; it was only nine in the morning. This was what the prophet Joel had spoken about: God pouring out His Spirit on all people, sons and daughters, young and old, servants, men and women.","Peter then preached Jesus: a man proved by God through miracles, handed over and crucified, whom God raised from the dead because death could not hold Him. God has made this Jesus both Lord and Messiah. The people were cut to the heart and asked what they should do. Peter told them to repent and be baptised in the name of Jesus Christ for the forgiveness of their sins, and they would receive the gift of the Holy Spirit. About three thousand were added that day. They devoted themselves to the apostles’ teaching, to fellowship, to breaking bread and to prayer. They shared what they had, ate together with glad hearts, and the Lord added to their number daily. This passage, Acts 2:42-47, is the picture JoshGen builds its church life on."],"id":"story-pentecost"},{"e":"c","t":"Silver or gold I do not have","r":"Acts 3","p":"Peter; John; A lame man","s":"At the temple gate called Beautiful, a man lame from birth begs Peter and John for money. Peter says 'Silver or gold I do not have, but what I do have I give you. In the name of Jesus, walk.' He jumps up, walking and praising God.","w":"We give what we have: Jesus.","q":"What do I have in Jesus that I could give someone?","l":["One afternoon Peter and John were going up to the temple at the time of prayer, three o’clock. A man who had been lame from birth was being carried to the temple gate called Beautiful, where he was put every day to beg. When he saw Peter and John about to go in, he asked them for money.","Peter looked straight at him, as did John, and told the man to look at them. He gave them his attention, expecting to get something. Peter said he had no silver or gold, but what he did have he would give: in the name of Jesus Christ of Nazareth, walk. He took him by the right hand and helped him up, and instantly the man’s feet and ankles became strong. He jumped to his feet and went with them into the temple courts, walking and jumping and praising God.","People recognised him as the beggar from the gate and were filled with wonder. As a crowd came running, Peter asked why they stared as if he and John had made the man walk by their own power or godliness. It was the name of Jesus, and faith in that name, that had made this man strong. He called them to repent and turn to God, so that their sins would be wiped out and times of refreshing would come from the Lord. The next day, before the same council that had condemned Jesus, Peter declared that salvation is found in no one else. The leaders were astonished at the courage of these ordinary, unschooled men, and took note that they had been with Jesus."],"id":"story-silver-or-gold-i-do-not-have"},{"e":"c","t":"Ananias and Sapphira","r":"Acts 5:1-11","p":"Ananias; Sapphira; Peter","s":"Ananias and Sapphira sell land and pretend to give all the money to the church while secretly keeping some. Peter says they have lied to the Holy Spirit, and both fall down dead. Great fear seizes the church.","w":"God takes honesty in His church seriously.","q":"Where am I pretending to be more devoted than I am?","l":["The early believers were one in heart and mind. No one claimed that any possession was their own; they shared everything. From time to time those who owned land or houses sold them and brought the money to the apostles, to be given to anyone in need. A man named Joseph, whom the apostles called Barnabas, ‘son of encouragement’, sold a field and brought the money.","A man named Ananias, with his wife Sapphira, also sold a piece of property. With his wife’s full knowledge he kept back part of the money for himself, but brought the rest and laid it at the apostles’ feet as if it were the whole amount. Peter asked him how Satan had so filled his heart that he lied to the Holy Spirit. The land had been his before he sold it, and the money was his to use afterwards. He had not lied just to people, but to God. When Ananias heard this, he fell down and died, and great fear seized everyone who heard.","About three hours later his wife came in, not knowing what had happened. Peter asked her whether this was the price they got for the land, and she said yes, that was the price. Peter asked how they could agree to test the Spirit of the Lord, and she too fell down and died. Great fear seized the whole church. Their sin was not keeping some of the money, which they were free to do. It was pretending, wanting the reputation of generosity without the reality. At the birth of the church, God showed how seriously He takes truthfulness among His people."],"id":"story-ananias-and-sapphira"},{"e":"c","t":"Stephen","r":"Acts 6:8-7:60","p":"Stephen; Saul","s":"Stephen, full of grace and power, is falsely accused before the council. After his speech he sees Jesus standing at God's right hand. As they stone him he prays 'Lord, do not hold this sin against them'. A young man named Saul looks after their coats.","w":"Forgiving to the end, like Jesus.","q":"Who do I need to pray 'do not hold it against them' for?","l":["As the number of disciples grew, the Greek-speaking Jewish widows were being overlooked in the daily food distribution. The apostles had the church choose seven men full of the Spirit and wisdom to take care of it. One of them was Stephen, a man full of God’s grace and power, who performed great wonders among the people. Some men argued with him but could not stand up against his wisdom, so they stirred up false witnesses, and Stephen was brought before the council. Everyone looking at him saw that his face was like the face of an angel.","Asked whether the charges were true, Stephen retold Israel’s story: Abraham, Joseph, Moses, the wilderness, the tabernacle and the temple. Again and again, he showed, God’s people had rejected the ones God sent, and God had never been confined to one building. He ended bluntly: they were stiff-necked, always resisting the Holy Spirit, and they had now betrayed and murdered the Righteous One.","The council was furious. But Stephen, full of the Holy Spirit, looked up to heaven and saw the glory of God, and Jesus standing at God’s right hand, and said so. They covered their ears, rushed at him, dragged him out of the city and began to stone him. The witnesses laid their coats at the feet of a young man named Saul. While they were stoning him, Stephen prayed, asking the Lord Jesus to receive his spirit. Then he fell on his knees and cried out, asking the Lord not to hold this sin against them, and he died. Saul approved of the killing. A great persecution broke out that day, and the believers were scattered, preaching the word wherever they went."],"id":"story-stephen"},{"e":"c","t":"Philip and the Ethiopian","r":"Acts 8:26-40","p":"Philip; The Ethiopian official","s":"An angel sends Philip to a desert road, where an Ethiopian official is reading Isaiah in his chariot. Philip explains it is about Jesus. When they reach water, the man asks to be baptised.","w":"God sets up appointments for those who listen.","q":"Am I open to God's interruptions?","l":["Philip, another of the seven, had gone to a city in Samaria, where crowds believed and there was great joy. In the middle of that success, an angel of the Lord told him to go south to the desert road that runs from Jerusalem down to Gaza. He got up and went.","On the road he met an Ethiopian, an important official in charge of the whole treasury of the Kandake, the queen of the Ethiopians. He had been to Jerusalem to worship and was on his way home, sitting in his chariot reading aloud from the prophet Isaiah. The Spirit told Philip to go and stay near the chariot. Philip ran up, heard him reading and asked whether he understood it. The man asked how he could unless someone explained it, and invited Philip to sit with him. He was reading from Isaiah 53, about one led like a sheep to the slaughter. He asked who the prophet was talking about. Philip began with that very passage and told him the good news about Jesus.","As they travelled, they came to some water, and the official asked what could stand in the way of his being baptised. He ordered the chariot to stop, both went down into the water, and Philip baptised him. When they came up out of the water, the Spirit of the Lord suddenly took Philip away. The official did not see him again, but went on his way rejoicing, carrying the gospel toward Africa. Philip found himself at Azotus and travelled on, preaching in all the towns."],"id":"story-philip-and-the-ethiopian"},{"e":"c","t":"Saul on the Damascus road","r":"Acts 9:1-19","p":"Saul; Jesus; Ananias","s":"Saul is on his way to arrest Christians when a light flashes and Jesus asks 'Saul, Saul, why do you persecute me?' Blind for three days, he is visited by a nervous believer named Ananias. Something like scales fall from his eyes, and he is baptised.","w":"No one is beyond Jesus' reach, not even the church's worst enemy.","q":"Who have I given up praying for?","l":["Saul was still breathing out murderous threats against the Lord’s disciples. He went to the high priest and asked for letters to the synagogues in Damascus, so that he could arrest any followers of the Way he found there, men or women, and take them as prisoners to Jerusalem.","As he came near Damascus, suddenly a light from heaven flashed around him. He fell to the ground and heard a voice asking, “Saul, Saul, why do you persecute me?” He asked who was speaking. The answer came: “I am Jesus, whom you are persecuting.” To hurt the church was to hurt Jesus Himself. He was told to get up and go into the city, where he would be told what to do. When Saul got up and opened his eyes, he could see nothing, so they led him by the hand into Damascus. For three days he was blind and did not eat or drink anything.","In Damascus the Lord spoke in a vision to a disciple named Ananias and sent him to a house on Straight Street to ask for Saul of Tarsus. Ananias objected: he had heard how much harm this man had done. The Lord told him to go, because Saul was His chosen instrument to carry His name to the Gentiles, to kings and to the people of Israel. Ananias went, placed his hands on him and called him “Brother Saul”. Jesus had sent him so that Saul might see again and be filled with the Holy Spirit. Immediately something like scales fell from Saul’s eyes. He got up and was baptised, and after eating regained his strength. At once he began to preach in the synagogues that Jesus is the Son of God. He became known as Paul, and wrote much of the New Testament."],"id":"story-saul-on-the-damascus-road"},{"e":"c","t":"Peter and Cornelius","r":"Acts 10","p":"Peter; Cornelius","s":"A Roman centurion, Cornelius, is told by an angel to send for Peter. Meanwhile Peter sees a vision of a sheet full of unclean animals and hears 'Do not call anything impure that God has made clean'. While Peter preaches in Cornelius's house, the Holy Spirit falls on the Gentiles.","w":"The gospel is for every nation.","q":"Is there anyone I keep at a distance that God welcomes?","l":["In Caesarea lived Cornelius, a centurion in the Italian Regiment. He and his family were devout and God-fearing; he gave generously to those in need and prayed regularly. One afternoon he clearly saw an angel in a vision, who told him that his prayers and gifts had come up before God, and that he should send men to Joppa for a man named Simon Peter, staying with a tanner by the sea.","About noon the next day, as the men approached the city, Peter went up on the roof to pray. He became hungry and fell into a trance. He saw heaven opened and something like a large sheet being let down by its four corners, containing all kinds of animals, reptiles and birds. A voice told him to get up, kill and eat. Peter refused, saying he had never eaten anything impure or unclean. The voice answered that he must not call anything impure that God has made clean. This happened three times. While Peter was wondering what it meant, the men arrived, and the Spirit told him to go with them without hesitating.","Cornelius had gathered his relatives and close friends. Peter said that it was against Jewish law to visit a Gentile, but God had shown him not to call anyone impure or unclean. He told them he now realised that God does not show favouritism, but accepts people from every nation who fear Him. He told them about Jesus: His life, death and resurrection, and forgiveness through His name. While Peter was still speaking, the Holy Spirit came on all who heard the message. The Jewish believers with him were astonished that the gift of the Spirit had been poured out even on Gentiles, because they heard them speaking in tongues and praising God. Peter had them baptised. The door of the church was now open to every nation."],"id":"story-peter-and-cornelius"},{"e":"c","t":"The church at Antioch","r":"Acts 11:19-30; 13:1-3","p":"Barnabas; Saul","s":"Believers scattered by persecution reach Antioch and start telling Greeks about Jesus, and many believe. Barnabas is sent to help, fetches Saul from Tarsus, and they teach there for a year. The disciples are first called Christians at Antioch. Later, while the church is worshipping and fasting, the Holy Spirit sets Barnabas and Saul apart to be sent out.","w":"A healthy local church encourages, teaches, gives and sends.","q":"How am I helping my church to be a sending church?","l":["The believers scattered by the persecution after Stephen’s death travelled as far as Phoenicia, Cyprus and Antioch, at first telling the message only to Jews. But some of them, men from Cyprus and Cyrene, went to Antioch and began to speak to Greeks as well, telling them the good news about the Lord Jesus. The Lord’s hand was with them, and a great number believed.","When the church in Jerusalem heard, they sent Barnabas. When he arrived and saw what the grace of God had done, he was glad and encouraged them all to remain true to the Lord with all their hearts. He was a good man, full of the Holy Spirit and faith. Then Barnabas went to Tarsus to look for Saul, brought him back, and for a whole year the two of them met with the church and taught great numbers of people. The disciples were called Christians first at Antioch. When a prophet named Agabus predicted a severe famine, the disciples, each according to their ability, sent help to the believers in Judea.","In the church at Antioch there were prophets and teachers from very different backgrounds: Barnabas, Simeon called Niger, Lucius of Cyrene, Manaen, who had grown up with Herod the ruler, and Saul. While they were worshipping the Lord and fasting, the Holy Spirit told them to set apart Barnabas and Saul for the work He had called them to. After fasting and praying, they laid hands on them and sent them off. The first missionary journey began from a local church at worship."],"id":"story-the-church-at-antioch"},{"e":"c","t":"Peter's escape from prison","r":"Acts 12:1-17","p":"Peter; Rhoda; Herod","s":"Herod arrests Peter, chained between two soldiers, while the church prays earnestly. An angel wakes Peter, the chains fall off and the iron gate opens by itself. He knocks at the prayer meeting, and the servant girl Rhoda is so excited she leaves him standing outside.","w":"God answers prayer, sometimes faster than we believe.","q":"Am I expecting God to answer my prayers?","l":["King Herod Agrippa arrested some who belonged to the church, intending to persecute them. He had James, the brother of John, put to death with the sword. When he saw that this pleased the Jewish leaders, he seized Peter too, during the Festival of Unleavened Bread, and put him in prison, guarded by four squads of four soldiers each. Herod meant to bring him out for public trial after the Passover. So Peter was kept in prison, but the church was earnestly praying to God for him.","The night before the trial, Peter was sleeping between two soldiers, bound with two chains, with sentries at the entrance. Suddenly an angel of the Lord appeared and a light shone in the cell. The angel struck Peter on the side to wake him and told him to get up quickly, and the chains fell off his wrists. He told Peter to put on his clothes, sandals and cloak and follow. Peter followed, thinking he was seeing a vision. They passed the first and second guards and came to the iron gate leading to the city, which opened for them by itself. After they had walked the length of one street, the angel left him. Then Peter came to himself and realised the Lord had really rescued him.","He went to the house of Mary the mother of John Mark, where many people had gathered and were praying. Peter knocked at the outer entrance, and a servant named Rhoda came to answer. When she recognised Peter’s voice, she was so overjoyed that she ran back without opening the door and announced that Peter was at the door. They told her she was out of her mind. When she kept insisting, they said it must be his angel. But Peter kept on knocking, and when they opened the door and saw him, they were astonished. He motioned for them to be quiet, described how the Lord had brought him out, and then left for another place. The church was praying earnestly, and still could hardly believe the answer when it knocked."],"id":"story-peter-s-escape-from-prison"},{"e":"c","t":"Lydia opens her home","r":"Acts 16:6-15","p":"Paul; Silas; Timothy; Lydia","s":"Paul's plans are blocked twice by the Spirit. Then he has a vision of a man from Macedonia begging for help, and the team sails for Europe. In Philippi they find a group of women praying by the river. Lydia, a dealer in purple cloth, listens, the Lord opens her heart, and she and her household are baptised. Her home becomes the base for the church.","w":"God redirects closed doors toward open hearts.","q":"What closed door might be God's redirection?","l":["On his second journey, Paul travelled with Silas and a young disciple named Timothy. They were kept by the Holy Spirit from preaching in the province of Asia. They tried to enter Bithynia, but the Spirit of Jesus would not allow them to. So they passed on and went down to the port of Troas, not knowing where to go next.","During the night Paul had a vision of a man of Macedonia standing and begging him to come over and help them. They got ready at once to leave, concluding that God had called them to preach the gospel there. Luke joins the story here, writing ‘we’. They sailed across and travelled to Philippi, a Roman colony and the leading city of that district of Macedonia. It was the gospel’s first recorded step into Europe.","There was apparently no synagogue in Philippi, so on the Sabbath they went outside the city gate to the river, where they expected to find a place of prayer. They sat down and spoke to the women who had gathered. One of them was Lydia, a dealer in purple cloth from the city of Thyatira, who was a worshipper of God. The Lord opened her heart to respond to Paul’s message. When she and the members of her household were baptised, she invited them to come and stay at her house, if they considered her a believer in the Lord, and she persuaded them. Her home became the meeting place for the church at Philippi, the church Paul later wrote to with such affection."],"id":"story-lydia-opens-her-home"},{"e":"c","t":"Paul and Silas in prison","r":"Acts 16:16-40","p":"Paul; Silas; The jailer","s":"In Philippi, Paul and Silas are beaten and put in stocks. At midnight they are praying and singing hymns when an earthquake opens every door. The jailer asks 'What must I do to be saved?' and his whole household is baptised that night.","w":"Worship in the dark can open doors.","q":"Can I worship before my situation changes?","l":["In Philippi, Paul and Silas were followed for many days by a slave girl who had a spirit by which she predicted the future, earning a great deal of money for her owners. She kept shouting that these men were servants of the Most High God. Finally Paul turned and commanded the spirit, in the name of Jesus Christ, to come out of her, and it left her. When her owners realised that their hope of making money was gone, they dragged Paul and Silas into the marketplace before the authorities and accused them of throwing the city into an uproar.","The crowd joined in the attack. The magistrates ordered them to be stripped and beaten with rods. After a severe flogging, they were thrown into prison, and the jailer put them in the inner cell and fastened their feet in the stocks. About midnight Paul and Silas were praying and singing hymns to God, and the other prisoners were listening to them. Suddenly there was such a violent earthquake that the foundations of the prison were shaken. At once all the prison doors flew open, and everyone’s chains came loose.","The jailer woke up, saw the doors open and drew his sword to kill himself, because he thought the prisoners had escaped and he would pay with his life. Paul shouted not to harm himself, because they were all there. The jailer called for lights, rushed in and fell trembling before Paul and Silas. He brought them out and asked what he must do to be saved. They replied that he should believe in the Lord Jesus, and he would be saved, he and his household. They spoke the word of the Lord to everyone in his house. That same hour of the night the jailer washed their wounds, and immediately he and all his household were baptised. He set a meal before them, filled with joy because he had come to believe in God."],"id":"story-paul-and-silas-in-prison"},{"e":"c","t":"Paul in Athens","r":"Acts 17:16-34","p":"Paul","s":"Paul is distressed by the idols in Athens. At the Areopagus he points to an altar 'to an unknown god' and tells them about the God who made the world and raised Jesus from the dead. Some mock, but some believe.","w":"Start where people are, and point them to Jesus.","q":"What 'unknown god' do people around me worship?","l":["While Paul was waiting for Silas and Timothy in Athens, he was greatly distressed to see that the city was full of idols. So he reasoned in the synagogue and in the marketplace day by day with whoever happened to be there. A group of Epicurean and Stoic philosophers began to debate with him. Some called him a babbler; others said he seemed to be advocating foreign gods, because he was preaching Jesus and the resurrection. They brought him to a meeting of the Areopagus, the city’s council, to explain this new teaching.","Paul stood up and began where they were. He said he could see they were very religious, because as he walked around he had even found an altar with the inscription: to an unknown god. What they worshipped as unknown, he would now proclaim to them. The God who made the world and everything in it is Lord of heaven and earth and does not live in temples built by human hands. He is not served by human hands as if He needed anything; He Himself gives everyone life and breath. From one man He made all the nations, so that people would seek Him, though He is not far from any one of us. Paul even quoted their own poets: in Him we live and move and have our being.","Since we are God’s offspring, Paul said, we should not think the divine being is like gold or silver or stone. In the past God overlooked such ignorance, but now He commands all people everywhere to repent, because He has set a day when He will judge the world with justice by the man He has appointed, and He has given proof of this by raising Him from the dead. When they heard about the resurrection of the dead, some sneered, but others said they wanted to hear him again. Some became followers and believed, among them Dionysius, a member of the Areopagus, and a woman named Damaris."],"id":"story-paul-in-athens"},{"e":"c","t":"Eutychus falls asleep","r":"Acts 20:7-12","p":"Paul; Eutychus","s":"Paul preaches in Troas until midnight. A young man named Eutychus, sitting in a window, falls asleep and falls three storeys to his death. Paul throws his arms around him, and he is alive.","w":"God's grace even covers falling asleep in church!","q":"How can I stay alert to what God is saying?","l":["Near the end of his third journey, Paul spent seven days in Troas. On the first day of the week the believers came together to break bread. Paul spoke to the people and, because he intended to leave the next day, kept on talking until midnight. There were many lamps in the upstairs room where they were meeting, which would have made it warm and stuffy.","Seated in a window was a young man named Eutychus, who was sinking into a deep sleep as Paul talked on and on. When he was sound asleep, he fell to the ground from the third storey and was picked up dead. Paul went down, threw himself on the young man and put his arms around him. He told them not to be alarmed, because he was alive.","Then Paul went upstairs again, broke bread and ate. After talking until daylight, he left. The people took the young man home alive and were greatly comforted. Luke, who was a doctor and was there that night, tells the story plainly. It is a comfort to anyone who has ever struggled to stay awake in a long meeting, and a reminder of how hungry that church was to hear the word."],"id":"story-eutychus-falls-asleep"},{"e":"c","t":"Shipwrecked on Malta","r":"Acts 27-28:10","p":"Paul","s":"On the way to Rome as a prisoner, Paul's ship is caught in a storm for two weeks. An angel promises everyone will survive, and all 276 people reach land. On Malta a viper bites Paul and he is unharmed. He heals the island chief's father and many others.","w":"God keeps His word through every storm.","q":"What promise of God carries me through my storm?","l":["Paul was arrested in Jerusalem, held for two years in Caesarea and finally appealed to Caesar. He was put on a ship for Rome under a centurion named Julius, with Luke and Aristarchus travelling with him. Sailing was already dangerous because it was late in the year. Paul warned that the voyage would be disastrous, but the centurion followed the advice of the pilot and the ship’s owner, and they sailed on.","Before long a wind of hurricane force, called the Northeaster, swept down from the island of Crete. The ship was caught and driven along. They threw the cargo overboard, then the ship’s tackle. When neither sun nor stars appeared for many days and the storm kept raging, they gave up all hope of being saved. Then Paul stood up and urged them to keep up their courage. An angel of the God he belonged to and served had stood beside him that night and said not to be afraid: he must stand trial before Caesar, and God had graciously given him the lives of all who sailed with him. On the fourteenth night, as they neared land, Paul urged everyone to eat, took bread, gave thanks to God in front of them all and broke it. All 276 on board were encouraged and ate.","At daylight they ran the ship aground on a sandbar, and the stern was broken to pieces by the surf. The soldiers planned to kill the prisoners, but the centurion wanted to spare Paul and stopped them. Everyone reached land safely, some swimming and some on planks. The island was Malta, and the islanders showed unusual kindness, building a fire because it was raining and cold. As Paul gathered brushwood, a viper driven out by the heat fastened itself on his hand. The islanders expected him to swell up or drop dead, but he shook the snake off into the fire and suffered no ill effects. Paul prayed for the father of Publius, the chief official, who was sick with fever, and he was healed, and then the rest of the sick on the island came and were cured. Three months later they sailed on, and so Paul came to Rome."],"id":"story-shipwrecked-on-malta"}];
